const MOCK1 = [
{n:1,topic:"AFSPs and Other Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Declan is a tax preparer. He does not hold any formal licensing or have an AFSP certificate. The IRS is auditing his client's business returns. Declan prepared all the returns, and his client wants Declan to represent him during the audit. What can Declan do in order to represent his client?",opts:["Declan may represent his client before the IRS, as long as his client is present during the examination.","Declan may represent his client before the IRS, as long as his client gives informed consent in writing.","Declan may not represent his client before the IRS.","Declan may represent his client before the IRS, as long as his client signs a power of attorney."],a:2,why:"Since 2016, unenrolled preparers WITHOUT an AFSP Record of Completion have no representation rights at all — even for returns they prepared and signed. Only AFSP holders retain limited representation rights (and only for returns they prepared and signed, before revenue agents, customer service reps, and similar IRS employees).",wrongs:["The client being present does not create representation rights for an unlicensed, non-AFSP preparer.","Written consent from the client cannot grant representation rights the preparer doesn't have under Circular 230.",null,"A power of attorney (Form 2848) can only designate someone who is eligible to practice; Declan is not."]},
{n:2,topic:"Knowledge of Client's Omission",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Genevieve is an enrolled agent representing a client, Alek, before the examination division of the IRS. The IRS is questioning Alek about charitable contributions he claimed. While reviewing documentation, Genevieve discovers contributions made to a nonqualified organization (a political organization). Alek did not realize the amounts were not deductible. What is the correct course of action for Genevieve?",opts:["She must immediately advise the IRS examiner of the nonqualified contributions.","She must advise Alek promptly of the omission or error and the consequences of not correcting the error or omission.","She must notify the IRS that she is no longer representing Alek by withdrawing her Form 2848.","She must advise Alek on how to keep the omission or error from being discovered by the IRS."],a:1,why:"Circular 230 §10.21 requires a practitioner who knows of a client's error or omission to promptly advise the CLIENT of the error and of the consequences under the Code of not correcting it. The duty runs to the client, not to the IRS.",wrongs:["A practitioner may not disclose the error to the IRS without the client's consent — that would breach confidentiality.",null,"Withdrawal is not required; the required action is advising the client.","Helping conceal an error is a serious Circular 230 violation and potentially criminal."]},
{n:3,topic:"AFSPs and Other Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"The IRS currently offers two types of voluntary return preparer designations/licensing. What are they?",opts:["The Enrolled Agent credential and the Registered Tax Return Preparer.","The Enrolled Agent credential and Certified Acceptance Agent program.","The Enrolled Agent credential and the Annual Filing Season Program.","Registered Tax Return Preparer and the Annual Filing Season Program."],a:2,why:"The IRS's two voluntary preparer programs are the Enrolled Agent credential (full practice rights) and the Annual Filing Season Program (limited representation rights).",wrongs:["The RTRP program was struck down in Loving v. IRS and no longer exists.","Acceptance Agents assist with ITIN applications — that is not a preparer designation.",null,"The RTRP program no longer exists."]},
{n:4,topic:"Taxpayer Requests: CDP/CAP Hearings",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"How long does a taxpayer have to request a CDP hearing after receiving an IRS collection notice?",opts:["60 days","10 days","30 days","90 days"],a:2,why:"A taxpayer has 30 days from the date of the collection notice (e.g., Notice of Intent to Levy or Notice of Federal Tax Lien filing) to request a Collection Due Process hearing, which also suspends collection and preserves the right to petition the Tax Court.",wrongs:["60 days is not the CDP deadline.","10 days relates to the initial notice-and-demand payment window, not CDP.",null,"90 days is the deadline to petition Tax Court after a notice of deficiency, not for CDP requests."]},
{n:5,topic:"Taxpayer Bill of Rights",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"With regard to the Taxpayer Bill of Rights, what does the \"Right to Appeal an IRS Decision\" ensure for taxpayers?",opts:["The right to have their case reviewed, but only by the same IRS agent.","The right to litigate any tax assessment without having to pay the tax first.","The right to protest any tax assessment as a sovereign citizen of the United States.","The right to a fair and impartial administrative appeal of most IRS decisions, including many penalties, and the right to take their cases to court."],a:3,why:"The Taxpayer Bill of Rights guarantees a fair and impartial administrative appeal in an independent forum (the IRS Independent Office of Appeals) for most IRS decisions, plus the right to take the case to court.",wrongs:["Appeals must be independent — not review by the same agent who made the decision.","Prepayment litigation is only available in Tax Court for deficiencies; district court and Court of Federal Claims require paying first. It's not a blanket right.","\"Sovereign citizen\" arguments are frivolous positions, not rights.",null]},
{n:6,topic:"Most Common EITC, AOTC, and Child Tax Credit Errors",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Janessa is 42 and has a 10-year-old son that she claims on her return as a dependent. Janessa's only income is from wages and she would like to claim the Earned Income Credit. Which of the following tests is not applicable to the EITC?",opts:["Residency test.","Age test.","Full-time student test.","Relationship test."],a:2,why:"A qualifying child for EITC must meet the relationship, age, residency, and joint-return tests. Full-time student status is only one way to satisfy the age test for children 19–23 — it is not a separate required test, and it's irrelevant for a 10-year-old.",wrongs:["The child must live with the taxpayer in the U.S. for more than half the year — residency applies.","The child must be under 19 (or under 24 if a full-time student, or any age if permanently disabled) — the age test applies.",null,"The child must bear a qualifying relationship to the taxpayer — the relationship test applies."]},
{n:7,topic:"IRS Divisions and the Taxpayer Advocate",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Brodie amended a prior year return within the statute period for a refund. The official processing time for Form 1040X is eight to twelve weeks. However, Brodie has been waiting for more than twelve months for his refund to be processed. He has contacted IRS customer service and they always say his return is still processing. What can Brodie do?",opts:["Brodie may sue the IRS for a refund.","Brodie may request intervention from the Taxpayer Advocate Service.","Brodie may request intervention from TIGTA.","Brodie must wait until the return processes, no matter how long it takes."],a:1,why:"The Taxpayer Advocate Service is an independent organization within the IRS that helps taxpayers experiencing significant delays or hardship after normal IRS channels have failed — exactly Brodie's situation.",wrongs:["A refund suit is premature and costly; TAS intervention is the appropriate administrative remedy here.",null,"TIGTA investigates IRS employee misconduct and fraud — it does not resolve processing delays.","Taxpayers are not required to wait indefinitely; TAS exists for this."]},
{n:8,topic:"Third-Party Authorizations",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Where is a third-party designee authorization recorded?",opts:["Directly in the taxpayer's account with the IRS.","In the CAF system.","The taxpayer must record the third-party designee authorization in his own account.","This authorization is not recorded by the IRS; it is recorded by the practitioner."],a:0,why:"The \"checkbox\" third-party designee authorization on a tax return is recorded directly on the taxpayer's account for that specific return and year. It is limited in scope and expires automatically (generally one year from the return due date).",wrongs:[null,"The CAF (Centralized Authorization File) records Forms 2848 and 8821 — not the checkbox designee authorization.","The taxpayer doesn't record anything; the IRS records it from the filed return.","The IRS does record it — on the taxpayer's account."]},
{n:9,topic:"Abatement of Penalties and Interest",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Michael works for Sloppy Auto, Inc, and earns $150,000 in wages. His boss switches payroll providers mid-year, and as a result Michael's Social Security taxes were over-withheld. His employer refuses to fix the W-2 or the withholding. Michael has only worked for Sloppy Auto during the year. Does Michael have any recourse?",opts:["Michael cannot request a refund of the excess withholding. The employer must correct it.","Michael can fill out IRS Form 843, Claim for Refund and Request for Abatement, to request a refund of excess withholding.","Michael can request a refund of excess withholding directly from the Social Security office.","Michael can simply take a credit on his return; no additional forms are necessary."],a:1,why:"When a SINGLE employer over-withholds Social Security tax and refuses to correct it, the employee's remedy is Form 843, Claim for Refund and Request for Abatement.",wrongs:["The employee has recourse even when the employer won't fix it — Form 843.",null,"The Social Security Administration does not refund excess FICA withholding; the IRS does.","The automatic credit on Form 1040 only applies to excess SS tax withheld by MULTIPLE employers. With one employer, Form 843 is required."]},
{n:10,topic:"Tax Refunds",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"A preparer cannot charge a separate fee for:",opts:["Direct deposit of a taxpayer's refund check.","Preparing multiple tax returns for the same family.","E-filing a tax return.","Filing spouses' returns as married filing separately."],a:0,why:"E-file rules prohibit charging a separate fee for direct deposit of a refund. Direct deposit is a free feature of the tax system, not a billable service.",wrongs:[null,"Charging for each return prepared is normal, permitted billing.","A preparer may charge for e-filing services (though they may not charge fees contingent on the refund).","Preparing two MFS returns is legitimate work that may be billed."]}
,
{n:11,topic:"Penalties Imposed on Tax Preparers",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Tamara is an enrolled agent who has been personally audited after being reported by a former client for possible misconduct. The IRS examiner discovers that Tamara has made an understatement on several tax returns due to reckless misconduct and willfully ignoring the law. What is the potential penalty under IRC §6694(b)?",opts:["The greater of $5,000 or 100% of the income derived by the tax return preparer with respect to the return or claim for refund.","The lesser of $500 or 100% of the income derived by the tax return preparer with respect to the return or claim for refund.","The greater of $5,000 or 75% of the income derived by the tax return preparer with respect to each return or claim for refund.","The greater of $50,000 or 50% of the income derived by the tax return preparer with respect to each return or claim for refund."],a:2,why:"IRC §6694(b) — willful or reckless conduct — carries a penalty of the greater of $5,000 or 75% of the income derived by the preparer with respect to each return or claim.",wrongs:["The percentage is 75%, not 100%.","This mixes up the amounts; §6694(b) is far more severe than $500.",null,"$50,000/50% is not the statutory formula."]},
{n:12,topic:"AFSPs and Other Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"The Annual Filing Season Program (AFSP) is a ________________.",opts:["Voluntary program.","Illegal program.","Mandatory program.","Volunteer program, like VITA."],a:0,why:"The AFSP is a voluntary IRS program that recognizes unenrolled preparers who complete continuing education; it grants limited representation rights for returns they prepare and sign.",wrongs:[null,"It is an official IRS program, entirely legal.","No preparer is required to participate — only a PTIN is mandatory for paid preparers.","VITA is unpaid volunteer return preparation; AFSP participants are paid preparers."]},
{n:13,topic:"IRS Collections & Enforcement of Unpaid Taxes",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following types of income/assets are NOT exempt from IRS levy?",opts:["Undelivered mail.","Worker's compensation.","Unemployment benefits.","Social Security payments."],a:3,why:"Social Security payments are NOT exempt — the IRS can levy them (e.g., up to 15% continuously under the Federal Payment Levy Program).",wrongs:["Undelivered mail is statutorily exempt from levy.","Workers' compensation is exempt from levy.","Unemployment benefits are exempt from levy.",null]},
{n:14,topic:"Disciplinary Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which sanction results in a tax practitioner being ineligible to represent taxpayers before the IRS for a specific time period?",opts:["Reprimand","Suspension","Monetary penalty","Censure"],a:1,why:"Suspension bars a practitioner from practice before the IRS for a specified period (typically 1 to 59 months), after which eligibility can be restored.",wrongs:["A reprimand is a private letter of criticism — practice rights continue.",null,"A monetary penalty is financial; it doesn't remove practice rights.","Censure is a public reprimand — the practitioner may still practice."]},
{n:15,topic:"Penalties for Failure to Exercise Due Diligence",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Bernice prepared her client's 2025 tax return on January 30, 2026. The client, Rudolf, claimed three children as dependents, claiming EITC for each child, and also claimed head of household status. Bernice didn't ask any questions about the children or collect any documents. The IRS audit found the children were his girlfriend's, unrelated to him, and all EITC credits and HOH status were disallowed. What penalty can apply to Bernice?",opts:["The IRS may assess a penalty of $650 per failure against Bernice.","The IRS may assess a penalty of $55 per failure against Bernice.","The IRS may assess a penalty of $1,000 per return against Bernice.","No penalty. All the penalties will apply to Rudolf."],a:0,why:"IRC §6695(g) imposes an inflation-adjusted penalty ($650 per failure for returns filed in 2026) on preparers who fail due diligence requirements for EITC, CTC/ACTC, AOTC, and head of household status. Each failed credit/status on the return is a separate failure, so penalties stack.",wrongs:[null,"$55 is roughly the §6695 penalty for other failures (e.g., failure to sign or furnish a copy), not due diligence.","The due diligence penalty is per failure, not a flat $1,000 per return.","The taxpayer faces his own consequences, but the preparer is independently penalized for skipping due diligence."]},
{n:16,topic:"Practitioner Fees",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following fee arrangements is not allowed under Circular 230?",opts:["Hourly fee.","Fee based on a percentage of a taxpayer's refund.","Fixed fee schedule.","Initial consultation fee."],a:1,why:"Circular 230 §10.27 prohibits contingent fees for preparing original returns — including any fee based on a percentage of the refund.",wrongs:["Hourly billing is permitted.",null,"Fixed/published fee schedules are permitted.","Consultation fees are permitted."]},
{n:17,topic:"AFSPs and Other Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Melanie is a tax preparer who has an AFSP certificate. How often must she renew her certificate?",opts:["Every year","Every three years","Every five years","Never"],a:0,why:"The AFSP Record of Completion is an annual certificate — the preparer must complete the required continuing education and renew every filing season.",wrongs:[null,"Three years is the EA enrollment cycle, not AFSP.","Five years is not a renewal cycle for any IRS preparer program.","The AFSP is not permanent; it lapses without annual renewal."]},
{n:18,topic:"Seeking Relief from Joint Liability (Including Innocent Spouse)",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"\"Separation of liability\" relief can relieve one spouse from having to pay the other spouse's share of understated taxes from a joint return. Which of the following would make a taxpayer ineligible to apply for separation of liability relief?",opts:["The spouses are estranged but still live in the same home.","The spouses are not divorced, but have been living apart for over 12 months.","Taxes were understated due to errors on a jointly-filed return.","The requesting spouse signed the joint return."],a:0,why:"To qualify for separation of liability relief, the requesting spouse must be divorced, widowed, legally separated, OR not a member of the same household as the other spouse for the 12 months before the request. Estranged spouses still living together fail this test.",wrongs:[null,"Living apart for 12+ months satisfies the eligibility requirement — it doesn't disqualify.","An understatement on a joint return is precisely what this relief addresses.","Signing the joint return is expected — that's why joint liability exists in the first place."]},
{n:19,topic:"Freedom of Information Act Requests (FOIA)",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Under the Freedom of Information Act (FOIA), what are federal agencies, including the IRS, required to do upon receiving a written request for records?",opts:["Automatically accept the request, as long as it is not for tax avoidance purposes.","Forward the request to the U.S. Treasury Department for immediate processing.","Withhold the requested records unless the taxpayer who is requesting them has a valid court order.","Disclose the requested records unless they can be withheld under certain exceptions allowed in the FOIA."],a:3,why:"FOIA requires agencies to disclose requested records unless the records fall under one of FOIA's specific exemptions or exclusions (e.g., classified material, certain law enforcement records, personal privacy).",wrongs:["There's no automatic acceptance, and 'tax avoidance purpose' is not the FOIA standard.","Each agency processes its own FOIA requests; the IRS doesn't forward them to Treasury.","FOIA presumes disclosure — no court order is needed to request records.",null]},
{n:20,topic:"Enrolled Agent Licensing",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following statements is NOT correct about becoming an enrolled agent?",opts:["All applicants must be at least 18 years old.","All applicants must apply for enrollment by submitting Form 23.","All applicants must pass a background check that includes both a tax compliance check and a suitability check.","All applicants must pass the three parts of the Special Enrollment Examination."],a:3,why:"Not ALL applicants must pass the SEE — former IRS employees with qualifying technical experience can become EAs without taking the exam. That makes this statement incorrect.",wrongs:["Applicants must be at least 18 — this is a true requirement.","Form 23 is the enrollment application everyone submits — true.","All applicants undergo the tax compliance and suitability check — true.",null]}
,
{n:21,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Bashir is an enrolled agent. He has done work for two business partners who are now dissolving their partnership. The partners' relationship has deteriorated and Bashir is concerned there is a potential conflict of interest if he continues to represent both. What is Bashir's responsibility?",opts:["Terminate his client relationship with both partners.","Terminate his client relationship with one of the partners, but not with both.","Continue his client relationship with both partners only if he believes he can provide competent and diligent representation to each and obtain either oral or written permission to waive the conflict of interest from both clients.","Continue his client relationship with both partners only if he believes he can provide competent and diligent representation to each and obtain written consent to waive the conflict of interest from both clients."],a:3,why:"Circular 230 §10.29 permits representation despite a conflict only if the practitioner reasonably believes competent, diligent representation of each client is possible, the representation isn't prohibited by law, and each affected client gives informed consent CONFIRMED IN WRITING (retained for 36 months after the engagement ends).",wrongs:["Termination isn't required if the conflict can be properly waived.","Dropping one client isn't required either if written waivers are obtained.","Oral consent is insufficient — Circular 230 specifically requires written confirmation.",null]},
{n:22,topic:"Trust Fund Recovery Penalty",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Esther is the chief financial officer of Denver Realty, Inc. The company has 10 employees. Denver Realty is having financial troubles and Esther decides to pay other business expenses rather than remitting payroll taxes to the federal government. Esther is not an owner, but she is a company officer. Esther could face which of the following penalties?",opts:["Payroll penalty tax.","Social Security tax penalty.","FICA tax fine.","Trust fund recovery penalty."],a:3,why:"The Trust Fund Recovery Penalty (IRC §6672) applies personally to any \"responsible person\" who willfully fails to collect or pay over withheld payroll taxes — including officers like a CFO, regardless of ownership. The penalty equals 100% of the unpaid trust fund taxes.",wrongs:["\"Payroll penalty tax\" is not an actual penalty name.","\"Social Security tax penalty\" is not the correct term.","\"FICA tax fine\" is not an actual penalty.",null]},
{n:23,topic:"The 90-Day Letter (Notice of Deficiency)",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"The IRS assessed tax, penalties, and interest of $10,000 on Gracie. She failed to file a petition to take her case to the U.S. Tax Court within the required 90 days after the date of the notice of deficiency. How much time does Gracie have to pay the contested tax deficiency without incurring additional penalties and interest?",opts:["21 days.","30 days.","60 days.","90 days."],a:0,why:"After assessment, the IRS sends a notice and demand for payment. Paying within 21 calendar days of the notice and demand (10 business days if the amount is $100,000 or more) avoids additional failure-to-pay penalties and interest on the assessed amount.",wrongs:[null,"30 days is the CDP request window, not the payment window.","60 days is not the applicable period.","90 days was the Tax Court petition deadline, which has already passed."]},
{n:24,topic:"Standards for Tax Returns and Documents",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Kendall is an enrolled agent. She meets with a new client, Frederick, who gives her information that seems inconsistent with other statements he has made. Under Circular 230 §10.34, Kendall should:",opts:["Terminate the engagement if the client fails to produce adequate documentation of the information he has provided.","Independently verify the information.","Make reasonable inquiries of the client since the information appears inconsistent.","Rely in good faith without verification of the information."],a:2,why:"A practitioner may generally rely in good faith on client information WITHOUT verification — but may not ignore implications of other known information. When information appears incorrect, inconsistent, or incomplete, the practitioner must make reasonable inquiries.",wrongs:["Termination isn't the prescribed first step; inquiry is.","Independent verification (auditing the client) is not required — reasonable inquiry is the standard.",null,"Good-faith reliance is only allowed when nothing appears inconsistent; here something does."]},
{n:25,topic:"Penalties Imposed on Tax Preparers",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Ingrid is an enrolled agent who prepared Jerry's returns for three years. Jerry later gets an IRS notice for unreported income from a Form 1099-INT he never gave Ingrid. When asked, Jerry says he forgot about the account. Ingrid did not know the account existed and Jerry never revealed it when the return was prepared. Will Ingrid be subject to a preparer penalty under IRC §6694?",opts:["Ingrid may be subject to a suspension or a censure for not investigating her client's income more diligently.","Ingrid will be subject to a penalty, as well as possible sanctions.","Ingrid will be subject to a preparer penalty.","Ingrid will not be subject to a preparer penalty."],a:3,why:"A preparer may rely in good faith on information furnished by the client and isn't required to audit or independently verify it. Since nothing indicated the account existed and Jerry withheld the information, the understatement wasn't due to an unreasonable position or misconduct by Ingrid — no §6694 penalty applies.",wrongs:["No duty was breached; there was nothing inconsistent to trigger further inquiry.","There is no basis for a penalty or sanctions on these facts.","§6694 requires an unreasonable position or willful/reckless conduct by the preparer — absent here.",null]},
{n:26,topic:"Federal Tax Law",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Tax law is determined by all three branches of the federal government. Which branch of the U.S. government has the primary function of originating tax laws?",opts:["The President.","Congress.","The U.S. Treasury Department.","The Supreme Court."],a:1,why:"The Constitution gives Congress the power to levy taxes; revenue bills originate in the House of Representatives (Ways and Means Committee). Congress writes the Internal Revenue Code.",wrongs:["The President signs or vetoes tax bills but doesn't originate tax law.",null,"Treasury (including the IRS) administers and interprets tax law through regulations — it doesn't enact it.","The Supreme Court interprets tax law in litigation; it doesn't create it."]},
{n:27,topic:"AFSPs and Other Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Tereza is an unenrolled tax return preparer with an AFSP certificate. She has a new client named Jasper who received an IRS notice stating that his 2024 tax return is under examination. Jasper was not Tereza's client in the past, and she did not prepare the return. Can Tereza represent Jasper in the audit?",opts:["Yes, if she files a signed Form 2848, with Jasper designating her as his representative.","Yes, if Jasper signs a notarized statement saying he wants to designate her as his representative.","Yes, but Jasper must be present at all IRS hearings. Tereza cannot attend on his behalf.","No, she cannot represent him."],a:3,why:"AFSP participants have only LIMITED representation rights: they may represent clients only for returns they themselves prepared AND signed. Since Tereza didn't prepare Jasper's 2024 return, she cannot represent him in its examination.",wrongs:["A Form 2848 can't expand her rights beyond returns she prepared and signed.","A notarized statement has no effect on representation eligibility.","There is no 'client present' workaround — she simply has no representation rights for this return.",null]},
{n:28,topic:"E-File Rejection",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"When can a rejected individual e-filed return be retransmitted without new signatures or authorizations?",opts:["If the changes do not differ from the amount on the original electronic return by more than $50 to \"total income\" or \"AGI\", or more than $14 to \"total tax\", \"federal income tax withheld\", \"refund,\" or \"amount you owe\".","If the changes do not differ from the amount on the original electronic return by more than $14 to \"total income\" or \"AGI\", or more than $50 to \"total tax\", \"federal income tax withheld\", \"refund\", or \"amount you owe\".","Never.","Always."],a:0,why:"IRS e-file rules allow retransmission without new signatures if corrections change \"total income\" or \"AGI\" by no more than $50, or \"total tax,\" \"federal income tax withheld,\" \"refund,\" or \"amount you owe\" by no more than $14.",wrongs:[null,"This reverses the thresholds — $50 goes with income/AGI, $14 with the tax fields.","Retransmission without new signatures is allowed within the tolerances.","Changes beyond the tolerances require new signatures/authorizations."]},
{n:29,topic:"The Taxpayer's Representative",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Jimmy prepared his own tax return using Turbo-Tax. Jimmy makes a large error on his tax return and later receives an IRS underreported notice. Which of the following individuals is NOT permitted to represent Jimmy before the IRS?",opts:["Pauly, a certified public accountant (CPA)","Sandra, a licensed enrolled agent","Trudy, who is Jimmy's mother","Ginger, an AFSP certificate holder"],a:3,why:"An AFSP holder may represent a taxpayer only for returns SHE prepared and signed. Jimmy self-prepared his return, so Ginger has no representation rights here.",wrongs:["CPAs have unlimited practice rights and may represent anyone.","EAs have unlimited practice rights.","Immediate family members (parent, spouse, child, sibling) may represent a taxpayer under the limited practice rules.",null]},
{n:30,topic:"Enrolled Practitioners",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"In order to practice before the IRS, attorneys and CPAs that are licensed to practice in a particular state must:",opts:["Be in good standing in that state and may practice before the IRS only in that state.","Take the EA exam in order to practice outside the state in which they are licensed.","Be in good standing in that state and may practice before the IRS in any state.","None of the above."],a:2,why:"IRS practice is federal. An attorney or CPA in good standing with any state licensing authority may practice before the IRS nationwide — state borders don't limit federal representation.",wrongs:["Their IRS practice rights are not confined to their licensing state.","No EA exam is needed; their state credential plus good standing suffices for IRS practice anywhere.",null,"C is correct."]}
,
{n:31,topic:"Federal Tax Law",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Sometimes, taxpayers will disagree with the IRS's interpretation of the Internal Revenue Code. The IRS is not bound to change its regulations due to a loss in court. The only exception to this rule is:",opts:["A U.S. Tax Court ruling.","A U.S. District Court ruling.","A Congressional sanction.","A Supreme Court ruling."],a:3,why:"Only a U.S. Supreme Court decision binds the IRS nationwide and forces it to conform its position. Decisions of lower courts bind only the parties (and circuits), and the IRS may 'nonacquiesce' and keep litigating elsewhere.",wrongs:["The IRS can nonacquiesce to Tax Court decisions.","District court rulings bind only that case/jurisdiction.","'Congressional sanction' isn't a court ruling — Congress changes the law itself, not IRS litigation posture.",null]},
{n:32,topic:"IRS Methods of Payment",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"All of the following statements regarding IRS installment agreements are correct EXCEPT:",opts:["An IRS levy may be served during an installment agreement.","During the course of the installment agreement, penalties and interest continue to accrue.","A taxpayer who owes $10,000 or less in taxes will be automatically approved for an installment agreement.","An installment agreement allows a taxpayer to pay a set amount toward a tax liability on a monthly basis."],a:0,why:"While an installment agreement is in effect (or pending), the IRS generally may NOT levy the taxpayer's property. That's what makes statement A false — and therefore the correct answer to this EXCEPT question.",wrongs:[null,"True — interest and late-payment penalties keep accruing during the agreement.","True — guaranteed installment agreements exist for liabilities of $10,000 or less (with compliance conditions).","True — that's the definition of an installment agreement."]},
{n:33,topic:"Due Diligence",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Shawn is an enrolled agent. He has a new client named Mary with three children. In determining whether Mary and her family are eligible for the Earned Income Tax Credit, Shawn:",opts:["Should review Social Security cards for each member of the family, but it is not required.","Should not review Social Security cards because they are not required to claim the Earned Income Tax Credit.","Is required to review Social Security cards for each member of the family.","Is prohibited from reviewing Social Security cards for each member of the family."],a:0,why:"Reviewing Social Security cards is a recommended best practice for EITC due diligence (valid SSNs are required for the credit), but no rule REQUIRES the preparer to inspect the physical cards. Due diligence requires reasonable inquiries and Form 8867 — not specific documents.",wrongs:[null,"Valid SSNs are required for EITC, so checking cards is clearly useful — it's just not mandatory.","No regulation mandates physically reviewing the cards.","Nothing prohibits reviewing them; clients can voluntarily provide them."]},
{n:34,topic:"Enrolled Agent Licensing",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"An enrolled agent is NOT eligible to practice before:",opts:["A U.S. tax court judge.","An IRS criminal investigation special agent.","A revenue agent.","A revenue officer."],a:0,why:"\"Practice before the IRS\" does not include the courts. Representing taxpayers in U.S. Tax Court requires admission to the Tax Court bar (attorneys, or non-attorneys who pass the Tax Court exam) — the EA credential alone doesn't qualify.",wrongs:[null,"EAs may represent clients before all administrative levels of the IRS, including Criminal Investigation.","Revenue agents (examiners) are IRS personnel — EAs practice before them routinely.","Revenue officers (collections) are IRS personnel — within EA practice rights."]},
{n:35,topic:"IRS Publications and Forms",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following types of guidance does NOT have \"substantial authority\" when it comes to federal tax law?",opts:["Official IRS publications.","Treasury regulations.","Court cases.","Congressional committee hearing reports."],a:0,why:"IRS publications are helpful plain-language summaries, but they are NOT substantial authority for tax positions. Authority comes from the Code, regulations, cases, rulings, and legislative history.",wrongs:[null,"Treasury regulations are among the highest administrative authority.","Court decisions are substantial authority.","Committee reports (legislative history) constitute substantial authority."]},
{n:36,topic:"E-File Program",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Safeguarding of IRS e-file from fraud and abuse is the responsibility of:",opts:["The IRS and the Police.","Authorized IRS e-file Providers.","The IRS and Authorized IRS e-file Providers.","The IRS."],a:2,why:"Per IRS Publication 3112, safeguarding IRS e-file from fraud and abuse is a SHARED responsibility of the IRS and Authorized IRS e-file Providers. Providers must be diligent, report suspected abuse, and cooperate with investigations.",wrongs:["Law enforcement isn't the designated partner in Pub 3112 — providers are.","Providers alone don't carry the whole burden; it's shared with the IRS.",null,"The IRS alone isn't responsible; providers share the duty."]},
{n:37,topic:"Applying to the E-File Program",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Steven is a tax preparer who works full-time as an employee for a CPA firm. He is not an EA, CPA, or attorney, but he has an AFSP certificate. He wants to become an ERO and get his own EFIN this year, so he can prepare tax returns from home as a side-business. Which of the following is NOT required in order for him to get his own EFIN?",opts:["He may have to go through a criminal background check when he submits his efile application.","He will have to get an e-services account in order to submit an efile application.","He will have to get a separate PTIN for his side-business. He cannot use the same PTIN when he prepares returns for his employer.","He will have to submit fingerprints with his efile application."],a:2,why:"A preparer has exactly ONE PTIN, which follows the individual — it's used for all returns prepared, whether for an employer or a side business. A separate PTIN is never issued.",wrongs:["True requirement — non-credentialed applicants undergo a suitability/background check.","True — the e-file application is submitted through an IRS e-services account.",null,"True — non-exempt applicants (not EA/CPA/attorney) must submit fingerprints."]},
{n:38,topic:"Disciplinary Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Fadil is a tax attorney who is representing a client before the IRS. Which of the following actions during the course of an examination would lead to an immediate referral and disciplinary action by the OPR?",opts:["Fadil made math errors on his client's tax return","Fadil failed to file his own tax return on time","Fadil knowingly provides false information to the IRS examiner","Fadil is not a U.S. citizen, but he does have a current license to practice law"],a:2,why:"Knowingly giving false or misleading information to the IRS is disreputable conduct under Circular 230 §10.51 and triggers mandatory referral to the Office of Professional Responsibility.",wrongs:["Honest math errors aren't misconduct.","A single late filing may be a compliance issue but isn't automatic grounds for immediate OPR referral (a willful PATTERN of noncompliance is different).",null,"U.S. citizenship is not required to practice; a licensed attorney in good standing may represent clients."]},
{n:39,topic:"Advising Clients on Potential Penalties",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Jasmine is an enrolled agent. In exercising her due diligence responsibilities, she discovers her new client, Jimmy, has omitted income on a prior year's Form 1040. Under Circular 230, she is required to:",opts:["She must amend his previous tax return and correct the error.","Report the unreported income to the IRS.","Promptly inform the client of the unreported income and advise the client of the consequences under the Code and regulations for that error.","Do nothing so as not to upset a new client."],a:2,why:"Circular 230 §10.21: upon discovering a client's error or omission, the practitioner must promptly advise the CLIENT of the error and its consequences. The practitioner cannot force an amendment and must not disclose to the IRS without consent.",wrongs:["The decision to amend belongs to the client — the practitioner can't unilaterally amend.","Disclosing to the IRS without client consent violates confidentiality.",null,"Silence violates the §10.21 duty to advise."]},
{n:40,topic:"Revenue Rulings and Revenue Procedures",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the legality of revenue rulings and revenue procedures?",opts:["Revenue rulings and revenue procedures are binding in court.","Revenue rulings and revenue procedures are not binding in court.","Revenue rulings are binding in court, but revenue procedures are not.","Revenue rulings and revenue procedures are binding in Tax Court, but not in U.S. district courts."],a:1,why:"Revenue rulings and procedures represent the IRS's official position and bind the IRS, but courts are not bound by them — they carry persuasive weight only. (They do count as substantial authority for penalty purposes.)",wrongs:["Courts may reject IRS rulings; only statutes, regulations (generally), and precedent bind courts.",null,"Neither type binds a court.","No court, including the Tax Court, is bound by them."]}
,
{n:41,topic:"Practice Before the IRS",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following actions does NOT constitute \"Practice Before the IRS\"?",opts:["Representing a taxpayer at a hearing.","Signing a closing agreement regarding a tax liability.","Communicating with the IRS for a taxpayer regarding the taxpayer's rights.","Furnishing information at the request of an IRS employee."],a:3,why:"Merely furnishing information at the IRS's request (or appearing as a witness) is expressly excluded from the definition of practice — anyone may do it, even suspended or disbarred individuals.",wrongs:["Representation at hearings/conferences is the core of practice.","Executing closing agreements on a client's behalf is practice.","Advocating and communicating on a taxpayer's rights or liabilities is practice.",null]},
{n:42,topic:"Paper Returns",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Which of the following is not considered an \"Electronic Return Submission\"?",opts:["Filing a paper return after generating the tax return in professional tax software.","Electronically sending the return to a Transmitter that will transmit the return to the IRS.","Providing a return to an Intermediate Service Provider for processing prior to transmission to the IRS.","Directly transmitting the return to the IRS."],a:0,why:"A return mailed on paper is a paper filing regardless of whether software was used to prepare it. Electronic submission requires electronic transmission to the IRS.",wrongs:[null,"Sending to a Transmitter is part of the e-file chain — electronic submission.","Using an Intermediate Service Provider before transmission is electronic submission.","Direct transmission to the IRS is obviously electronic submission."]},
{n:43,topic:"Offer in Compromise Program",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is the required period a taxpayer must remain in good standing with the IRS (by filing returns and paying on-time) after their Offer in Compromise (OIC) is accepted?",opts:["Five years","Ten years","Six years","One year"],a:0,why:"After an OIC is accepted, the taxpayer must stay compliant — timely filing and paying all taxes — for FIVE years (or until the offer is paid in full, if longer). Default means the IRS can reinstate the full original liability.",wrongs:[null,"Ten years is the collection statute (CSED), not the OIC compliance period.","Six years is not the OIC term.","One year is far too short."]},
{n:44,topic:"Freedom of Information Act Requests (FOIA)",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"A FOIA request for records can generally be made by any person to the IRS. However, the IRS may charge a reasonable fee for the records. What types of fees may the IRS charge under FOIA?",opts:["Fees for searching, reviewing, and copying records","Fees for a legal process server to deliver the records","Fees for temporary storage of the records","Fees for outside legal advice relating to the records request"],a:0,why:"FOIA authorizes agencies to charge reasonable standard fees for record SEARCH, REVIEW, and DUPLICATION, depending on the requester category.",wrongs:[null,"Process servers aren't part of FOIA fulfillment.","Storage fees aren't chargeable under FOIA.","Agencies can't bill requesters for legal advice."]},
{n:45,topic:"Disciplinary Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following statements regarding disbarment is correct?",opts:["Even without bringing a criminal prosecution, the IRS can disbar a practitioner.","The IRS can disbar a practitioner as long as a subsequent referral is made to the Department of Justice.","A practitioner is not liable for any preparer penalties if he diverts a portion of the refund to himself with the client's permission.","The IRS cannot disbar a practitioner without first seeking criminal prosecution through the Department of Justice."],a:0,why:"Disbarment is an ADMINISTRATIVE sanction imposed through OPR proceedings under Circular 230. No criminal prosecution or DOJ involvement is required.",wrongs:[null,"No DOJ referral is required for administrative discipline.","Diverting any portion of a client's refund is sanctionable misconduct — client 'permission' doesn't cure it (and endorsing/negotiating client refund checks is prohibited).","Criminal prosecution is not a prerequisite to disbarment."]},
{n:46,topic:"Tax Avoidance vs. Tax Evasion",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"IRS auditors are trained to look for indicators of fraudulent activity. Which of the following would be considered a \"badge of fraud\"?",opts:["Accounting irregularities found in a business that keeps two sets of books.","A low-income taxpayer with multiple children that claims the Earned Income Tax Credit.","A taxpayer with two businesses, each reported on a separate Schedule C.","High income reported by a taxpayer who lives and works overseas."],a:0,why:"Keeping two sets of books is a classic badge of fraud — deliberate concealment of income through accounting irregularities.",wrongs:[null,"Claiming EITC when eligible is lawful use of a credit.","Filing separate Schedules C for separate businesses is exactly what the rules require.","Earning high income abroad and reporting it is compliant behavior."]},
{n:47,topic:"Offer in Compromise Program",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is the primary purpose of an Offer in Compromise (OIC)?",opts:["To increase tax revenue for the government.","To encourage taxpayers to delay payment.","To settle tax debts for less than the full amount owed.","To penalize taxpayers who cannot pay their full tax liability."],a:2,why:"An OIC lets a taxpayer settle tax debt for less than the full amount owed when full payment is doubtful or would create hardship — providing a fresh start and collecting what's reasonably collectible.",wrongs:["It typically collects LESS than the assessed liability.","It's a settlement tool, not a delay mechanism.",null,"It's relief, not a penalty."]},
{n:48,topic:"Seeking Relief from Joint Liability (Including Innocent Spouse)",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"For purposes of federal tax law, which of the following statements is correct about joint tax returns?",opts:["Joint and several liability is a concept that does not apply if only one spouse earned all the income in a marriage.","Joint and several liability is a concept that applies to all currently-married spouses who file jointly.","Joint and several liability is a concept that applies to married spouses who file either joint returns or separate returns.","Joint and several liability is a concept that applies to married, separated, and divorced spouses who currently file jointly or have done so in the past."],a:3,why:"Each spouse who signs a joint return is fully liable for the entire tax — and that liability survives separation and divorce for years the couple filed jointly. A divorce decree cannot override it as to the IRS.",wrongs:["Liability applies regardless of which spouse earned the income.","It's not limited to CURRENTLY married couples — it follows past joint returns too.","MFS filers are each liable only for their own return; joint and several liability applies to JOINT returns.",null]},
{n:49,topic:"Due Diligence",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"What is the purpose of Form 8867, Paid Preparer's Due Diligence Checklist, in the context of the American Opportunity Tax Credit (AOTC)?",opts:["The Form 8867 is used to determine the correct amount of the AOTC.","The Form 8867 is used to list the educational institutions eligible for the AOTC.","The Form 8867 is used to verify the eligibility of the taxpayer for the AOTC.","The Form 8867 is used to claim the AOTC."],a:2,why:"Form 8867 documents that the paid preparer performed the required due diligence in determining the taxpayer's ELIGIBILITY for the credit (and HOH status). It must be submitted with the return.",wrongs:["Form 8863 computes the AOTC amount, not 8867.","No form lists eligible institutions; eligibility is checked via the school's status (e.g., FAFSA code).",null,"The credit is claimed on Form 8863, not 8867."]},
{n:50,topic:"Office of Professional Responsibility",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"The Office of Professional Responsibility (OPR) generally has responsibility for which practitioner-related matters?",opts:["Continuing Professional Education for enrolled practitioners.","Administering competency testing for the enrolled agent exam.","Practitioner conduct, disciplinary proceedings and sanctions.","Matters related to the issuance of PTINs."],a:2,why:"OPR has exclusive responsibility for practitioner conduct, discipline, and sanctions under Circular 230 (censure, suspension, disbarment, monetary penalties).",wrongs:["CE and enrollment administration belong to the RPO (Return Preparer Office).","The SEE is administered through the RPO and its testing vendor (Prometric).",null,"PTINs are issued by the Return Preparer Office."]}
,
{n:51,topic:"Tax Return Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Under Circular 230, a \"tax return\" is defined as:",opts:["An original return and a claim for refund.","An original return, an amended return, or an information return.","An original return, a claim for refund, or an amended return.","An original return only."],a:2,why:"Circular 230 defines \"tax return\" to include an original return, an amended return, AND a claim for refund.",wrongs:["This omits amended returns.","Information returns aren't part of the Circular 230 definition; claims for refund are.",null,"The definition is broader than just original returns."]},
{n:52,topic:"Disclosure Statements",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"When should a practitioner file Form 8275, Disclosure Statement?",opts:["To fulfill his EITC due diligence requirements.","To avoid accuracy-related penalties by disclosing positions on a tax return that are not otherwise adequately disclosed.","To submit a power of attorney for a client.","To explain why a tax return is being filed on paper rather than e-filed."],a:1,why:"Form 8275 discloses return positions that lack substantial authority but have a reasonable basis — adequate disclosure can protect the taxpayer and preparer from accuracy-related and preparer penalties.",wrongs:["EITC due diligence uses Form 8867.",null,"A POA is submitted on Form 2848.","Paper-filing explanations use Form 8948 (preparer explanation for not e-filing)."]},
{n:53,topic:"Small Case Procedure",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"All of the following statements are correct about the small tax case procedure of the U.S. Tax Court except:",opts:["The disputed tax must be $50,000 or less for any one year or period.","Cases are handled under simpler and less formal procedures than regular cases.","No appeal is available for cases decided under this procedure.","The tax must have been assessed and paid before any Tax Court proceedings."],a:3,why:"The Tax Court is the PREPAYMENT forum — a taxpayer petitions BEFORE paying the deficiency. So the statement that tax must be assessed and paid first is false, making it the exception.",wrongs:["True — S-case limit is $50,000 per year/period.","True — S cases use simplified, informal procedures.","True — S-case decisions are final and non-appealable.",null]},
{n:54,topic:"E-Signature Requirements",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Rachel donated a car to a charity, and the charity gave her a Form 1098-C which must be submitted with her return. Rachel wants to e-file if possible, but she has to find a way to attach the 1098-C. Which form is used to attach one or more documents to an e-filed return?",opts:["Form 8879","Form 8453","Schedule J","Form 3112"],a:1,why:"Form 8453, U.S. Individual Income Tax Transmittal for an IRS e-file Return, is used to mail required paper attachments (like Form 1098-C) to the IRS after e-filing.",wrongs:["Form 8879 is the e-file signature authorization, not a transmittal.",null,"Schedule J is income averaging for farmers/fishermen.","'Form 3112' — Publication 3112 covers e-file provider rules; it's not an attachment form."]},
{n:55,topic:"Prohibited Actions During Suspension or Disbarment",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Edward is an enrolled agent who was disbarred from practice this year. He has an existing client, Celestine, who is living overseas in France. She has no U.S. bank account and has an IRS refund coming as a paper check. Can Edward receive Celestine's IRS refund check and hold it for her?",opts:["Yes, Edward can receive the check, but NOT cash it. He can then forward the check to Celestine by mail or private delivery service.","Yes, Edward can receive the check and cash it, then wire his client the money, as long as Celestine signs a power of attorney allowing him to do so.","No, Edward cannot receive the check, or cash it.","Yes, Edward can receive the check but NOT cash it, as long as Celestine signs a power of attorney allowing him to do so."],a:2,why:"A disbarred practitioner may not engage in any conduct connected with practice, and receiving or negotiating a client's refund check is prohibited. Even ACTIVE practitioners may never endorse or negotiate client refund checks.",wrongs:["He's disbarred — he can't receive client refund checks at all.","Cashing a client's refund check is prohibited for any practitioner, let alone a disbarred one.",null,"A POA can't authorize a disbarred person to handle client refund checks."]},
{n:56,topic:"Diligence as to Accuracy, Best Practices, and Competence",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Lionel is a tax preparer. His new client, Emilee, had her prior-year return prepared at another tax office and brings it to her appointment. Emilee has a capital loss carryover from the prior year from selling cryptocurrency at a loss. Is Lionel permitted to use the prior year return to calculate the carryover, even though he did not prepare it?",opts:["Lionel can only rely on the work product of another preparer if his client signs a waiver.","Lionel cannot rely on the work product of another preparer. He must figure out the carryover himself by requesting his client's prior-year records.","Lionel is required to contact the prior preparer to confirm the amount of the carryover.","Lionel may rely on another preparer's work product, but due care must be taken if he relies on the work product of another person."],a:3,why:"A preparer may rely in good faith on another preparer's work product, exercising due care in evaluating it (e.g., checking that the carryover appears reasonable and consistent).",wrongs:["No waiver is required to use a prior return.","Recomputing from scratch isn't required if reliance is reasonable.","There's no duty to contact the prior preparer.",null]},
{n:57,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"With the taxpayer's properly signed power of attorney, an enrolled agent can:",opts:["Receive and endorse a taxpayer's refund check and invest the proceeds in the taxpayer's own mutual fund.","Receive a taxpayer's refund check and endorse it in order to pay tax preparation fees only.","Receive a taxpayer's refund check and deposit it in order to split the fees.","Receive a taxpayer's refund check, but not endorse or cash the check."],a:3,why:"A POA can authorize a practitioner to RECEIVE (but never endorse, negotiate, or cash) a client's refund check. Endorsing or negotiating client refund checks is flatly prohibited by Circular 230 §10.31.",wrongs:["Endorsing and investing the proceeds is prohibited.","Endorsing to collect fees is prohibited — no exceptions.","Depositing a client's refund into the practitioner's account is prohibited.",null]},
{n:58,topic:"Due Diligence",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"The IRS makes due-diligence educational phone calls and office visits to preparers to:",opts:["Review specific client return information","Request client records","Demand payment","Help preparers meet due diligence requirements and avoid penalties"],a:3,why:"The IRS's due diligence educational contacts (letters, calls, visits) are outreach designed to help preparers understand and comply with EITC/CTC/AOTC/HOH due diligence rules BEFORE penalties become necessary.",wrongs:["Educational contacts aren't examinations of specific client returns.","Records requests happen in due diligence AUDITS, not educational visits.","They are not collection actions.",null]},
{n:59,topic:"IRS Collections & Enforcement of Unpaid Taxes",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is a \"certificate of service\" in relation to an IRS tax lien?",opts:["A certificate that must be signed to document that a taxpayer was properly served with a summons.","An official notice that a taxpayer will be subject to a tax lien within 90 days.","A certificate that must be signed by a third-party record keeper when he is served with a summons.","An official notice that a taxpayer is scheduled to appear in a U.S. district court to contest a tax lien."],a:0,why:"A certificate of service is the signed documentation proving the taxpayer was properly served with a summons — establishing that legal service requirements were met.",wrongs:[null,"It's proof of service, not an advance lien warning.","It documents service ON the taxpayer; the third party doesn't sign it.","It's not a court appearance notice."]},
{n:60,topic:"IRS Written Determinations (TAM, PLR)",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Is the Internal Revenue Manual (IRM) considered \"substantial authority\" for positions taken on a tax return?",opts:["Only for tax submission processing.","No, the IRM is not considered substantial authority.","Only for criminal investigations.","Yes, the IRM is considered substantial authority."],a:1,why:"The IRM is the IRS's internal employee handbook. It confers no rights on taxpayers and is NOT substantial authority for return positions.",wrongs:["There's no processing carve-out making it authority.",null,"No criminal-investigation exception exists either.","It is internal guidance only — never substantial authority."]}
,
{n:61,topic:"E-File Advertising Standards",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"E-file providers must not use improper or misleading advertising in relation to IRS e-file, including RALs. Which of the following statements would be an example of prohibited advertising?",opts:["E-file providers who offer RALs may refer to or describe the RAL as a short-term loan.","E-file providers may advertise the availability of a RAL, and may advertise a flat fee for providing this financial service.","E-file providers who offer RALs may refer to or describe the funds as a quicker \"tax refund.\"","E-file providers who advertise the availability of a RAL may refer to or describe the funds as a loan or other financial product."],a:2,why:"Describing a Refund Anticipation Loan as the taxpayer's actual \"refund\" (or a faster refund) is prohibited as misleading. A RAL is a loan and must be advertised as one.",wrongs:["Calling it a short-term loan is accurate and permitted.","Advertising RAL availability with a flat fee is permitted.",null,"Describing the funds as a loan/financial product is the REQUIRED accurate description."]},
{n:62,topic:"Advising Clients on Potential Penalties",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Ryuu is an enrolled agent who works as an independent tax consultant. In the course of consulting work for a building contractor, Ryuu discovers the company has been taking tax deductions to which it is not entitled. He does not prepare the company's tax returns. Does Ryuu have any obligations under Circular 230?",opts:["No, because Circular 230 only covers ethical issues related to individual tax returns, not business tax returns.","Yes, he must notify his client of the erroneous deductions and the consequences of not correcting them.","No, because Circular 230 only covers practitioners preparing tax returns and does not pertain to tax advisors or consultants.","Yes, he must notify his client of the erroneous deductions, as well as correct the errors. If the client refuses to correct the errors, he must terminate his consulting engagement."],a:1,why:"The §10.21 duty applies to any practitioner who knows of a client's noncompliance or error — even if he didn't prepare the returns. He must advise the client of the error and the consequences of not correcting it. Nothing more is required.",wrongs:["Circular 230 covers all federal tax matters, business and individual alike.",null,"Circular 230 governs practitioners in all federal tax work, including advising and consulting.","He is not required to correct the errors himself or terminate the engagement — only to advise the client."]},
{n:63,topic:"Tax Avoidance vs. Tax Evasion",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"What is the consequence of tax avoidance?",opts:["Increased tax liability","Imprisonment","Legal reduction of taxable income","Fines and penalties"],a:2,why:"Tax AVOIDANCE is the legal use of deductions, credits, and planning to reduce taxes. Its consequence is simply a lawful, lower tax bill. Tax EVASION — willful illegal concealment — is what triggers fines and imprisonment.",wrongs:["Avoidance decreases, not increases, liability.","Imprisonment is a consequence of evasion, not avoidance.",null,"Penalties attach to evasion and noncompliance, not legal avoidance."]},
{n:64,topic:"IRS Collections & Enforcement of Unpaid Taxes",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Once the IRS sends out a Notice of Intent to Levy, how long must the IRS wait before it can seize taxpayer property to satisfy the tax debt?",opts:["30 days.","45 days.","10 days.","60 days."],a:0,why:"The IRS must send a Final Notice of Intent to Levy and Notice of Your Right to a Hearing at least 30 DAYS before levying — the same window in which the taxpayer may request a CDP hearing.",wrongs:[null,"45 days is not the statutory waiting period.","10 days relates to the notice-and-demand stage, before the levy notice.","60 days is not the levy waiting period."]},
{n:65,topic:"Tax Refunds",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"With regards to Direct Deposit of IRS refunds, a taxpayer may NOT designate refunds to which type of account?",opts:["Traditional IRA account.","Coverdell education savings account (ESA).","Checking Account.","Credit Card account."],a:3,why:"Refund direct deposits can only go to accounts that accept ACH deposits in the taxpayer's name — checking, savings, IRAs, HSAs, Coverdell ESAs, TreasuryDirect, etc. Credit card accounts are not eligible deposit destinations.",wrongs:["IRAs are allowed direct deposit destinations (Form 8888).","Coverdell ESAs are allowed.","Checking accounts are the most common destination.",null]},
{n:66,topic:"Tax Return Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"How often is a tax preparer required to renew his or her PTIN?",opts:["Annually.","Every two years.","Every three years.","No renewal is required once a preparer has been assigned a PTIN."],a:0,why:"PTINs expire December 31 each year and must be renewed annually for the upcoming filing season.",wrongs:[null,"Two years is not a PTIN cycle.","Three years is the EA enrollment renewal cycle, not PTIN.","PTINs lapse without annual renewal."]},
{n:67,topic:"Federal Tax Law",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Section 330 of Title 31 of the US Code authorizes what type of regulations?",opts:["Regulation of the practice of representatives before the U.S. Tax Court.","Regulation of the practice of representatives before U.S. Congress.","Regulation of the practice of representatives before the Department of the Treasury, including the IRS, and determinations of practitioner \"fitness\" to practice.","Regulation of the practice of representatives before the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network."],a:2,why:"31 USC §330 is the statutory authority for Circular 230 — it authorizes Treasury to regulate practice of representatives before the Department (including the IRS) and to require demonstration of good character, reputation, qualifications, and competency.",wrongs:["The Tax Court regulates its own bar; §330 covers Treasury practice.","Lobbying Congress is not within §330.",null,"FinCEN practice is not the subject of §330."]},
{n:68,topic:"Contact of Third Parties",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Garrett's tax return is under examination. The IRS wants to ask his employer some questions related to Garrett's tax issues. There is no pending criminal investigation and no evidence that contacting his employer will result in reprisal or jeopardize collection. Before the IRS contacts Garrett's employer, which action must the IRS take?",opts:["Obtain Garrett's written permission for the contact.","Mail Garrett a statement of his legal rights.","Provide Garrett with reasonable notice of the contact.","Obtain a search warrant to search Garrett's workplace."],a:2,why:"IRC §7602(c) requires the IRS to give the taxpayer reasonable advance notice before contacting third parties about the taxpayer's liability (absent jeopardy, reprisal risk, or criminal investigation).",wrongs:["Permission isn't required — only notice.","The requirement is notice of intended third-party contacts, not a general rights statement.",null,"No warrant is needed to ask an employer questions."]},
{n:69,topic:"E-File Program",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Are financial institutions or other fiduciaries that file Forms 1041 as a trustee required to e-file Form 1041?",opts:["No. Fiduciaries who file returns in their fiduciary capacity are not considered tax return preparers and are, therefore, not covered by the e-file requirement.","Yes, fiduciaries who file returns in their fiduciary capacity are covered by the e-file requirement.","Fiduciaries who file returns in their fiduciary capacity are required to e-file if they are paid for their services.","Fiduciaries who file returns in their fiduciary capacity are covered by the e-file requirement only if they file 11 or more returns during a taxable year."],a:0,why:"The e-file mandate applies to \"specified tax return preparers.\" A fiduciary filing returns for the trust/estate it administers is acting in a fiduciary capacity — not as a tax return preparer — so the mandate doesn't apply.",wrongs:[null,"Fiduciaries are outside the preparer e-file mandate.","Compensation as a fiduciary doesn't convert them into preparers.","The 11-return threshold applies to preparers, which fiduciaries are not."]},
{n:70,topic:"How Returns are Selected for Examination",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"How does the IRS use third-party information in the selection of tax returns for examination?",opts:["To verify the taxpayer's legal identity","To calculate the taxpayer's refund","To determine the taxpayer's eligibility for legitimate tax deductions","To match the information reported on the tax return"],a:3,why:"The IRS's Information Returns Processing / document-matching program compares W-2s, 1099s, and other third-party reports against what taxpayers report. Mismatches flag returns for notices (CP2000) or examination.",wrongs:["Identity verification is a separate process, not exam selection.","Refunds are computed from the return itself.","Deduction eligibility is determined during an exam, not by matching for selection.",null]}
,
{n:71,topic:"Firm Compliance Procedures",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Karen is the owner of a tax firm who supervises the work of 15 preparers. After tax season, she becomes aware that one of her employees has been claiming improper deductions for his clients and diverting part of his clients' refunds for his own use. Karen discusses the situation with her employee, but takes no other action because she is afraid she will get sued. Is Karen in violation of Circular 230?",opts:["Insufficient information to determine the answer.","Yes, she is in violation of Circular 230.","No, she is not responsible for the actions of an employee.","No, because she had no direct knowledge of the wrongdoing while it was happening."],a:1,why:"Under Circular 230 §10.36, a practitioner with principal authority over a firm's tax practice must take reasonable steps to ensure firm compliance — and can be sanctioned if she knows of noncompliance and fails to take prompt corrective action. Merely 'discussing it' and doing nothing violates this duty.",wrongs:["The facts are sufficient: she knew and failed to act.",null,"Firm principals ARE responsible for establishing and enforcing compliance procedures.","Liability attaches once she learns of it and fails to correct it — contemporaneous knowledge isn't required."]},
{n:72,topic:"Prohibited Actions During Suspension or Disbarment",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Thaddeus is a CPA who was disbarred for criminal tax fraud. Which of the following taxpayers may he represent before the IRS?",opts:["Thaddeus may represent an estate if he was an attorney for the estate before he was disbarred.","Thaddeus may represent a client with a Trust Fund Recovery Penalty, as long as it is only related to payroll filings.","Thaddeus may represent a corporation if he filed the corporate return.","Thaddeus may represent an irrevocable trust as the trust's fiduciary."],a:3,why:"A fiduciary (trustee) stands in the shoes of the entity — he IS the taxpayer, not a representative. Acting as a trust's fiduciary is therefore not 'practice' and remains permissible even after disbarment.",wrongs:["Prior engagements don't survive disbarment — representation rights are gone.","Disbarment removes all representation rights, including TFRP matters.","Having prepared the return grants no rights to a disbarred individual.",null]},
{n:73,topic:"IRS Methods of Payment",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Chaya owes federal taxes from a prior year. She is currently paying on an installment agreement and has never been late on her payments. In the current year, she files her tax return and discovers she is eligible for a large refund. What will happen?",opts:["The refund will be allocated ratably over the life of her installment agreement.","The refund will be released if she files a hardship petition.","The refund will be allocated to a future tax debt.","Her refund will be applied against the delinquent amount that she owes."],a:3,why:"As a condition of every installment agreement, any refund due in a later year is OFFSET against the outstanding tax liability. The offset doesn't replace her regular monthly payment, but it does reduce the balance.",wrongs:["There's no ratable allocation — the full refund is applied at once.","Hardship relief doesn't release refunds owed against existing federal tax debt in an IA.","It's applied to the EXISTING delinquency, not future debts.",null]},
{n:74,topic:"Practice Before the IRS",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Of the following statements, which one is NOT considered practice before the Internal Revenue Service?",opts:["Representing a taxpayer at conferences, hearings, or meetings with the Internal Revenue Service.","Preparing and filing necessary documents at the request of the Internal Revenue Service for a taxpayer and discussing issues.","Preparing a client's tax return at the request of the Internal Revenue Service.","Communicating with the Internal Revenue Service for a taxpayer regarding the taxpayer's rights, privileges, or liabilities under laws and regulations administered by the Internal Revenue Service."],a:2,why:"Mere tax return preparation is expressly NOT practice before the IRS — anyone with a PTIN may prepare returns. Practice means representation: advocacy, communication, and appearing on a taxpayer's behalf.",wrongs:["Representation at conferences/hearings is core practice.","Preparing and filing documents on a taxpayer's behalf and discussing issues with the IRS is practice.",null,"Communicating with the IRS on a taxpayer's rights or liabilities is practice."]},
{n:75,topic:"Rules for Limited Practice",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following individuals is NOT allowed to practice before the IRS?",opts:["An enrolled agent who has been censured by the IRS.","An enrolled agent who has been placed on inactive status.","A U.S. resident alien who is also an enrolled agent.","A nonresident alien that is an enrolled agent."],a:1,why:"An EA on INACTIVE status (e.g., failed to renew or complete CE) may not practice until reinstated to active status.",wrongs:["Censure is a public reprimand — practice rights continue.",null,"Citizenship/residency status doesn't matter; an EA in good standing may practice.","Even nonresident aliens can be EAs and practice if enrolled and active."]},
{n:76,topic:"Revenue Rulings and Revenue Procedures",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following IRS pronouncements have the highest authority?",opts:["Revenue rulings.","Private letter rulings.","Official IRS publications.","Advice published on the official IRS website."],a:0,why:"Among these, revenue rulings rank highest: they're published official IRS positions applying the law to facts, citable as precedent and substantial authority.",wrongs:[null,"PLRs bind the IRS only as to the requesting taxpayer and can't be cited as precedent by others.","Publications are plain-language guides without substantial authority.","Website FAQs/advice carry even less authoritative weight."]},
{n:77,topic:"Offer in Compromise Program",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Corbin had been negotiating an offer in compromise with the IRS, but the IRS turned down his offer. How much time does Corbin have to appeal the rejected offer?",opts:["60 days.","10 days.","90 days.","30 days."],a:3,why:"A rejected OIC may be appealed within 30 days of the rejection letter using Form 13711 (Request for Appeal of Offer in Compromise).",wrongs:["60 days is not the OIC appeal window.","10 days is too short.","90 days is the Tax Court petition period for deficiency notices, not OIC appeals.",null]},
{n:78,topic:"Advertising Restrictions",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following statements is correct regarding a practitioner's right to advertise his services, per Circular 230?",opts:["Advertising is allowed for enrolled agents, but not for other practitioners.","Advertising is always allowed.","Advertising is never allowed.","Advertising is allowed, but it cannot violate federal or state law or other applicable rules"],a:3,why:"Circular 230 §10.30 permits advertising but prohibits false, fraudulent, coercive, or misleading claims, and requires compliance with all applicable laws (e.g., EAs must use approved descriptions like 'enrolled to represent taxpayers before the IRS').",wrongs:["All practitioner categories may advertise.","It's allowed subject to restrictions — not unconditionally.","It is clearly permitted.",null]},
{n:79,topic:"Paper Returns",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Angela's son, David, is 2 years old. David is a popular child actor and has worked on several diaper commercials. David has a filing requirement due to his acting income, but he is too young to sign his return. What is the proper procedure for submitting a return for a minor child who cannot sign?",opts:["David's mother, Angela, can sign her child's name in the space provided.","The return should be signed by David using a notary.","The return can be signed using David's thumbprint.","The return must remain unsigned, because legally, only David can sign his own return."],a:0,why:"A parent or guardian signs the child's name in the signature area followed by \"By (parent's signature), parent for minor child.\"",wrongs:[null,"A notary can't supply a signature for a 2-year-old.","Thumbprints are not an IRS signature method.","Returns must be signed; the parent signs for the minor."]},
{n:80,topic:"Prohibited Actions During Suspension or Disbarment",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Clovis is an attorney who was stripped of his license and disbarred by his state licensing authority for felony wire fraud. Which of the following is NOT a prohibited action for Clovis to perform, even though he is currently disbarred?",opts:["Signing a tax return on behalf of a taxpayer","Furnish information at the request of the IRS or any of its officers or employees","Signing waivers, consents, or closing agreements on behalf of a client","Providing written advice on potential tax avoidance arrangements"],a:1,why:"Furnishing information at the IRS's request (and appearing as a witness) is not 'practice' — so even a disbarred individual may do it.",wrongs:["Signing returns as a representative is prohibited conduct connected with practice.",null,"Executing waivers/consents/closing agreements for clients is practice — prohibited.","Rendering written tax advice is practice — prohibited while disbarred."]}
,
{n:81,topic:"Revenue Rulings and Revenue Procedures",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"What is the primary purpose of a Revenue Procedure?",opts:["To inform and guide taxpayers concerning an IRS position.","To announce pending changes in tax laws before Congress passes them.","To summarize U.S. Tax Court decisions for better understanding by IRS personnel.","To provide general tax advice to the U.S. Tax Court."],a:0,why:"A revenue procedure is an official published statement of IRS procedure and administration that guides taxpayers on how to comply — e.g., how to make elections, request rulings, or follow filing procedures.",wrongs:[null,"The IRS doesn't announce legislation before Congress acts.","Revenue procedures aren't court-decision digests.","The IRS doesn't advise the Tax Court."]},
{n:82,topic:"Diligence as to Accuracy, Best Practices, and Competence",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"What are the consequences of a tax preparer not meeting due diligence requirements?",opts:["The IRS can initiate disciplinary action through the Office of Professional Responsibility","The IRS can expel the preparer from the IRS efile program","The IRS can assess a preparer penalty","All of the above"],a:3,why:"Due diligence failures can trigger all three: §6695(g) monetary penalties per failure, OPR disciplinary action for Circular 230 practitioners, and suspension or expulsion from IRS e-file.",wrongs:["True, but incomplete by itself.","True, but incomplete by itself.","True, but incomplete by itself.",null]},
{n:83,topic:"E-Signature Requirements",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"On an e-filed tax return prepared by a paid preparer, who must sign the tax return?",opts:["Taxpayer, paid preparer, and ERO.","Taxpayer.","Taxpayer and paid preparer.","Taxpayer, paid preparer, ERO, and transmitter."],a:2,why:"The return itself must be signed by the taxpayer (via PIN/e-signature) and the paid preparer. The ERO signs the Form 8879 authorization — not the return — and transmitters sign nothing.",wrongs:["The ERO signs Form 8879, not the return itself (often the preparer and ERO are the same person, but the roles differ).","The paid preparer must also sign.",null,"Transmitters never sign returns."]},
{n:84,topic:"IRS Written Determinations (TAM, PLR)",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"What is the primary purpose of the Internal Revenue Manual (IRM)?",opts:["To serve as a guide for IRS employees in all aspects of their work.","To provide taxpayers with detailed tax filing instructions for their individual tax returns.","To announce new tax laws and regulations in a timely manner.","To publish IRS annual reports."],a:0,why:"The IRM is the IRS's internal operations handbook — policies, procedures, and guidelines for IRS employees performing their duties.",wrongs:[null,"Taxpayer filing instructions come from form instructions and publications.","New laws and regs are announced via the Internal Revenue Bulletin, not the IRM.","Annual reports are separate publications."]},
{n:85,topic:"IRS Information Requests",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Gabby is an enrolled agent who has information about possible misconduct involving another tax preparer. What is her responsibility if the IRS's Office of Professional Responsibility contacts her?",opts:["She must furnish information about the preparer's possible misconduct, but she would not be required to testify in disbarment or suspension proceedings.","She must furnish information about the preparer's possible misconduct and may be required to testify in disbarment or suspension proceedings.","She is not required to furnish information about the preparer's possible misconduct.","She can choose whether or not to furnish information about the preparer's possible misconduct."],a:1,why:"Under Circular 230 §10.20(b), when OPR (or any IRS officer) lawfully requests information concerning possible violations by another person, a practitioner must provide it — and may be required to testify in disciplinary proceedings — unless she believes in good faith and on reasonable grounds the information is privileged.",wrongs:["Testimony CAN be required.",null,"Compliance is mandatory absent privilege.","It is a duty, not a choice."]},
{n:86,topic:"Durable Power of Attorney and Other POA Topics",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Darrel is an enrolled agent. He fired his client, Madalyn, for non-payment of her bill. He wants to withdraw from representation, but he has a power of attorney in effect with the IRS. How can Darrel withdraw from the representation?",opts:["A POA automatically expires after one year from the date of signing, so Darrel does not need to take any special action.","Darrel must write the word \"REVOKE\" on the POA form and mail or fax it to the IRS.","Darrel can call the IRS directly and revoke the power of attorney over the phone.","Darrel must write the word \"WITHDRAW\" on the POA form and send it to the IRS via mail, fax or secure document upload."],a:3,why:"A REPRESENTATIVE withdrawing writes \"WITHDRAW\" across the top of the first page of the Form 2848 copy, signs and dates it, and sends it to the IRS. (\"REVOKE\" is what the TAXPAYER writes when revoking.)",wrongs:["POAs don't auto-expire after one year.","REVOKE is the taxpayer's action, not the representative's.","Withdrawal can't be done by phone — it requires the written, signed submission.",null]},
{n:87,topic:"Penalties Imposed Upon Taxpayers",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Edwin's return was selected for examination. The examiner found that he failed to report hundreds of cash deposits from his business. The underpayment on his return was determined to be due to fraud. What is the penalty for a fraudulent underpayment of tax?",opts:["75% of the underpayment of tax.","25% of the underpayment of tax.","100% of the underpayment of tax.","50% of the underpayment of tax."],a:0,why:"The civil fraud penalty under IRC §6663 is 75% of the portion of the underpayment attributable to fraud.",wrongs:[null,"25% is the maximum failure-to-file or failure-to-pay accumulation, not fraud.","100% relates to the trust fund recovery penalty, not fraudulent underpayment.","50% is not the civil fraud rate."]},
{n:88,topic:"IRS Divisions and the Taxpayer Advocate",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Under which of the following scenarios will the Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS) provide assistance to a taxpayer?",opts:["If the taxpayer needs help to appeal an adverse U.S. Tax Court decision.","If the taxpayer needs representation during an IRS examination.","If the taxpayer needs help filing their tax return because they do not speak English.","If the taxpayer has made multiple attempts to contact the IRS without receiving a response."],a:3,why:"TAS helps when normal IRS channels have broken down — e.g., repeated unanswered contacts, delays past normal timeframes, or financial hardship from IRS action.",wrongs:["Court appeals are litigation — outside TAS's role.","TAS doesn't act as a taxpayer's representative in examinations.","Return preparation help comes from VITA/TCE programs, not TAS.",null]},
{n:89,topic:"Disciplinary Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following is specifically cited as conduct that could trigger sanctions against a tax preparer, according to Circular 230 §10.51?",opts:["Parking tickets.","Conviction on a criminal offense charge.","Felony arrest for breach of trust.","Felony arrest on a criminal offense charge."],a:1,why:"§10.51 lists CONVICTION of a criminal offense under federal tax laws, or of any offense involving dishonesty or breach of trust, as incompetence/disreputable conduct. The key word is conviction, not arrest.",wrongs:["Parking tickets aren't disreputable conduct.",null,"An ARREST isn't a conviction — §10.51 requires conviction.","Same flaw: arrest alone doesn't trigger §10.51."]},
{n:90,topic:"Enrolled Agent Licensing",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Ander is an enrolled agent who provided advice on a client's tax return and prepared a single schedule of the return. His business partner, Helga, also an enrolled agent, prepared the rest of the return, including a Schedule K-1 that included 85% of the income reported. Who is considered the \"preparer\" of the return for signing purposes?",opts:["Ander.","Since both helped prepare the return, either Helga or Ander may sign it.","Helga.","Both Helga and Ander."],a:2,why:"The signing preparer is the one with primary responsibility — the person who prepared the SUBSTANTIAL portion of the return. Helga prepared most of it, including the schedule with 85% of the income.",wrongs:["Ander prepared only a single schedule and gave advice — not the substantial portion.","Only one individual signs as preparer: the one primarily responsible.",null,"Only one preparer signature goes on a return."]}
,
{n:91,topic:"Privacy of Taxpayer Information and Allowable Disclosures",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Rashid is an enrolled agent. He believes a client may be engaged in felonious business activities, so he reports his suspicions to authorities. Which of the following statements is correct regarding Rashid's action?",opts:["He needed to obtain written consent from his client before disclosing information to authorities.","He is allowed to report a possible crime to authorities, assuming he does so in good faith.","He is in violation of IRC §7216, which is a criminal provision designed to punish tax preparers who disclose private client information.","He must have absolute proof that his client is committing a crime before he is allowed to disclose the information to authorities."],a:1,why:"The confidentiality rules (including §7216) contain exceptions — a preparer may in good faith report suspected criminal activity to appropriate authorities without client consent.",wrongs:["Consent isn't required to report suspected crimes in good faith.",null,"§7216 targets improper disclosure/use of return information for gain — not good-faith crime reporting, which is excepted.","Good-faith belief suffices; absolute proof is not the standard."]},
{n:92,topic:"IRS Divisions and the Taxpayer Advocate",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Sophia's return was selected for examination. Her IRS examiner has refused to return her calls and has rescheduled her audit three times. Sophia believes the IRS is not handling her case in a timely and appropriate manner. Who should she contact for assistance?",opts:["Taxpayer Advocate Service.","IRS Help Line.","IRS Appeals office.","U.S. Tax Court."],a:0,why:"When normal IRS processes break down — unreturned calls, repeated delays — the Taxpayer Advocate Service is the independent body that intervenes on the taxpayer's behalf.",wrongs:[null,"The general help line can't fix a mishandled exam assignment.","Appeals handles disputes over exam RESULTS, not process complaints during the exam.","Tax Court requires a notice of deficiency — premature here."]},
{n:93,topic:"Offer in Compromise Program",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Sabrina gets a notice from the IRS that says she owes $19,000. She does not agree. Donald, an enrolled agent, pulls her transcripts and finds duplicated income on her self-prepared return — she accidentally entered her rental income twice. She didn't receive other notices because she was living overseas, and her account has gone to collections. Donald recommends an offer in compromise. What type of OIC should be submitted?",opts:["Sabrina cannot submit an OIC based on these facts.","Doubt as to collectibility","Doubt as to liability","Effective tax administration"],a:2,why:"She doesn't actually OWE the assessed tax — the liability itself is wrong due to the duplicated income. That is precisely a Doubt as to Liability OIC (Form 656-L).",wrongs:["An OIC is available; the liability is genuinely disputed.","Doubt as to collectibility applies when the tax is correct but can't be fully collected — not the issue here.",null,"Effective tax administration applies when the tax is correct and collectible but collection would be inequitable/hardship."]},
{n:94,topic:"Referrals to OPR",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Donnelly is a self-employed carpenter whose Schedule C return is audited. He hires Karen, a CPA, to represent him. Karen says she will \"do whatever it takes\" and helps him fabricate receipts in Photoshop. The IRS examiner, Mr. Smith, suspects Karen is lying and that receipts have been altered or fabricated. What action must Mr. Smith take if he believes Karen has violated Circular 230?",opts:["Mr. Smith should write a letter to the taxpayer, and also issue a written warning to Karen, the CPA","Mr. Smith should discuss the violation with Karen, the CPA","Mr. Smith should issue a verbal warning to Karen, the CPA","Mr. Smith must file a written report to the OPR"],a:3,why:"IRS employees who believe a practitioner has violated Circular 230 are REQUIRED to make a written referral (Form 8484) to the Office of Professional Responsibility.",wrongs:["Warnings to the practitioner aren't the prescribed procedure.","Informal discussion doesn't satisfy the mandatory referral duty.","Verbal warnings aren't the required action.",null]},
{n:95,topic:"Official Complaint Process and Response",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following statements is correct regarding an official OPR complaint against a practitioner?",opts:["The duration must be specified only when a reprimand is recommended.","The duration of the suspension does not have to be specified.","If a suspension is sought, the duration must be specified.","The duration must be specified only when disbarment is recommended."],a:2,why:"When the complaint seeks suspension, it must specify the duration of the suspension sought.",wrongs:["Reprimands have no duration to specify.","The duration MUST be stated when suspension is sought.",null,"Disbarment is indefinite (minimum 5 years before reinstatement petition) — no duration to specify."]},
{n:96,topic:"Official Complaint Process and Response",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Odin is an administrative law judge who presided over a disbarment hearing of an enrolled agent, Frank, who is accused of tax fraud. How long does he have to render his decision about Frank's disbarment?",opts:["90 days.","60 days.","180 days.","120 days."],a:2,why:"The administrative law judge must enter a decision within 180 days after the conclusion of the hearing (Circular 230 §10.76).",wrongs:["90 days is not the ALJ deadline.","60 days is not the ALJ deadline.",null,"120 days is not the ALJ deadline."]},
{n:97,topic:"Offer in Compromise Program",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Kellie has over $200,000 in various debts, which she is unable to pay. She files for bankruptcy during the year. She also owes back taxes and would like to file an Offer in Compromise. When can she file an Offer in Compromise?",opts:["Kellie can file an Offer in Compromise once her bankruptcy is finalized and her debt is discharged and closed.","Kellie cannot file an Offer in Compromise if she has filed for bankruptcy in the last three years.","Kellie must file an Offer in Compromise after she files for bankruptcy, but before the bankruptcy is finalized.","Kellie must file an Offer in Compromise before she files for bankruptcy."],a:0,why:"A taxpayer in an OPEN bankruptcy proceeding is ineligible for an OIC — tax debts are handled within the bankruptcy. She may file an OIC only after the bankruptcy is resolved (discharged and closed).",wrongs:[null,"There's no three-year lookback ban — only the open-proceeding bar.","During an open bankruptcy the IRS will return the offer.","Filing before bankruptcy isn't required; the OIC would be terminated when bankruptcy is filed anyway."]},
{n:98,topic:"US Tax Court",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"In an IRS examination, who has the \"burden of proof\" when there is a question raised about deductions or other items claimed on a tax return?",opts:["Substantiation only applies to items of $1,000 or more on a tax return.","The IRS.","Both the IRS and the taxpayer have an equal burden of proof.","The taxpayer who is being audited."],a:3,why:"Deductions and credits are a matter of \"legislative grace\" — the taxpayer must substantiate entitlement to every item claimed. The burden of proof in an exam rests on the taxpayer.",wrongs:["There is no $1,000 threshold for substantiation.","The IRS bears the burden only in limited situations (e.g., fraud, or under §7491 when strict conditions are met).","The burdens aren't equal — the taxpayer must prove the items.",null]},
{n:99,topic:"Practice Before the IRS",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following disciplined practitioners may represent clients before the IRS?",opts:["Donald, a practitioner who has been suspended from practice for two years.","Angela, a practitioner who has been publicly censured by OPR.","Grady, a practitioner who has been disbarred.","None of these individuals may practice before the IRS in any capacity."],a:1,why:"Censure is a PUBLIC REPRIMAND — embarrassing, but it does not remove practice rights. Angela may continue to represent clients (possibly subject to conditions).",wrongs:["Suspension removes practice rights for its duration.",null,"Disbarment removes practice rights entirely.","Angela (censured) may still practice, so 'none' is wrong."]},
{n:100,topic:"IRS Written Determinations (TAM, PLR)",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"What is a Private Letter Ruling (PLR)?",opts:["A notice issued by the IRS to announce annual penalties for undisclosed tax shelter transactions.","A summary of U.S. Tax Court decisions published in the Internal Revenue Bulletin.","A public notice issued by the U.S. Treasury Department to inform taxpayers of changes in tax laws.","A written statement issued to a taxpayer that interprets and applies tax laws to the taxpayer's specific case."],a:3,why:"A PLR is a written determination issued to a specific taxpayer, at the taxpayer's request, applying the tax law to their specific proposed facts. It binds the IRS only as to that taxpayer.",wrongs:["Tax shelter penalty announcements are different guidance.","Court decision summaries aren't PLRs.","Public notices of law changes are announcements/notices, not PLRs.",null]}
]; // END_MOCK1

const MOCK2 = [
{n:1,topic:"Penalties Imposed on Tax Preparers",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Ariana is preparing her client's income tax return. During the appointment, her client, Sergio, complains about the very small amount of compensation he received for serving on jury duty for all of May. Ariana does not ask about the jury duty pay, or report any income from jury duty on Sergio's tax return. Who can be subject to a penalty for willful understatement?",opts:["Ariana cannot be subject to a penalty for willful understatement, but Sergio can.","Ariana and Sergio both can be subject to a penalty for willful understatement.","Sergio cannot be subject to a penalty for willful understatement, but Ariana can.","Neither will be subject to a penalty for willful understatement, because jury duty is not taxable."],a:1,why:"Jury duty pay is taxable income. Ariana heard about it and deliberately ignored it — willful disregard by the preparer (§6694(b)). Sergio signed a return he knew omitted income, so he faces taxpayer penalties too. Both can be penalized.",wrongs:["Ariana knew of the income and willfully omitted it — she's exposed under §6694(b).",null,"Sergio also signed a return omitting income he knew about — he's not off the hook.","Jury duty pay IS taxable — this premise is wrong."]},
{n:2,topic:"IRS Collections & Enforcement of Unpaid Taxes",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"The IRS can certify a taxpayer has \"seriously delinquent tax debt,\" which can lead to the denial and/or revocation of a taxpayer's passport, if the taxpayer owes more than how much in federal tax debt?",opts:["$64,000","$10,000","$25,000","$26,000"],a:0,why:"\"Seriously delinquent tax debt\" for passport certification (IRC §7345) is an inflation-adjusted threshold — $64,000 for 2025. Above it, the State Department can deny or revoke a passport.",wrongs:[null,"$10,000 relates to lien-filing and streamlined agreement thresholds, not passport certification.","$25,000 is a streamlined installment agreement figure, not this threshold.","$26,000 is not the certification amount."]},
{n:3,topic:"Other Duties and Prohibited Acts in Circular 230",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following statements regarding Circular 230 is false?",opts:["A Circular 230 requirement that a practitioner exercise due diligence in preparing, approving, and filing returns does not apply if the practitioner is merely assisting in preparing or filing returns.","A practitioner is subject to sanction under Circular 230 if he or she does not receive proper consent to represent conflicting interests before the IRS.","A criminal conviction for embezzlement is considered disreputable conduct under Circular 230.","Circular 230 never permits a practitioner to sign a return as a preparer if the return contains a frivolous position."],a:0,why:"FALSE — §10.22 due diligence applies to preparing, approving, AND filing returns, including when merely ASSISTING in preparation or filing. There is no 'assisting' exception.",wrongs:[null,"True — unwaived conflicts of interest are sanctionable under §10.29.","True — embezzlement is a crime of dishonesty/breach of trust under §10.51.","True — §10.34 flatly prohibits signing returns with frivolous positions."]},
{n:4,topic:"Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Some refund claims include extra due diligence requirements for tax preparers. Which of the following credits or tax situations do not have additional due diligence requirements?",opts:["Additional Child Tax Credit.","Head of Household filing status.","American Opportunity Tax Credit.","Premium Tax Credit."],a:3,why:"The Form 8867 due diligence requirements cover EITC, CTC/ACTC/ODC, AOTC, and Head of Household status. The Premium Tax Credit is NOT on that list.",wrongs:["ACTC is covered by preparer due diligence rules.","HOH status was added to the due diligence requirements.","AOTC is covered.",null]},
{n:5,topic:"Tax Practitioner Confidentiality Privilege",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"A Circular 230 practitioner must, when requested by the Office of Professional Responsibility, provide OPR with any information he or she may have regarding a violation of Circular 230 regulations by any person, except in which of the following circumstances?",opts:["If the practitioner has withdrawn from representation.","If the practitioner believes in good faith that the taxpayer is insolvent and unable to pay their tax debts.","If the client is no longer paying the practitioner for services.","If the practitioner believes in good faith that such information is privileged, or believes the request is of doubtful legality."],a:3,why:"§10.20 requires compliance with lawful OPR information requests UNLESS the practitioner believes in good faith and on reasonable grounds that the information is privileged or the request is of doubtful legality.",wrongs:["Withdrawal doesn't excuse the duty to provide information.","The client's financial condition is irrelevant to the duty.","Fee disputes don't excuse compliance.",null]},
{n:6,topic:"E-File Program",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"An electronically filed return is not considered \"filed\" until:",opts:["The electronic portion of the return has been rejected by the IRS.","The electronic portion of the return has been submitted to the transmitter.","The electronic portion of the return has been submitted to the IRS.","The electronic portion of the return has been acknowledged by the IRS."],a:3,why:"An e-filed return is 'filed' only when the IRS ACKNOWLEDGES acceptance of the electronic transmission. Submission alone isn't filing — a rejected return was never filed.",wrongs:["A rejected return is NOT filed.","Reaching the transmitter is just a step in the pipeline.","Submission without IRS acceptance/acknowledgment doesn't count.",null]},
{n:7,topic:"Seeking Relief from Joint Liability",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"All of the following are types of relief from \"joint and several liability\" except:",opts:["Innocent spouse relief.","Separation of liability relief.","Equivalent relief.","Equitable relief."],a:2,why:"The three types of relief from joint and several liability are: innocent spouse relief, separation of liability relief, and equitable relief. \"Equivalent relief\" is not one of them (an 'equivalent hearing' is a CDP concept).",wrongs:["A real type — relief for understatements the spouse didn't know about.","A real type — allocates the deficiency between spouses.",null,"A real type — the catch-all when the other two don't apply."]},
{n:8,topic:"Filing Status",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Married Filing Jointly means that the spouses _______________.",opts:["Combine their income and deductions on the same return.","Combine their income, but not their deductions, on the same return.","Report their respective income as a qualified joint venture.","Report their own incomes and deductions on separate returns."],a:0,why:"MFJ means one return combining BOTH spouses' income, deductions, and credits, with joint and several liability for the result.",wrongs:[null,"Deductions are combined too, not just income.","A qualified joint venture is a business election for spouse-owned businesses, not a filing status definition.","Separate returns describe Married Filing Separately."]},
{n:9,topic:"FBAR Requirements",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Roxanne is an enrolled agent. She has a new client, Michael, who informs her that he has a foreign bank account. Roxanne looks at the bank statements and determines that Michael has an FBAR reporting requirement. What is Roxanne required to do in this case?",opts:["Roxanne must advise the client of his filing obligations. She is not obligated to prepare the FBAR for the client unless she feels competent to do so and the client has agreed to this additional service.","Roxanne should advise her client, and disengage if he refuses to file the FBAR.","Roxanne is required to file the FBAR, whether or not the taxpayer pays a fee for the service.","Roxanne should prepare the FBAR return, or fire the client."],a:0,why:"The practitioner's duty is to ADVISE the client of the FBAR obligation and potential penalties. Actually preparing the FinCEN Form 114 is a separate service she may take on only if competent and engaged to do so.",wrongs:[null,"Disengagement isn't required if the client declines — advising is the duty.","She's never required to file it for free (or at all).","Neither preparing it nor firing the client is mandated."]},
{n:10,topic:"Estimated Tax Penalty",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Andrew's total tax for 2025 is $1,600. He had $400 in withholding on his Form W-2. Andrew owes $1,200 when he files his return. His prior year's tax was $2,000. Will Andrew be subject to an estimated tax penalty?",opts:["He will not be charged a penalty as long as he pays all the tax owed when he files his return.","No. He will not owe a penalty.","There is not enough information to make a determination.","Yes. He will owe a penalty."],a:3,why:"No penalty applies if the balance due after withholding is under $1,000, OR withholding covers 90% of current-year tax ($1,440) or 100% of prior-year tax ($2,000). Andrew owes $1,200 (≥$1,000) and his $400 withholding meets neither safe harbor — so he owes the penalty.",wrongs:["Paying at filing doesn't erase the underpayment penalty for the year.","He fails both safe harbors and the $1,000 threshold.","The facts given are sufficient to determine the penalty applies.",null]}
,
{n:11,topic:"Currently Not Collectible",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Terry is in debt $32,000 to the IRS, and he agrees with the amount he owes. Unfortunately, he is currently without a job and financially insolvent, making it impossible for him to settle his IRS debt. His accountant advises applying for Currently Not Collectible (CNC) status. What is the main advantage of Terry's account being placed in CNC status by the IRS?",opts:["The IRS will slowly forgive Terry's tax debt over time.","The IRS will stop assessing interest and penalties on his account.","The IRS will not attempt to collect from Terry, such as levying his assets and income while in CNC status.","The IRS will extend the time he has to pay his taxes."],a:2,why:"CNC status halts active collection — no levies on assets or income — while the taxpayer's hardship continues. The debt itself remains and the IRS reviews the account periodically.",wrongs:["CNC is not forgiveness; the debt stays on the books.","Interest and penalties CONTINUE to accrue during CNC.",null,"CNC doesn't extend the payment deadline — it just suspends collection activity."]},
{n:12,topic:"Collection Financial Standards",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is the purpose of Collection Financial Standards in determining a taxpayer's ability to pay a delinquent tax liability?",opts:["To impartially evaluate a taxpayer's ability to pay a delinquent tax liability.","To extend the time period for tax collection for an individual taxpayer.","To provide a fixed amount of tax relief for any taxpayer who requests it.","To gradually reduce the taxpayer's debt by gradual forgiveness of penalties and interest."],a:0,why:"Collection Financial Standards (national and local allowable living expense standards) give the IRS an objective, uniform way to measure what a taxpayer can actually pay when evaluating installment agreements, OICs, and hardship claims.",wrongs:[null,"They don't affect the collection statute.","They aren't a relief program — just a measuring tool.","They don't forgive penalties or interest."]},
{n:13,topic:"AFSP Administration",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which IRS office administers the Annual Filing Season Program for tax return preparers?",opts:["The Office of Professional Responsibility","The Return Preparer Office","The Taxpayer Advocate's Office","The IRS Office of Appeals"],a:1,why:"The Return Preparer Office (RPO) administers PTINs, enrollment, continuing education, and the AFSP. OPR handles discipline; RPO handles credentialing programs.",wrongs:["OPR handles practitioner conduct and sanctions, not the AFSP.",null,"TAS helps taxpayers with unresolved problems.","Appeals resolves tax disputes."]},
{n:14,topic:"Filing Status",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"A tax professional is required to determine a taxpayer's correct filing status. A taxpayer's filing status generally depends on whether the taxpayer is single or married. Whether a taxpayer is single or married is typically determined by ____________.",opts:["The taxpayer's marital status at the beginning of the tax year.","The taxpayer's marital status at the end of the tax year.","The taxpayer's marital status as determined by the courts.","The taxpayer's marital status in the middle of the year."],a:1,why:"Marital status for the whole year is determined as of December 31 — the last day of the tax year. Married on 12/31 means married all year for filing purposes.",wrongs:["January 1 status is irrelevant.",null,"Courts determine divorces, but the tax test is the year-end date.","Mid-year status doesn't control."]},
{n:15,topic:"Substantial Authority",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Marika is trying to find tax law information about a specific tax issue for a deduction she wants to take on her return. In order to avoid imposition of the accuracy-related penalty for a substantial understatement, which of the following IRS guidance is a type of authority Marika may rely on to show \"substantial authority\" for her position?",opts:["Official IRS Publications.","Revenue rulings.","Information on the IRS website.","IRS Form instructions."],a:1,why:"Revenue rulings are published official IRS positions and count as substantial authority. Publications, form instructions, and website content are helpful but carry no authoritative weight.",wrongs:["Publications are not substantial authority.",null,"Website content is not substantial authority.","Form instructions are not substantial authority."]},
{n:16,topic:"Currently Not Collectible",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following actions will the IRS take before placing a taxpayer's account in Currently Not Collectible (CNC) status?",opts:["Ask the taxpayer to provide an IRS collection financial statement.","Forgive a portion of the taxpayer's outstanding tax debt.","Temporarily suspend all interest and penalty charges.","Automatically extend the collection period beyond ten years."],a:0,why:"Before granting CNC, the IRS generally requires a Collection Information Statement (Form 433-F/433-A) documenting income, expenses, and assets to verify the hardship.",wrongs:[null,"CNC forgives nothing.","Interest and penalties keep accruing in CNC.","The 10-year collection statute isn't extended by CNC placement."]},
{n:17,topic:"Accuracy-Related Penalties",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Hunter took a large charitable deduction in a prior year, which was disallowed by the IRS when his return was audited. Hunter is assessed a penalty for substantial understatement of his income tax. An understatement is considered \"substantial\" if it is more than the larger of 10% of the correct tax or __________.",opts:["$10,000","$25,000","$1,000","$5,000"],a:3,why:"For individuals, an understatement is 'substantial' if it exceeds the GREATER of 10% of the correct tax or $5,000.",wrongs:["$10,000 relates to the corporate threshold ($10M cap concept differs).","$25,000 is not the individual threshold.","$1,000 is too low — that's the estimated tax penalty threshold.",null]},
{n:18,topic:"OPR Proceedings",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"The Office of Professional Responsibility has initiated a disciplinary proceeding against Travis, who is an enrolled agent. Travis contends that he has done nothing wrong. What is OPR's burden of proof in a disciplinary proceeding against a tax practitioner?",opts:["OPR must prove by \"clear and convincing evidence\" that Travis willfully promoted a tax shelter.","OPR must prove by \"clear and convincing evidence\" that Travis willfully violated one or more provisions of Circular 230.","OPR must prove that Travis negligently made inaccurate or unreasonable omissions on tax returns.","OPR must prove that Travis unintentionally violated one or more provisions of Circular 230."],a:1,why:"For serious sanctions (suspension/disbarment), OPR must prove willful violation of Circular 230 by clear and convincing evidence.",wrongs:["Tax shelter promotion isn't the required element — a Circular 230 violation is.",null,"Simple negligence isn't the standard for discipline.","Unintentional violations don't meet the willfulness requirement."]},
{n:19,topic:"Disreputable Conduct",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"The Office of Professional Responsibility specifies many instances in which a practitioner might be sanctioned for incompetence or disreputable conduct. All of the following are listed except:",opts:["Promoting abusive tax shelters.","Threatening an IRS officer.","Aiding and abetting understatement of a tax liability.","Hiring an employee who has been censured by the IRS."],a:3,why:"Censure doesn't remove practice rights, so employing a censured person is not disreputable conduct. (Knowingly employing a SUSPENDED or DISBARRED person would be a violation.)",wrongs:["Listed — abusive tax shelter promotion is sanctionable.","Listed — threats/intimidation of IRS employees are disreputable conduct.","Listed — aiding understatement is directly cited in §10.51.",null]},
{n:20,topic:"EA Renewal",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Enrolled agents who do not comply with the requirements for renewal of enrollment will be contacted by the Return Preparer Office. How much time does the EA have to respond to the RPO?",opts:["90 days from the date of the notice.","60 days from the date of the notice.","30 days from the date of the notice.","60 days from the date of receipt."],a:1,why:"The RPO sends a notice of noncompliance, and the EA has 60 days FROM THE DATE OF THE NOTICE to respond.",wrongs:["90 days is not the response window.",null,"30 days is too short.","The clock runs from the notice date, not the date of receipt."]}
,
{n:21,topic:"E-File Program Roles",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"With regards to the IRS e-file program, what is a \"Responsible Official?\"",opts:["An individual with the authority to sign tax returns.","An individual with primary authority over the Provider's IRS e-file operation at a location.","An individual with primary authority to collect taxpayer information and transmit completed tax returns.","An individual with the authority to prepare refund advance loans."],a:1,why:"Per Publication 3112, the Responsible Official is the person with primary authority and responsibility for the Provider's e-file operations at one or more locations, ensuring compliance with IRS e-file rules.",wrongs:["Signing returns is the preparer's role, not the definition.",null,"That describes transmission functions, not the Responsible Official role.","RALs have nothing to do with the definition."]},
{n:22,topic:"Frivolous Positions",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Maximus argued a position before the U.S. Tax Court, but he took a patently frivolous position, telling the court that he believed the income tax laws are invalid and do not apply to most citizens. What is the maximum penalty Maximus could face for this frivolous position?",opts:["$10,000","$5,000","$1,000","$25,000"],a:3,why:"The Tax Court may impose a penalty of up to $25,000 under IRC §6673 when a taxpayer maintains frivolous or groundless positions or institutes proceedings primarily for delay.",wrongs:["$10,000 is not the Tax Court maximum.","$5,000 is the frivolous RETURN penalty (§6702), not the court penalty.","$1,000 is far below the maximum.",null]},
{n:23,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"When a taxpayer submits an offer in compromise based on \"doubt as to liability,\" which of the following is not required?",opts:["Taxpayer attestation and signature.","Form 656-L.","A written statement explaining why the tax debt or a portion of the debt is incorrect.","Application fee."],a:3,why:"Doubt-as-to-liability offers (Form 656-L) require NO application fee and no financial statements — because the dispute is about whether the tax is owed, not ability to pay.",wrongs:["The form must be signed under penalties of perjury.","Form 656-L is the required form for this offer type.","An explanation of why the assessment is wrong is required.",null]},
{n:24,topic:"Enrolled Agent Licensing",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"An enrolled agent is a person who has earned the privilege of representing taxpayers before the IRS by either: (1) passing a three-part examination, or (2) ________________.",opts:["By going to an IRS-approved college.","Through experience as a licensed auditor in a CPA firm.","Through certain experience as a former IRS employee.","By earning an accounting degree."],a:2,why:"The second path to enrollment is qualifying technical experience as a former IRS employee (generally 5 years in a position applying the tax code).",wrongs:["No college route exists for enrollment.","CPA firm experience doesn't confer enrollment.",null,"Degrees don't substitute for the exam or IRS experience."]},
{n:25,topic:"Recordkeeping",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"The IRS is auditing Wendy's chiropractic business. Wendy's business is an LLC that files a Schedule C. Of the following items, which in itself would NOT be adequate documentation to support her expenses on Schedule C?",opts:["Copies of invoices and sales receipts provided to customers.","A calendar that shows the time, place, date, and purpose of business meetings.","Canceled checks.","Mileage logs that show where, when, why, and how far she drove for business purposes."],a:2,why:"A canceled check alone shows money left the account, but not WHAT was purchased or its business purpose. It must be paired with an invoice, receipt, or bill to substantiate the expense.",wrongs:["Invoices/receipts show the item and business nature — adequate.","A contemporaneous calendar documents business purpose — adequate for meetings.",null,"A proper mileage log is the prescribed substantiation for vehicle use."]},
{n:26,topic:"EINs",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"A business entity may apply for an Employer Identification Number (EIN) online if their principal business activity is located in:",opts:["The continental U.S. only.","The United States or in any U.S. Territory or U.S. possession.","The United States or in any foreign country.","The United States, excluding U.S. Territories."],a:1,why:"Online EIN applications are available to entities whose principal business is located in the U.S. or any U.S. territory/possession.",wrongs:["Alaska, Hawaii, and territories are included too.",null,"Foreign-based entities must apply by phone, fax, or mail — not online.","Territories ARE included."]},
{n:27,topic:"Preparer Designations",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following designations is no longer recognized by the IRS?",opts:["Enrolled Retirement Plan Agent","Registered Tax Return Preparer","Enrolled Actuary","Enrolled Agent"],a:1,why:"The RTRP program was invalidated by Loving v. IRS (2013) and no longer exists.",wrongs:["ERPAs remain recognized (though new enrollment ended, existing ones may practice).",null,"Enrolled actuaries remain a recognized category.","EAs are very much recognized."]},
{n:28,topic:"Nonresident Returns",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Ayame is a nonresident alien who is a citizen of Japan. She does not have to pay U.S. federal income tax, but she does own a small number of U.S. investments. During the year, she had a small amount of federal taxes withheld on income from a U.S. source investment. What can she do to receive a refund of her withheld taxes?",opts:["She must file a refund claim on Form 1040.","She must report the appropriate income and withholding amounts on Form 1040-NR.","She must ask the brokerage firm for a refund of incorrectly withheld amounts.","She must write a formal protest."],a:1,why:"Nonresident aliens claim refunds of over-withheld U.S. tax by filing Form 1040-NR reporting the income and withholding.",wrongs:["Form 1040 is for citizens and residents.",null,"The broker can't refund tax already remitted to the IRS.","A protest is not a refund mechanism."]},
{n:29,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Sabrina is an enrolled agent. She is representing Alexandra before the IRS for a tax matter. Alexandra's ex-husband, Paxton, also asked Sabrina to represent him for the same matter. Which of the following is NOT required for Sabrina to represent both?",opts:["The representation is not prohibited by law.","Sabrina must reasonably believe that she will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to both taxpayers.","Sabrina must notify the Office of Professional Responsibility that she will be representing both taxpayers.","Both taxpayers must waive the conflict of interest and give informed consent in writing to Sabrina."],a:2,why:"§10.29 requires: reasonable belief of competent representation, no legal prohibition, and written informed consent from each client. There is NO requirement to notify OPR — the consents are simply retained (36 months) and produced if OPR requests them.",wrongs:["Required element.","Required element.",null,"Required element — written waivers from both."]},
{n:30,topic:"Disciplinary Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which disciplinary action(s) by the IRS is not recorded in the Internal Revenue Bulletin?",opts:["Reprimand and censure.","Reprimand.","Reprimand, censure, and suspension.","Only disbarments are recorded in the Internal Revenue Bulletin."],a:1,why:"A reprimand is a PRIVATE letter of criticism — it is not published. Censure, suspension, and disbarment are public and announced in the Internal Revenue Bulletin.",wrongs:["Censure IS published — it's a public reprimand.",null,"Censure and suspension are both published.","Censures and suspensions are also published, not just disbarments."]}
,
{n:31,topic:"Tax Avoidance vs. Evasion",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Which of the following is illegal under the Internal Revenue Code?",opts:["Possessing offshore bank accounts.","Tax avoidance.","Possessing foreign trusts.","Tax evasion."],a:3,why:"Tax evasion — willfully concealing income or falsifying facts to escape tax — is a felony. Offshore accounts, foreign trusts, and tax planning are legal if properly reported.",wrongs:["Legal if disclosed (FBAR/Form 8938 as required).","Avoidance is the LEGAL minimization of tax.","Foreign trusts are legal with proper reporting.",null]},
{n:32,topic:"PTIN Requirements",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Hamad is employed by a tax preparation firm. He is directly supervised by an EA in the firm who signs the tax returns that Hamad has prepared. Hamad:",opts:["Is not required to obtain a PTIN because he is not yet a practitioner.","Is not required to obtain a PTIN because he does not sign the tax returns himself.","Is only required to obtain a PTIN if he prepares a \"substantial portion\" of a client's tax return.","Needs to obtain a PTIN."],a:3,why:"Anyone who prepares or substantially assists in preparing returns for compensation must have a PTIN — including supervised, non-signing preparers.",wrongs:["Practitioner status is irrelevant to the PTIN requirement.","Non-signing preparers still need PTINs.","Supervised preparers need a PTIN regardless.",null]},
{n:33,topic:"Fiduciaries",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"A fiduciary of a trust is treated by the IRS as __________________________.",opts:["The taxpayer themselves.","The beneficiary of the trust.","A limited partner.","The grantor of the trust."],a:0,why:"A fiduciary stands in the shoes of the entity — the IRS treats the fiduciary AS the taxpayer, not as a representative. That's why no Form 2848 is needed for a fiduciary to act.",wrongs:[null,"The beneficiary is a different party with different interests.","Partnership roles are unrelated.","The grantor created the trust; the fiduciary administers it."]},
{n:34,topic:"Refund Offsets",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Willow prepares her current year tax return and is owed a refund of $1,200. However, she owes delinquent federal taxes of $390 from an earlier tax year. Willow requests direct deposit of her refund. What will happen to her refund?",opts:["Her refund will be held until the overdue balance from the prior year is paid in full.","After her past due amount is offset, the balance will be sent as a paper check, since the refund amount cannot be legally altered when a taxpayer chooses direct deposit.","After her past due amount is offset, the balance will be deposited into her bank account.","Her refund will be held indefinitely pending official IRS review."],a:2,why:"The IRS offsets the $390 debt and direct deposits the remaining $810 as requested. Offsets don't cancel the direct deposit election.",wrongs:["The refund isn't held — the debt is simply netted out.","The remainder still goes by direct deposit, not paper check.",null,"No indefinite hold occurs for a simple offset."]},
{n:35,topic:"Notice of Deficiency",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"The more commonly used name of a statutory Notice of Deficiency is:",opts:["30-day letter.","A CP2000 notice.","A federal tax levy.","90-day letter."],a:3,why:"The statutory Notice of Deficiency is the \"90-day letter\" — the taxpayer has 90 days (150 if addressed abroad) to petition the Tax Court.",wrongs:["The 30-day letter is the earlier appeal-rights letter after examination.","A CP2000 is a proposed underreporter adjustment, not a statutory notice.","A levy is a seizure action, not a notice of deficiency.",null]},
{n:36,topic:"Direct Deposit Rules",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Kendra is an enrolled agent preparing a return for Richard, who has no bank account for direct deposit. Richard is distraught that a paper check will take longer, and asks Kendra if she can deposit his tax refund into HER bank account and turn the money over to him. What should Kendra do?",opts:["Kendra can suggest he borrow a bank account number from a friend because the taxpayer's name does not need to be on the bank account.","Kendra can offer to use her account to receive the direct deposit, and turn the money over to Richard once the refund is deposited.","Kendra can explain that a taxpayer's federal or state refund cannot be deposited into a tax preparer's bank account and he will have to open an account in his own name to have the refund direct deposited.","Kendra can suggest he borrow a bank account number from a family member because the taxpayer's name does not need to be on the bank account if the bank account belongs to a related person."],a:2,why:"Refunds may only be deposited into accounts in the taxpayer's own name (or spouse's on a joint return). A preparer receiving a client's refund into her own account is strictly prohibited.",wrongs:["Refunds can't go to accounts not in the taxpayer's name.","Prohibited — preparers may never receive client refunds into their accounts.",null,"Same problem — the account must be the taxpayer's."]},
{n:37,topic:"OPR",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which governmental organization administers and enforces the regulations governing practice before the IRS?",opts:["The Return Preparer Office.","Treasury Inspector General for Tax Administration.","The Office of Professional Responsibility.","The Taxpayer Advocate's Office."],a:2,why:"OPR administers and enforces Circular 230 — the regulations governing practice before the IRS — including discipline of practitioners.",wrongs:["RPO handles PTINs, enrollment, and CE — not enforcement of practice rules.","TIGTA investigates IRS misconduct and threats.",null,"TAS assists taxpayers with unresolved problems."]},
{n:38,topic:"Primary Authority",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following is considered a primary authority of U.S. tax law?",opts:["U.S. Tax Court cases.","A professional law review.","International court cases.","Supreme Court cases."],a:3,why:"Supreme Court decisions are the highest judicial primary authority — binding nationwide on all parties including the IRS.",wrongs:["Tax Court decisions carry weight but can be nonacquiesced by the IRS and overruled on appeal; the Supreme Court is the definitive answer here.","Law reviews are secondary authority — commentary, not law.","Foreign court decisions have no authority over U.S. tax law.",null]},
{n:39,topic:"Form 8821 vs 2848",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Russell is a taxpayer who filed an appeal of an IRS examination. He signed IRS Form 8821, Tax Information Authorization, for his tax preparer, Emily. Which of the following is correct?",opts:["Emily may represent Russell before IRS Appeals with a note attached by the client.","Emily may not represent the client before IRS Appeals.","Emily may represent Russell before IRS Appeals based on the Form 8821.","Emily may represent Russell before IRS Appeals with the oral consent of the client."],a:1,why:"Form 8821 only authorizes RECEIVING tax information — it grants no representation rights. Representation requires Form 2848 (and eligibility to practice).",wrongs:["A note can't create representation authority.",null,"8821 is information-only.","Oral consent doesn't confer representation rights."]},
{n:40,topic:"Use of Estimates",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Julian's business records and computer were completely destroyed by a house fire. He tries to recreate his records using estimates, but has no substantiation or receipts. Lilly is an enrolled agent who prepares Julian's return using estimates. What should Lilly do in this case?",opts:["Lilly may prepare the tax return using reasonable estimates.","Lilly should refer Julian to a tax attorney.","Lilly cannot prepare a tax return using only estimates.","Julian must request a Private Letter Ruling if he is using estimates."],a:0,why:"When records are destroyed through no fault of the taxpayer, reasonable estimates and reconstructed records are acceptable (the Cohan rule supports reasonable approximations).",wrongs:[null,"No legal issue requires an attorney here.","Estimates are permitted when reasonable and disclosed appropriately.","PLRs address uncertain legal questions, not recordkeeping."]}
,
{n:41,topic:"Installment Agreements",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Ahmed, an enrolled agent, prepared an individual income tax return for Camille. Camille has a balance due of $25,597. Camille is not able to pay the entire amount upon filing and would like to set up an installment agreement. Which of the following statements is correct with regards to this agreement?",opts:["Camille will not be charged interest and penalties while making installment payments.","Since Camille owes more than $25,000, she may not apply for an installment agreement.","Camille will not be charged a user fee to set up this installment agreement.","Camille must be in filing compliance."],a:3,why:"To obtain an installment agreement, the taxpayer must have filed all required returns (filing compliance). Interest, penalties, and user fees still apply.",wrongs:["Interest and late-payment penalties continue during the agreement.","Amounts over $25,000 are still eligible (streamlined up to $50,000).","Setup user fees apply (though they can be reduced/waived for low income).",null]},
{n:42,topic:"EA Practice Rights",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"An enrolled agent (EA) can represent a taxpayer:",opts:["Before IRS collections, examinations, and U.S. Tax Court.","Before any administrative level of the IRS.","Only if the EA prepared the return.","At all tax-related federal court proceedings."],a:1,why:"EAs have unlimited practice rights before ALL administrative levels of the IRS — exam, collections, appeals — for any taxpayer and any return.",wrongs:["Tax Court is a court, not the IRS — EAs need separate Tax Court admission.",null,"EA rights don't depend on who prepared the return.","Court representation requires bar admission — not covered by the EA credential."]},
{n:43,topic:"EA Practice Limits",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"An enrolled agent is normally not eligible to practice before:",opts:["An IRS revenue officer.","An IRS appeals officer.","A U.S. Tax Court judge.","An IRS revenue agent."],a:2,why:"Practice before the IRS excludes the courts. Appearing before a Tax Court judge requires admission to the Tax Court bar.",wrongs:["Revenue officers (collections) are IRS personnel — within EA rights.","Appeals officers are IRS — within EA rights.",null,"Revenue agents (examiners) are IRS — within EA rights."]},
{n:44,topic:"Continuing Education",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Rohan is an enrolled agent who takes a continuing education class on federal taxes. The class consists of two 90-minute segments at a conference. How many CE hours will be credited to Rohan for the course?",opts:["Three.","One.","Two.","Four."],a:0,why:"A CE hour is 50 minutes of instruction. 180 total minutes ÷ 50 = 3.6, and partial hours round DOWN after the first full hour — so Rohan is credited 3 hours.",wrongs:[null,"180 minutes is far more than one 50-minute hour.","Two hours would only account for 100 minutes.","Four hours would require 200 minutes."]},
{n:45,topic:"Refund Offsets",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Jayden filed his tax return on a timely basis. His refund was offset by other debts, so he received no refund for the year. He is not sure what type of debt was responsible, but he does owe delinquent student loans. What is the most likely scenario?",opts:["Jayden's refund was transferred to Direct Pay.","Jayden's refund was retained for future liability.","Jayden's IRS refund was offset to pay his overdue student loans.","Jayden's refund was sent to the wrong bank."],a:2,why:"The Treasury Offset Program intercepts federal refunds for delinquent federal student loans, child support, state taxes, and other government debts.",wrongs:["Direct Pay is a payment tool, not an offset destination.","Refunds aren't held for hypothetical future debts.",null,"A banking error is far less likely than a TOP offset given his known delinquent loans."]},
{n:46,topic:"Written Determinations",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following types of tax advice is specific advice provided by the IRS to a taxpayer for a fee?",opts:["Private letter rulings.","Chief Counsel advice.","Technical advice memoranda.","Revenue rulings."],a:0,why:"A PLR is issued to a specific taxpayer at their request — and the taxpayer pays a user fee for it.",wrongs:[null,"CCA is internal IRS legal advice, not taxpayer-requested.","TAMs arise during examinations at the IRS's initiative.","Revenue rulings are published guidance for everyone, no fee."]},
{n:47,topic:"Collection Financial Standards",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"The IRS uses Collection Financial Standards to help determine a taxpayer's ability to pay a delinquent tax liability. National Standards have been established for five necessary expenses. Which of the following is NOT considered a \"necessary expense\" for IRS purposes?",opts:["Personal care products.","Housekeeping supplies.","Food.","Entertainment."],a:3,why:"The five National Standard categories are food, housekeeping supplies, apparel and services, personal care products/services, and miscellaneous. Entertainment is not a necessary expense.",wrongs:["A National Standard category.","A National Standard category.","A National Standard category.",null]},
{n:48,topic:"Collection Statute",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"After an assessment, the Internal Revenue Service generally has the authority to collect outstanding federal taxes for how many years?",opts:["Five years.","Fifteen years.","Ten years.","Twenty years."],a:2,why:"The collection statute of limitations (CSED) is generally 10 years from the date of assessment.",wrongs:["Too short.","Not the statutory period.",null,"Too long — 10 years is the rule (subject to suspensions/extensions)."]},
{n:49,topic:"Preparer Penalties",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Hoshi is an enrolled agent who owns her own tax franchise. One of Hoshi's employees, Donald, prepares a return for a client. The client's return is later selected for examination, and one of the larger deductions is disallowed. Hoshi will not be subject to a preparer penalty if:",opts:["The preparer based the return on the information provided by the client.","She warned the preparer that the deduction was an aggressive position to take.","There was a reasonable basis for the position, and the position was adequately disclosed.","She does not sign the return and instead directs her employee to sign it."],a:2,why:"§6694 penalties don't apply to a disallowed position if it had a reasonable basis AND was adequately disclosed (e.g., Form 8275) — or substantial authority if undisclosed.",wrongs:["Client-provided information alone doesn't protect an unreasonable position.","Warning the employee doesn't cure an unreasonable position.",null,"Shifting the signature doesn't eliminate responsibility — and could itself be misconduct."]},
{n:50,topic:"Due Diligence Records",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"In preparing an Earned Income Credit Worksheet and Form 8867, how long should a tax preparer retain copies of both documents?",opts:["One year from the filing of the return.","Two years from the filing of the return.","Three years from the filing of the return.","Six years from the filing of the return."],a:2,why:"Due diligence records — Form 8867, worksheets, and records of how the information was obtained — must be kept for 3 years.",wrongs:["Too short.","Too short.",null,"Six years exceeds the requirement."]}
,
{n:51,topic:"CDP Hearings",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"A new client, Samir, visits Deana, an enrolled agent. Samir believes the U.S. tax system is purely voluntary and filed a return showing no income tax, requesting all withholding be refunded. The IRS assessed a $5,000 frivolous return penalty. Samir has received a Notice of Intent to Levy and Right to CDP Hearing concerning the penalty. Samir wants Deana to present his previous arguments about the tax system. Which of the following is a correct statement regarding the CDP hearing request raising arguments previously deemed frivolous?",opts:["If the appeal is deemed frivolous, Samir will be given 30 days to withdraw or amend the CDP appeal.","The EA would not be subject to a frivolous return penalty by submitting the CDP hearing request.","Since a $5,000 return penalty has been assessed, a second penalty cannot be assessed for the same tax period.","In all circumstances, filing the CDP request will suspend any levies while Appeals considers the request."],a:0,why:"When a CDP request contains frivolous positions, the IRS notifies the taxpayer and allows 30 days to withdraw or amend the frivolous portion to avoid an additional penalty.",wrongs:[null,"A practitioner submitting frivolous arguments can face penalties too.","Additional frivolous submission penalties CAN be assessed.","Frivolous CDP requests don't get the normal levy suspension."]},
{n:52,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for eligibility to apply for an Offer in Compromise (OIC)?",opts:["Being in an open bankruptcy proceeding.","Making all required estimated tax payments for the current tax year.","Filing all required tax returns.","Making all required tax deposits for the current and past two quarters, if an employer."],a:0,why:"An open bankruptcy DISQUALIFIES a taxpayer from an OIC — it's the opposite of a requirement. The others are eligibility conditions.",wrongs:[null,"Required — current-year estimated payments must be made.","Required — filing compliance is mandatory.","Required for employers — federal tax deposit compliance."]},
{n:53,topic:"Penalty Disputes",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Isabelle is an enrolled agent. Her client, Frederick, is requesting her assistance with a proposed accuracy-related penalty in an examination. All of the following are methods of addressing the penalty, EXCEPT:",opts:["After the penalty has been assessed, a written request for abatement can be submitted.","After the penalty has been assessed and paid, Isabelle can prepare a claim for refund.","Prior to a penalty being assessed, it may be appealed via deficiency procedures.","Prior to assessment, Isabelle can request binding arbitration to reconsider the penalty."],a:3,why:"Binding arbitration is not an available method for contesting accuracy-related penalties. Abatement requests, refund claims, and deficiency procedures are the recognized routes.",wrongs:["Valid — penalty abatement requests are standard.","Valid — pay, claim a refund, then sue if denied.","Valid — penalties in a deficiency notice go through Tax Court deficiency procedures.",null]},
{n:54,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Alaina, an enrolled agent, represented her client, Inigo, and his former business partner, Danny, before the IRS with regard to a partnership tax matter. Due to the potential conflict of interest, Alaina obtained written consent from each of her clients waiving the conflict. Alaina must keep those written consents for how long after the conclusion of representation?",opts:["72 Months","24 Months","48 Months","36 Months"],a:3,why:"Circular 230 §10.29 requires conflict waivers to be retained for at least 36 months after the conclusion of the representation.",wrongs:["Too long — 36 months is the rule.","Too short.","Not the required period.",null]},
{n:55,topic:"Unenrolled Preparers",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Everett is an unenrolled return preparer. He has a PTIN, but he does not have an AFSP certificate. Which of the following statements is correct?",opts:["Everett may receive refund checks on behalf of the taxpayer if Form 8821 has been executed.","Everett may prepare and sign a taxpayer's return. However, he may not represent taxpayers before the Internal Revenue Service.","Everett is permitted to represent a taxpayer over the telephone with the Automated Collection System unit, as long as he is listed as a third-party designee on the return.","Everett may prepare tax returns, but he is only permitted to appear as a taxpayers' representative before a Customer Service Representative of the Internal Revenue Service."],a:1,why:"With just a PTIN and no AFSP certificate, Everett can prepare and sign returns but has NO representation rights whatsoever.",wrongs:["Form 8821 is information-only and doesn't authorize receiving refund checks.",null,"Third-party designee status doesn't confer representation before ACS.","Non-AFSP unenrolled preparers can't represent before anyone."]},
{n:56,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Casimir, an enrolled agent, is representing a married couple, Nathan and Henrietta, in an ongoing IRS examination. One afternoon, Henrietta shows up early to a meeting. Off the record, Henrietta confides that the examination is causing marital strife, and that she suspects Nathan may have taken erroneous business deductions on his Schedule C. All of the following activities would address the conflict of interest EXCEPT (choose the best answer):",opts:["Politely advising Henrietta that this meeting was not appropriate, and make sure that no further meetings occur unless both spouses are present.","If Henrietta does not consent to Casimir sharing her concerns with her husband, Casimir cannot obtain informed consent from the husband to continue representing both spouses without violating her confidences. Casimir should withdraw from representation of either spouse in order to avoid a lawsuit.","Advising both spouses that there could be a conflict of interest going forward in representing both of them, and they may wish to retain their own counsel in the matter.","Informing both spouses of the potential ability to seek an innocent spouse determination as part of this examination as it moves forward."],a:0,why:"Refusing to hear one spouse alone doesn't ADDRESS the conflict that now exists — Casimir already has the confidential information. Ignoring it and simply banning solo meetings leaves the conflict unmanaged.",wrongs:[null,"Withdrawal properly resolves an unwaivable conflict.","Advising of the conflict and suggesting separate counsel addresses it.","Informing both of innocent spouse options addresses the diverging interests."]},
{n:57,topic:"Accuracy-Related Penalties",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"The U.S. Tax Court has generally held that taxpayers who rely on software to justify errors on self-prepared returns are:",opts:["Not liable for the Sec. 6662 accuracy-related penalty.","Liable for 20% of the Sec. 6662 accuracy-related penalty.","Liable for 40% of the Sec. 6662 accuracy-related penalty.","Liable for the Sec. 6662 accuracy-related penalty."],a:3,why:"\"The software made me do it\" is not reasonable cause — courts hold that software output depends on user input, so taxpayers remain liable for the §6662 penalty.",wrongs:["Software reliance doesn't excuse the penalty.","The penalty IS 20% of the underpayment, but the answer describing liability for the penalty itself is the correct statement.","40% applies to gross valuation misstatements, not software reliance.",null]},
{n:58,topic:"Payment Methods",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Only taxpayers who file electronically can pay an amount due by:",opts:["Money order.","Credit card.","Electronic funds withdrawal, with the submission of the return itself.","Check."],a:2,why:"Electronic funds withdrawal (direct debit submitted WITH the return) is only available when e-filing — the payment rides along with the electronic submission.",wrongs:["Money orders work for paper filers too.","Credit card payments are available to everyone.",null,"Checks are available to everyone."]},
{n:59,topic:"Amended Returns",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Francine plans to amend a prior-year return due to a loss on worthless securities. How long does she have to amend her tax return with regards to worthless securities?",opts:["3 years","7 years","5 years","10 years"],a:1,why:"The special statute for claiming a refund from worthless securities (or bad debts) is 7 years from the due date of the return for the year the security became worthless.",wrongs:["3 years is the general refund claim period — worthless securities get longer.",null,"5 years is not the special period.","10 years applies to foreign tax credit claims."]},
{n:60,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Jane Smith is an enrolled agent who is representing a new client. Jane fills out a Form 2848 and asks the client to sign. Jane must also sign. Which of the following is NOT included in the \"declaration of representative\" that must be signed under penalties of perjury?",opts:["Jane is aware that she is subject to the Circular 230 regulations.","Jane is authorized to represent the taxpayer identified in the power of attorney for the matters specified therein.","Jane is not currently under suspension or disbarred from practice before the Internal Revenue Service.","That the taxpayer or Jane did not sign the power of attorney under duress."],a:3,why:"The declaration covers: awareness of Circular 230, not being suspended/disbarred, and authorization to represent for the specified matters. There is no 'duress' statement.",wrongs:["Included in the declaration.","Included in the declaration.","Included in the declaration.",null]}
,
{n:61,topic:"FBAR Penalties",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"When a taxpayer is required to file an FBAR and does not file the report, the person is potentially subject to:",opts:["Civil and criminal penalties for non-filing.","Censure for non-filing.","Criminal penalties only for non-filing.","Civil penalties only for non-filing."],a:0,why:"FBAR non-filing can trigger BOTH civil penalties (higher for willful violations) and criminal prosecution.",wrongs:[null,"Censure is a practitioner sanction, not a taxpayer penalty.","Civil penalties also apply.","Criminal penalties also apply for willful violations."]},
{n:62,topic:"Direct Deposit Rules",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Geena is expecting a tax refund of $5,400 this year. She chooses to direct deposit her refund. With regard to direct deposit, which of the following options is NOT permitted?",opts:["She may split the refund between three savings accounts.","She may split the refund between two savings accounts.","She can have her refund deposited onto a prepaid debit card.","She can have her refund directly deposited to a foreign bank account."],a:3,why:"IRS refunds can only be direct deposited to U.S.-based accounts (or U.S.-affiliated accounts) — foreign bank accounts are not permitted.",wrongs:["Up to three accounts is allowed via Form 8888.","Two accounts is fine.","Prepaid debit cards that accept deposits are allowed.",null]},
{n:63,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"The \"declaration of representative\" accompanying a power of attorney must be signed under penalties of perjury with the practitioner declaring that:",opts:["The practitioner is authorized to represent the taxpayer identified in the power of attorney for the matters specified therein.","The taxpayer and the preparer are both aware of Circular 230 regulations.","The practitioner has never been under suspension or disbarment from practice before the Internal Revenue Service.","The taxpayer did not sign the power of attorney under duress."],a:0,why:"The representative declares authorization to represent the taxpayer for the matters specified. (Also: current non-suspension and Circular 230 awareness — but only as to the PRACTITIONER, and only CURRENT status.)",wrongs:[null,"Only the practitioner declares Circular 230 awareness — not the taxpayer.","The declaration is about not being CURRENTLY suspended — not 'never.'","No duress declaration exists on the form."]},
{n:64,topic:"Child Tax Credit",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"In which of the following scenarios could a taxpayer potentially qualify for the Child Tax Credit?",opts:["Parent has an SSN, Child has an SSN.","Parent has an ITIN, Child has an ITIN.","Parent has an SSN, Child has an ITIN.","All of the above."],a:0,why:"The CTC requires the CHILD to have an SSN valid for employment. A parent-SSN/child-SSN combination clearly qualifies.",wrongs:[null,"A child with only an ITIN can't generate the CTC (only the $500 ODC).","Child ITIN means no CTC — only the Credit for Other Dependents.","B and C fail the child-SSN requirement."]},
{n:65,topic:"Return of Client Records",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Marion is an enrolled agent. Her client Leonardo has several unpaid invoices. Marion prepared Leonardo's tax return and called him to pick it up. Leonardo comes to Marion's office, demands the return of his records, and refuses to pay any outstanding invoices. What is Marion required to do?",opts:["Marion must promptly return any and all records, including the tax return that she prepared, regardless of any fee dispute.","Marion is required to return the client's records only when the client pays in full.","Marion is not required to return the client's records while there is an ongoing fee dispute.","Marion must promptly return any and all records belonging to the client that are necessary for him to comply with his federal tax obligations. She is not required to give Leonardo the tax return that she prepared."],a:3,why:"§10.28: the practitioner must promptly return the CLIENT'S OWN records needed for tax compliance, even amid a fee dispute. Her own work product (the return she prepared but wasn't paid for) may be withheld unless state law provides otherwise.",wrongs:["Her unpaid work product isn't a 'client record' she must surrender.","Client records must be returned regardless of payment.","Fee disputes don't excuse returning the client's own records.",null]},
{n:66,topic:"EFTPS",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is EFTPS?",opts:["EFTPS is a federal tax payment system that allows individuals and businesses to pay taxes online.","A secure IRS system for contacting taxpayers via email.","The official name of the IRS's e-file system.","EFTPS is a banking application for IRS payments."],a:0,why:"The Electronic Federal Tax Payment System is a free Treasury service for individuals and businesses to pay any federal tax online or by phone.",wrongs:[null,"The IRS doesn't initiate contact by email.","E-file is separate — EFTPS is for payments, not filings.","It's a government service, not a bank app."]},
{n:67,topic:"Head of Household",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"In determining whether or not a person qualifies for Head of Household filing status, which of the following is NOT one of the tests?",opts:["Qualifying person.","Income.","Marital status.","Cost of keeping up a home."],a:1,why:"HOH requires: being unmarried (or considered unmarried), paying more than half the cost of keeping up a home, and having a qualifying person. There's no income test.",wrongs:["Required — a qualifying person must live with the taxpayer (with exceptions for parents).",null,"Required — the taxpayer must be unmarried or considered unmarried.","Required — more than half the cost of the home."]},
{n:68,topic:"IRS Appeals",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Which of the following would be an acceptable basis for an IRS appeal?",opts:["An appeal based on religious grounds.","An appeal based on a recent court case.","An appeal based on moral grounds.","An appeal based on political grounds."],a:1,why:"Appeals considers disputes grounded in tax LAW — statutes, regulations, rulings, and court decisions. A recent court case is a legal argument.",wrongs:["Religious objections aren't legal grounds.",null,"Moral objections aren't legal grounds.","Political objections aren't legal grounds."]},
{n:69,topic:"Circular 230 Violations",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following constitutes a violation by a tax preparer of Circular 230?",opts:["Charging a contingent fee in connection with a refund claim filed for penalties or interest.","Advertising to existing clients.","Filing a tax return with a mathematical error.","Giving written tax advice based on audit probability."],a:3,why:"§10.37 prohibits written advice that takes into account the possibility that a return won't be audited or that an issue won't be raised on audit.",wrongs:["Contingent fees ARE permitted for claims solely involving penalties or interest.","Advertising is permitted if not misleading.","Honest math errors aren't Circular 230 violations.",null]},
{n:70,topic:"OPR Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Lazaro is an enrolled agent who was referred to the Office of Professional Responsibility for preparer misconduct. What type of sanction will NOT be imposed against Lazaro by the OPR?",opts:["Suspension.","Incarceration.","Censure.","Disbarment."],a:1,why:"OPR's sanctions are administrative: reprimand, censure, suspension, disbarment, and monetary penalties. Imprisonment requires criminal prosecution by the Department of Justice — outside OPR's power.",wrongs:["An available OPR sanction.",null,"An available OPR sanction.","An available OPR sanction."]}
,
{n:71,topic:"Installment Agreements",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"For the IRS to grant a guaranteed installment agreement, a taxpayer must have not failed to file any income tax returns or pay any tax shown on such returns during any of the preceding:",opts:["10 taxable years","5 taxable years","6 taxable years","3 taxable years"],a:1,why:"Guaranteed installment agreements ($10,000 or less) require a clean compliance history for the preceding 5 taxable years — no failures to file, pay, or prior installment agreements.",wrongs:["Not the statutory lookback.",null,"Not the statutory lookback.","The lookback is 5 years, not 3."]},
{n:72,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"When does a durable power of attorney expire?",opts:["When a taxpayer either marries or divorces.","When a taxpayer dies.","When a taxpayer is deemed incompetent.","When a taxpayer becomes mentally incapacitated."],a:1,why:"The defining feature of a DURABLE POA is that it survives the taxpayer's incapacity or incompetence — it terminates only at death.",wrongs:["Marital changes don't automatically end a durable POA.",null,"Surviving incompetence is exactly what makes it 'durable.'","It remains valid through incapacity — that's the point of a durable POA."]},
{n:73,topic:"Preparer Signature",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"A tax preparer is required to sign the preparer's section on each tax return they prepare for compensation. The preparer's declaration on signing states that the information contained in the return is true, correct, and complete based on all information he has. This preparer statement is signed ____________.",opts:["Without prejudice.","Under duress.","Under penalties of perjury.","Without requirements."],a:2,why:"The preparer's declaration, like the taxpayer's, is signed under penalties of perjury.",wrongs:["'Without prejudice' is a legal reservation, not a signing standard.","Duress would invalidate a signature.",null,"The declaration carries real legal weight."]},
{n:74,topic:"E-File Sanctions",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Which of the following infractions could cause an e-file provider to be permanently expelled from the IRS e-file program?",opts:["Level Three.","Level Two.","Level One.","All of the above."],a:0,why:"Level Three infractions are the most severe (fraud, criminal conduct, significant harm to e-file integrity) and can result in permanent expulsion.",wrongs:[null,"Level Two typically brings suspension (1–2 years), not permanent expulsion.","Level One is the least severe — usually a written reprimand.","Only Level Three carries permanent expulsion."]},
{n:75,topic:"Audit Reconsideration",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Amos received a notice from the IRS saying a prior year's tax return had been examined, creating a tax assessment of $2,560. Amos disagrees with the amount of tax assessed. Amos could request audit reconsideration in all of the following situations EXCEPT when:",opts:["There is new documentation for the examination.","Amos moved and never received the examination notice.","The full amount owed has already been paid.","Amos neither appeared for the examination nor sent information to the IRS."],a:2,why:"Audit reconsideration is only available for UNPAID assessments. Once fully paid, the remedy is a formal claim for refund (Form 1040-X/843), not reconsideration.",wrongs:["Valid grounds — new information supports reconsideration.","Valid grounds — never receiving the notice.",null,"Valid grounds — nonparticipation in the original exam."]},
{n:76,topic:"Identity Theft",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Brenda had her wallet stolen in January, and now she is worried about identity theft and refund fraud. What should Brenda consider doing to minimize the risk of refund fraud?",opts:["Brenda should request an IP PIN and file her tax return as early as possible in the tax season.","Brenda should file an extension and wait to file her return until the extended due date.","Brenda should request an IP PIN and file her tax return as late as possible in the tax season.","Brenda should file a police report and file her tax return on paper."],a:0,why:"An IP PIN blocks fraudulent e-filings under her SSN, and filing EARLY beats any fraudster to the punch.",wrongs:[null,"Waiting gives thieves more time to file first.","Filing late increases the window for fraud.","Paper filing doesn't prevent a thief from e-filing first."]},
{n:77,topic:"EA Renewal",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"How often must enrolled agents renew their enrollment?",opts:["Every three years","Every year","Every five years","Every two years"],a:0,why:"EAs renew enrollment every 3 years (based on the last digit of their SSN), completing 72 hours of CE per cycle.",wrongs:[null,"Annual renewal applies to the PTIN, not enrollment.","Five years is not the cycle.","Two years is not the cycle."]},
{n:78,topic:"Substantiation",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"In which of the following instances does the IRS NOT require additional documentary evidence to support a taxpayer's travel, gift, or transportation expenses when the taxpayer is traveling away from home on business?",opts:["An expense of $150 for lodging while traveling on business.","Auto expenses for travel from multiple work locations.","A business meal expense of $250.","Per diem allowances for meals and lodging reported to an employer under an accountable plan."],a:3,why:"Under an accountable plan using per diem allowances, the deemed-substantiation rules apply — no receipts needed for the per diem amounts.",wrongs:["Lodging requires documentary evidence regardless of amount.","Vehicle expenses require mileage logs/records.","Expenses of $75+ (and all lodging) require receipts.",null]},
{n:79,topic:"Advertising",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which IRS insignia may an enrolled agent use in their advertising?",opts:["The official IRS e-file logo.","The IRS eagle insignia.","The IRS Financial Management Service insignia.","The U.S. Treasury seal."],a:0,why:"Authorized e-file providers may display the IRS e-file logo. Official government seals and insignia may never be used — they'd falsely imply IRS endorsement.",wrongs:[null,"Prohibited — implies IRS affiliation.","Prohibited government insignia.","Prohibited — the Treasury seal is off-limits."]},
{n:80,topic:"Criminal Statutes",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"The statute of limitations for the criminal offense of willfully attempting to evade or defeat any tax is:",opts:["Fifteen years.","Three years.","Six years.","Ten years."],a:2,why:"Most criminal tax offenses, including willful evasion under IRC §7201, carry a 6-year statute of limitations for prosecution.",wrongs:["Too long.","Three years is the general civil assessment statute.",null,"Ten years is the collection statute, not the criminal one."]}
,
{n:81,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"What is considered a conflict of interest in IRS representation?",opts:["Representing a dissolving partnership where the enrolled agent has obtained informed consent from all affected partners and the partnership itself.","Representing a client where the enrolled agent and the client have differing political views.","Representing a married couple in an examination of a joint return. The couple is still legally married.","Representing a client whose case is materially limited by the enrolled agent's responsibilities to another client."],a:3,why:"§10.29 defines a conflict as representation directly adverse to another client, OR representation materially limited by responsibilities to another client, former client, third person, or the practitioner's own interests.",wrongs:["A properly waived conflict is permissible — not a live conflict problem.","Political differences aren't conflicts of interest.","Representing spouses on a joint return isn't inherently a conflict.",null]},
{n:82,topic:"IP PIN",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"What is the function of an IP PIN?",opts:["The IP PIN acts as a fraud prevention tool to validate the dependents listed on the tax return.","The IP PIN acts as an authentication number to validate the correct owner of the Social Security number(s) listed on the tax return.","The IP PIN acts as an authentication number to validate the taxpayer's eligibility for refundable credits.","The IP PIN acts as a substitute for an electronic signature on an e-filed return."],a:1,why:"The Identity Protection PIN is a six-digit number that authenticates the true owner of the SSN, preventing fraudsters from filing under that number.",wrongs:["It authenticates the SSN owner, not dependents' status.",null,"It has nothing to do with credit eligibility.","It's not a signature — signature PINs are separate."]},
{n:83,topic:"Form 8879",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"What does Form 8879 authorize?",opts:["An extension of time to e-file a taxpayer's return.","Transcript request.","An ERO to electronically file a tax return on behalf of the taxpayer.","Third Party Authorization."],a:2,why:"Form 8879, IRS e-file Signature Authorization, authorizes the ERO to enter or generate the taxpayer's PIN and e-file the return on their behalf.",wrongs:["Extensions use Form 4868.","Transcripts use Form 4506-T.",null,"Third-party designations are made on the return itself or Form 8821."]},
{n:84,topic:"EA Enrollment",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Weston recently passed all three parts of the EA exam. He wants to submit his application to become an enrolled agent. With regards to his enrollment, what types of personal tax issues could negatively impact Weston's application?",opts:["Lack of an Employer Identification Number.","An overdue tax return that has not been filed.","Lack of a Social Security number.","Weston does not have an EFIN."],a:1,why:"The enrollment suitability check reviews personal tax compliance — unfiled returns or unpaid taxes can cause denial of enrollment.",wrongs:["An EIN isn't required for enrollment.",null,"Applicants without SSNs have alternative procedures; it's not a compliance failure.","An EFIN relates to e-filing, not enrollment."]},
{n:85,topic:"E-File Mandates",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"The Internal Revenue Service requires businesses to e-file their information returns under certain conditions. Which of the following statements accurately reflects these regulations?",opts:["Businesses are required to e-file regardless of the number of information returns they file.","Businesses are required to e-file if they file 10 or more information returns.","Businesses are required to e-file if they file 5 or more information returns.","Businesses are not required to e-file any of their information returns."],a:1,why:"Under regulations effective for returns filed in 2024 and later, filers of 10 or more information returns (aggregated across types) must e-file.",wrongs:["Filers under the 10-return threshold may still paper file.",null,"The threshold is 10, not 5.","A mandate clearly exists."]},
{n:86,topic:"Innocent Spouse Relief",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Carlisle is an enrolled agent. His new client Monique received an assessment from the IRS regarding a joint return she filed in a prior year with her ex-husband, Darby. Carlisle learns that Darby was a gambling addict, and the IRS transcripts show several items of income left off the original return — all related to Darby's gambling. Monique had no idea the return had underreported income. Does Monique have any recourse?",opts:["Monique and her ex-husband are both jointly and severally liable for the tax on the previously filed return, and she has no recourse in this case.","Monique may qualify for relief as an injured spouse.","Monique may qualify for relief as an innocent spouse.","Monique may request a conference with the IRS Appeals Office."],a:2,why:"An UNDERSTATEMENT of tax caused by one spouse's unreported income, unknown to the other spouse, is the classic innocent spouse scenario (Form 8857).",wrongs:["Joint liability exists, but innocent spouse relief is exactly the recourse for this.","Injured spouse applies when YOUR share of a refund is offset for the other spouse's debts — not understated income.",null,"Appeals doesn't address the liability allocation issue; innocent spouse relief does."]},
{n:87,topic:"E-File Attachments",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"How does a taxpayer submit additional documentation to the IRS on an e-filed tax return?",opts:["The taxpayer should use Form 8453 and mail in supporting documentation.","The return must be paper-filed if additional documentation must be sent with the return.","A Form 1040-X must be filed with the paper attachments.","The taxpayer should use Form 2848 and mail in supporting documentation."],a:0,why:"Form 8453 is the transmittal used to mail required paper documents to the IRS after e-filing.",wrongs:[null,"Paper filing isn't required — Form 8453 solves this.","1040-X is for amending, not attaching documents.","2848 is a power of attorney."]},
{n:88,topic:"US Tax Court",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"A case in the U.S. Tax Court is commenced by _________.",opts:["An IRS impasse.","A formal protest.","A taxpayer's appeal.","The filing of a petition."],a:3,why:"A Tax Court case begins when the taxpayer files a PETITION, generally within 90 days of the notice of deficiency.",wrongs:["An impasse isn't a legal filing.","A protest goes to IRS Appeals, not the court.","'Appeal' is the administrative process; the court requires a petition.",null]},
{n:89,topic:"Partnership Audits",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Heartland Realty, LLP is a domestic partnership. Which of the following would prevent Heartland Realty from electing out of the \"Centralized Partnership Audit Regime?\"",opts:["Heartland Realty has a partner that is a single-member LLC.","Heartland Realty has a partner that is a C corporation.","Heartland Realty has a partner that is an S corporation.","Heartland Realty has 100 partners."],a:0,why:"Electing out requires 100 or fewer partners, ALL of whom are eligible partners (individuals, C corps, S corps, estates of deceased partners, foreign entities taxed as corporations). A disregarded entity like a single-member LLC is NOT an eligible partner.",wrongs:[null,"C corporations are eligible partners.","S corporations are eligible partners (with shareholder counting rules).","Exactly 100 partners still satisfies the '100 or fewer' rule."]},
{n:90,topic:"IRS Collections",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is the legal procedure by which the IRS can seize a taxpayer's property in order to satisfy a tax debt?",opts:["A deficiency seizure.","A levy.","A lien.","A jeopardy assessment."],a:1,why:"A LEVY is the actual legal seizure of property (wages, bank accounts, assets) to satisfy tax debt.",wrongs:["Not a real term.",null,"A lien is a CLAIM against property — it secures the debt but doesn't seize anything.","A jeopardy assessment accelerates assessment; it isn't the seizure mechanism itself."]}
,
{n:91,topic:"EFIN Application",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"The IRS conducts a suitability check on all EFIN applicants. Suitability checks may include which of the following?",opts:["A criminal background check.","A residency check.","A DMV driving record check.","A drug check."],a:0,why:"E-file suitability checks include criminal background checks, tax compliance verification, prior e-file non-compliance history, and citizenship status.",wrongs:[null,"Residency verification isn't the named component — the FBI criminal check is.","Driving records aren't reviewed.","Drug testing isn't part of the process."]},
{n:92,topic:"Refund Offsets",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Faustino filed his federal tax return on time, and was expecting a $4,000 refund. However, his entire refund was offset to pay off his past-due debts. Which of the following would NOT be a reason for the IRS to offset Faustino's refund?",opts:["Faustino has unpaid local utility bills.","Faustino has delinquent student loans.","Faustino has past-due state income tax obligations.","Faustino has past-due federal taxes."],a:0,why:"The Treasury Offset Program covers federal taxes, state income taxes, federal student loans, child support, and certain other GOVERNMENT debts. Private/local utility bills are not offsetable.",wrongs:[null,"Federal student loans are offsetable.","State income tax debts are offsetable.","Federal tax debts are offset first."]},
{n:93,topic:"E-File Mandate",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Daniella is a tax professional who prepares over 300 tax returns every year, so she is subject to the e-file mandate. She has a new client, Fernando, who refuses to e-file his tax return for religious reasons. What is the proper action for Daniella to take?",opts:["If her client refuses to e-file, Daniella must decline the engagement.","She must refer Fernando to a different tax return preparer.","She can prepare Fernando's return on paper and attach Form 8948.","She should encourage Fernando to file electronically. If he refuses, Daniella does not need to sign the return as a paid preparer."],a:2,why:"Form 8948, Preparer Explanation for Not Filing Electronically, documents the taxpayer's choice to paper file — the preparer attaches it and files on paper.",wrongs:["No need to decline — the exception exists.","No referral needed.",null,"She must still sign as paid preparer."]},
{n:94,topic:"Representation Rights",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"The following tax professionals have unlimited representation rights before the Internal Revenue Service EXCEPT:",opts:["Enrolled agents.","Attorneys.","AFSP certificate holders.","Certified Public Accountants."],a:2,why:"AFSP holders have only LIMITED representation rights — returns they prepared and signed, before limited IRS personnel. EAs, attorneys, and CPAs have unlimited rights.",wrongs:["Unlimited rights.","Unlimited rights.",null,"Unlimited rights."]},
{n:95,topic:"IRS Levies",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following is NOT exempt from IRS levy?",opts:["Child support.","Undelivered mail.","Unemployment benefits.","Wages."],a:3,why:"Wages are a prime levy target — a continuous wage levy attaches until released (only a modest exempt amount is protected). Child support received, undelivered mail, and unemployment benefits are statutorily exempt.",wrongs:["Exempt from levy.","Exempt from levy.","Exempt from levy.",null]},
{n:96,topic:"Substitute for Return",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Arturo was required to file a tax return but refused to do so. The IRS sends him multiple notices, which he ignores. The IRS finally prepares and executes a substitute for return (SFR) assessment against Arturo. What happens when the IRS files a \"substitute return\" for Arturo?",opts:["A substitute return will give Arturo credit for all exemptions, credits, and deductions he is entitled to receive. Arturo may still file his own tax return, and the IRS will generally adjust the account.","A substitute return may not give Arturo credit for all exemptions, credits, and deductions he is entitled to receive. Arturo may still file his own tax return to take advantage of any exemptions, credits, and deductions he is entitled to receive. The IRS will generally adjust the account to reflect the correct figures.","A substitute return will give Arturo credit for all exemptions, credits, and deductions he is entitled to receive. Arturo may not file his own tax return if a substitute return was filed.","A substitute return may not give Arturo credit for all exemptions, credits, and deductions he is entitled to receive. Arturo may not file his own tax return if a substitute return has been filed."],a:1,why:"An SFR is prepared in the government's favor — typically single/MFS status, standard deduction only, no credits. The taxpayer can still file his own return, and the IRS generally adjusts the account accordingly.",wrongs:["SFRs do NOT include all favorable items.",null,"SFRs are unfavorable, but he CAN still file his own return.","He can still file his own return after an SFR."]},
{n:97,topic:"Combat Zone Rules",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Katie and Easton are married and file jointly. Easton is in the U.S. Marines, currently serving in a combat zone. Katie has not been able to contact her husband for several months. When one spouse is serving in a combat zone, what is required in order to file a joint return?",opts:["Both spouses must sign, so Katie must appeal to the IRS for a special exemption.","Katie may sign the joint return on her husband's behalf, but only if she secured a power of attorney in advance.","Katie may sign on behalf of her husband, whether or not she has a signed power of attorney.","Katie may delay filing the tax return until Michael has returned to the United States and can sign the return."],a:2,why:"When a spouse is serving in a combat zone, the other spouse may sign the joint return on their behalf WITHOUT a power of attorney — attaching a statement explaining the situation.",wrongs:["No special exemption request is needed.","A POA is NOT required in the combat zone situation.",null,"No need to delay — she can sign for him."]},
{n:98,topic:"Form 2848 Submission",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Estelle is an enrolled agent who obtained a signed Form 2848 from her client and would like to have it processed as soon as possible. How can she submit this form to the IRS?",opts:["Estelle can email the form to the IRS.","Estelle can only submit the Form 2848 by mail.","Estelle can fax, mail, or upload the form on the IRS website.","In order to prevent fraud, Estelle cannot submit the form herself. Her client must submit the form to the IRS."],a:2,why:"Forms 2848 can be submitted by fax, mail, or online upload (Tax Pro Account / Submit Forms 2848 online).",wrongs:["The IRS doesn't accept emailed POAs.","Mail is not the only channel.",null,"The representative routinely submits the form."]},
{n:99,topic:"Taxpayer Advocate",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Marcel's tax return was chosen for audit. Marcel tried to contact the revenue agent several times over a number of days, but the agent won't return any calls. Marcel then attempts multiple times to talk with the agent's supervisor, also with no response. Marcel believes the IRS is not handling his case in a timely and appropriate manner. Who should be contacted for assistance?",opts:["IRS Appeals office.","IRS Help Line.","Taxpayer Advocate Service.","U.S. Tax Court."],a:2,why:"TAS intervenes when normal IRS channels fail — repeated unreturned contacts and stalled cases are its core mission.",wrongs:["Appeals handles disputed exam RESULTS, not unresponsiveness.","The help line can't fix a stalled examination.",null,"No notice of deficiency exists yet — court is premature."]},
{n:100,topic:"US Tax Court",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Gustavo's tax return was audited and he vehemently disagrees with the auditor's findings. He gets a 90-day letter three months later. Gustavo wants to contest the assessment in court. Which of the following is a benefit for Gustavo to file a petition in the U.S. Tax Court for his proposed deficiency (as opposed to other available Federal court systems)?",opts:["Gustavo can appeal his decision back to IRS Appeals if the judge rules against him.","Gustavo can contest tax deficiencies of any type or amount.","Gustavo can use an enrolled agent to represent him in court.","Gustavo does not have to pay his contested tax first."],a:3,why:"The Tax Court is the only PREPAYMENT forum — the taxpayer litigates before paying. District courts and the Court of Federal Claims require paying first and suing for a refund.",wrongs:["Court decisions aren't appealed back to IRS Appeals.","Tax Court jurisdiction is limited to certain taxes (e.g., no FBAR penalties).","Only Tax Court bar members can represent — an EA credential alone is insufficient.",null]}
]; // END_MOCK2

const MOCK3 = [
{n:1,topic:"OPR Proceedings",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the below statements is correct regarding complaints for the sanctioning of a practitioner, employer, firm, appraiser, or other entity practicing before the Internal Revenue Service?",opts:["In general, discovery may be permitted at the discretion of an Administrative Law Judge.","Within 30 days of receipt of the answer, the presiding judge will notify the parties of the right to request discovery and the timeframe for filing a request.","The complaint can only be served on the respondent in person by a designated employee of the Internal Revenue Service.","A United States Tax Court judge oversees proceedings regarding the complaint."],a:0,why:"In Circular 230 disciplinary proceedings, discovery is not automatic — the Administrative Law Judge may permit it at their discretion.",wrongs:[null,"No such 30-day discovery notification rule exists.","Service can be made by mail, delivery service, or in person — not only in person.","An ADMINISTRATIVE LAW JUDGE presides, not a Tax Court judge."]},
{n:2,topic:"Disciplinary Appeals",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Luther is an enrolled agent who was subject to a disbarment proceeding. The administrative law judge upheld the OPR's decision to disbar him. Does Luther have any appeal rights at this point?",opts:["Within 60 days after the ALJ decision, Luther may appeal to the Treasury Appellate Authority.","Within 60 days after the ALJ decision, Luther may appeal to the Return Preparer Office.","Within 30 days after the ALJ decision, Luther may appeal to the Treasury Appellate Authority.","No, Luther does not have any appeal rights after the administrative law judge makes his decision."],a:2,why:"Either party may appeal the ALJ's decision to the Treasury Appellate Authority within 30 days.",wrongs:["The window is 30 days, not 60.","The RPO is not the appellate body.",null,"Appeal rights DO exist after the ALJ decision."]},
{n:3,topic:"Client Consent to Disclose",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"In which of the following situations must a tax return preparer obtain client consent to disclose or receive sensitive tax return information?",opts:["For purposes of peer reviews.","The preparer receives a grand jury subpoena requesting client records.","A preparer reports a possible crime to authorities involving one of their clients.","None of the above disclosures requires permission from a client."],a:3,why:"All three are recognized exceptions to §7216: peer reviews, court orders/subpoenas, and good-faith reports of crimes don't require client consent.",wrongs:["Peer review is an exception — no consent needed.","Compliance with a subpoena is an exception.","Good-faith crime reporting is an exception.",null]},
{n:4,topic:"Prohibited Acts",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Agustina is an enrolled agent. Which of the following actions is she NOT permitted to do?",opts:["Prepare a formal written protest on behalf of a client to request an appeals conference.","Negotiate a taxpayer's refund check, as long as she has the client's written permission.","Represent a taxpayer before IRS collections.","Submit an e-filed return for a client who is domiciled overseas."],a:1,why:"§10.31 flatly prohibits a practitioner from endorsing or negotiating a client's refund check — no client permission can authorize it.",wrongs:["Permitted — protests are standard representation work.",null,"Permitted — EAs represent before collections.","Permitted — clients abroad can be e-filed for."]},
{n:5,topic:"E-File Rules",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"The IRS' e-file rules prohibit a tax preparer from:",opts:["Filing a return in early January using a client's last pay stub for wage information, if the taxpayer has not yet received his Form W-2.","Charging a separate fee to e-file.","Specifying that the IRS direct deposit a client's tax refund to the client's checking account.","All of the above."],a:0,why:"E-filing with a pay stub instead of the actual Form W-2 ('pay stub filing') is prohibited — the preparer must have the W-2 (or use Form 4852 substitute procedures after Feb 15).",wrongs:[null,"Preparers MAY charge for e-filing services.","Direct deposit to the client's own account is normal and allowed.","Only A is prohibited."]},
{n:6,topic:"Notice of Deficiency",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"A Statutory Notice of Deficiency is also known as a 90-day letter because:",opts:["The taxpayer has 90 days from the date of the letter to file a protest with the IRS Office of Appeals.","The taxpayer has 90 days from the date of the letter to file a formal protest with TIGTA.","The taxpayer has 90 days from the date of the letter to file a petition with the United States Tax Court.","The taxpayer has 90 days from the date of the letter to request a Collection Appeals hearing."],a:2,why:"The 90 days is the deadline to petition the U.S. TAX COURT (150 days if addressed outside the U.S.).",wrongs:["Protests to Appeals respond to the 30-day letter.","TIGTA doesn't take deficiency protests.",null,"CAP hearings relate to collection actions, not deficiency notices."]},
{n:7,topic:"Badges of Fraud",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"During the examination of Joey's Form 1040, the IRS examiner found numerous errors resulting in additional tax. One adjustment was a large amount of unreported income discovered in a concealed bank account. Some deductions were supported with altered documents. Joey also gave false statements throughout the examination. All of the acts of the taxpayer, when seen as a whole, most likely indicate:",opts:["Fraud","Negligence","A tax protester argument","Noncompliance"],a:0,why:"Concealed accounts, altered documents, and false statements are classic badges of FRAUD — deliberate deception, not carelessness.",wrongs:[null,"Negligence is careless, not deliberate concealment and falsification.","No frivolous constitutional arguments were made.","'Noncompliance' understates deliberate deception."]},
{n:8,topic:"Direct Deposit Limits",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"A single financial account is limited to ________ electronically deposited tax refunds per tax year.",opts:["Two","Three","One","No limit"],a:1,why:"The IRS limits any single financial account (or prepaid card) to THREE refund direct deposits per year — additional refunds convert to paper checks. This combats refund fraud.",wrongs:["The limit is three.",null,"The limit is three, not one.","A limit definitely exists."]},
{n:9,topic:"Interest Abatement",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following is NOT a reason for the IRS to abate interest on a taxpayer's tax liability?",opts:["Managerial act","Ministerial act","The interest was incurred when the taxpayer was in a combat zone or federal disaster area.","Reasonable cause"],a:3,why:"Reasonable cause can abate PENALTIES, but not interest. Interest is abated only for unreasonable IRS errors/delays (managerial or ministerial acts), combat zones, or disaster relief.",wrongs:["Valid — IRS managerial acts causing delay support abatement.","Valid — ministerial errors support abatement.","Valid — combat zone/disaster relief suspends interest.",null]},
{n:10,topic:"Identity Verification",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Which of the following documents can be used to verify a taxpayer's Social Security number and correct name spelling?",opts:["Driver's license","Birth certificate","Passport","Social Security card"],a:3,why:"The Social Security card is the document that shows both the SSN and the exact name as registered with SSA.",wrongs:["A license shows identity, not the SSN.","A birth certificate has no SSN.","A passport has no SSN.",null]}
,
{n:11,topic:"Examination Rights",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Trent decided to represent himself in an IRS audit. When he arrived at the IRS office, he became agitated during the initial examination interview and requested to speak to a representative. Which of the following statements is correct?",opts:["The IRS is not required to cease an examination when the taxpayer requests a representative.","The interview must be suspended if the taxpayer clearly requests to consult with a representative.","The taxpayer may leave the examination and finish the audit through correspondence.","If the taxpayer chooses to suspend the interview, he must return in person with his representative."],a:1,why:"IRC §7521(b)(2): if a taxpayer clearly states during an interview that he wants to consult a representative, the IRS MUST suspend the interview.",wrongs:["The IRS IS required to suspend upon a clear request.",null,"Switching to correspondence isn't the statutory right at issue.","No requirement to return in person with the representative."]},
{n:12,topic:"Refund Timing",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"The IRS cannot issue refunds for 2025 tax returns before February 15, 2026, if those returns claim which of the following credits?",opts:["Premium Tax Credit (PTC)","Credit for Excess Social Security and RRTA Tax Withheld","Earned Income Tax Credit (EITC)","Retirement Savings Contributions Credit"],a:2,why:"The PATH Act requires the IRS to hold ENTIRE refunds on returns claiming EITC or the Additional Child Tax Credit until at least February 15.",wrongs:["PTC refunds aren't PATH Act-delayed.","Excess FICA credits aren't delayed.",null,"The Saver's Credit isn't delayed."]},
{n:13,topic:"EITC Requirements",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Which of the following taxpayer numbers is valid for claiming the Earned Income Tax Credit?",opts:["Employer Identification Number","Individual tax identification number","Social Security number","Adoption taxpayer identification number"],a:2,why:"EITC requires a valid SSN (valid for employment) for the taxpayer, spouse, and any qualifying children. ITINs and ATINs don't qualify.",wrongs:["EINs are for businesses.","ITIN holders cannot claim EITC.",null,"ATINs don't qualify for EITC."]},
{n:14,topic:"E-File Program",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Safeguarding of IRS e-file from fraud and abuse is the shared responsibility of:",opts:["The IRS and the Police","The IRS and Authorized IRS e-file Providers","The IRS and the FBI","The Department of the U.S. Treasury and official tax software providers"],a:1,why:"Publication 3112 makes safeguarding e-file a shared duty of the IRS and Authorized IRS e-file Providers.",wrongs:["Police aren't the designated partner.",null,"The FBI isn't the designated partner.","The duty runs to the IRS and its authorized Providers."]},
{n:15,topic:"E-Signatures",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Patrick is a tax return preparer who accepts electronic signatures. He has a client who lives in a different state. How is Patrick required to authenticate the identity of the taxpayer?",opts:["The taxpayer must mail or fax a copy of his driver's license, with a picture ID, to Patrick for review","Patrick must verify that the name, Social Security number, address, date of birth, and other personal information provided by the taxpayer are consistent with information obtained through record checks with applicable agencies or institutions, or through credit bureaus or similar databases","No special verification procedures are required","The taxpayer must mail or fax a copy of his Social Security card to Patrick for review"],a:1,why:"For remote e-signature transactions, the ERO must use knowledge-based identity verification — checking the taxpayer's identifying data against records from agencies, institutions, or credit bureaus.",wrongs:["A mailed license copy isn't the prescribed remote verification.",null,"Remote transactions DO require identity verification.","A mailed SS card copy isn't the prescribed method."]},
{n:16,topic:"Confidentiality Privilege",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Clarence is an enrolled agent. His new client, Chloe, wants to know if their discussions during normal tax preparation are privileged. What type of information or activities would NOT be privileged?",opts:["The preparation of Chloe's income tax return","Non-criminal tax proceedings in federal court actions","Communications pertaining to tax advice","Non-criminal tax matters before the IRS"],a:0,why:"The §7525 practitioner privilege covers tax ADVICE — it does not extend to return preparation, since information given for preparing a return is intended for disclosure to the IRS.",wrongs:[null,"Covered — noncriminal federal court tax proceedings (brought by/against the U.S.).","Covered — tax advice communications are the heart of the privilege.","Covered — noncriminal matters before the IRS."]},
{n:17,topic:"ITINs",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"ITINs automatically expire after _____ years of nonuse.",opts:["Three","One","Five","Two"],a:0,why:"An ITIN expires if not used on a federal tax return at least once in three consecutive years.",wrongs:[null,"One year of nonuse doesn't expire it.","The rule is three years.","The rule is three years."]},
{n:18,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"In which of the following situations is an IRS power of attorney required?",opts:["Allowing the IRS to discuss return information with a fiduciary","Allowing the IRS to discuss return information with a third-party designee","Allowing a partnership representative to perform acts for the partnership","Allowing the tax practitioner to receive, but not cash, taxpayer refund checks"],a:3,why:"Authorizing a practitioner to RECEIVE (never negotiate) a client's refund check requires specific authorization on a Form 2848.",wrongs:["Fiduciaries stand in the taxpayer's shoes — no POA needed.","The checkbox designation on the return covers this.","The partnership representative's authority comes from the partnership return designation.",null]},
{n:19,topic:"IRS Summons Power",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"The IRS has the authority to issue a summons in all of the following instances EXCEPT:",opts:["To require a taxpayer to create a tax return when he has not filed one.","To determine the liability of a taxpayer.","To collect any internal revenue tax liability.","To prepare a substitute return when a taxpayer has not filed one."],a:0,why:"A summons can compel testimony and production of EXISTING records — it cannot force a taxpayer to CREATE a return or document that doesn't exist.",wrongs:[null,"Valid summons purpose — determining liability.","Valid summons purpose — collection.","Valid purpose — gathering data to prepare an SFR."]},
{n:20,topic:"Tax Authority Hierarchy",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which is the highest degree of tax authority for all taxpayers?",opts:["Private Letter Ruling","The Internal Revenue Code","Revenue Rulings","Treasury Department Regulations"],a:1,why:"The Internal Revenue Code — the statute enacted by Congress — is the highest tax authority. Everything else interprets it.",wrongs:["PLRs bind only the requesting taxpayer.",null,"Rulings rank below regulations and the Code.","Regulations interpret the Code and rank below it."]}
,
{n:21,topic:"IRS Information Requests",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"A practitioner must promptly submit records or information requested by the IRS unless the practitioner believes that the records are ____________?",opts:["Protected.","Confidential.","Privileged.","Destroyed or missing."],a:2,why:"§10.20: prompt submission is required unless the practitioner believes in good faith and on reasonable grounds that the records or information are PRIVILEGED.",wrongs:["'Protected' isn't the Circular 230 standard.","General confidentiality doesn't excuse compliance — privilege does.",null,"If records don't exist, the practitioner must say so — that's a different matter."]},
{n:22,topic:"EA Practice Rights",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"What practice rights do all enrolled agents have?",opts:["Limited practice rights before the IRS.","Unlimited practice rights before the IRS and the U.S. Tax Court.","Unlimited practice rights before the IRS.","Unlimited practice rights before the U.S. Treasury Department."],a:2,why:"EAs have UNLIMITED practice rights before the IRS — any taxpayer, any tax matter, any IRS office.",wrongs:["EA rights are unlimited, not limited.","Tax Court practice requires separate bar admission.",null,"The credential covers IRS practice, not all Treasury functions."]},
{n:23,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"The IRS may accept an offer in compromise based on three grounds. Which of the following is NOT a valid basis for submitting an offer in compromise to the IRS?",opts:["Doubt as to liability","Doubt as to collectibility","Effective tax administration","Undue hardship"],a:3,why:"The three OIC grounds are doubt as to liability, doubt as to collectibility, and effective tax administration. \"Undue hardship\" is not a standalone basis (hardship is considered WITHIN effective tax administration).",wrongs:["Valid ground.","Valid ground.","Valid ground.",null]},
{n:24,topic:"Innocent Spouse Claims",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"In the previous year, Beverly filed an Innocent Spouse claim that was denied. She now has new additional information that may help her case; can she file a second claim?",opts:["Yes, Beverly can file a second claim. The second claim must be filed with the Taxpayer Advocate Office.","Yes, Beverly can file a second claim, provide the new additional information, and it will be reconsidered by the IRS. However, she normally cannot appeal the redetermination to the U.S. Tax Court.","Yes, Beverly can file a second claim, but the claim must be filed with the U.S. Tax Court.","No, Beverly cannot file a second Innocent Spouse Claim after an IRS denial."],a:1,why:"A denied innocent spouse claim can be resubmitted with new information for IRS reconsideration — but the reconsideration decision generally can't be petitioned to the Tax Court (that right attached to the original determination).",wrongs:["TAS doesn't process innocent spouse claims.",null,"Second claims go to the IRS, not the court.","Resubmission with new information IS allowed."]},
{n:25,topic:"E-File Fees",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Which type of practitioner fee is prohibited by Publication 1345, Handbook for Authorized IRS e-file Providers?",opts:["Charging a fee for direct deposit","Fixed fees for bookkeeping services","Charging a fee for e-filing a return","Hourly fee rates for tax consulting"],a:0,why:"Pub 1345 prohibits charging a separate fee for direct deposit of a refund.",wrongs:[null,"Bookkeeping fees are normal billing.","E-filing service fees are permitted.","Hourly consulting fees are permitted."]},
{n:26,topic:"Injured Spouse",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Rosalee files jointly with her husband, who has delinquent student loans. Their entire tax refund is applied against his past-due student loans. Rosalee would like to claim her portion of their tax refund, so she files for ____________ relief.",opts:["Equitable relief.","Separation of liability.","Injured spouse.","Innocent spouse."],a:2,why:"INJURED spouse relief (Form 8379) recovers your share of a joint refund that was offset for the OTHER spouse's separate debts.",wrongs:["Equitable relief addresses understated/unpaid tax, not refund offsets.","Separation of liability allocates a deficiency, not a refund.",null,"Innocent spouse addresses understated tax from the other spouse's errors — not offsets."]},
{n:27,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following may the Internal Revenue Service settle by accepting an Offer in Compromise for less than the full amount of the balance due?",opts:["A tax deficiency plus penalties, but not accrued interest","A tax deficiency, but never penalties or accrued interest","A tax deficiency plus penalties and accrued interest","A tax deficiency plus accrued interest, but not penalties"],a:2,why:"An OIC can compromise the ENTIRE liability — tax, penalties, AND interest.",wrongs:["Interest can be compromised too.","Penalties and interest are both compromisable.",null,"Penalties can be compromised too."]},
{n:28,topic:"Practitioner Discipline",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Under Circular 230, in which of the following cases may the IRS suspend a certified public accountant from practice before the IRS?",opts:["The CPA moves outside the U.S. and attempts to represent overseas taxpayers","The CPA's license is suspended by the board of accountancy for a matter unrelated to taxation","The CPA hires unenrolled preparers to work in his office","The CPA takes an aggressive position on a tax return"],a:1,why:"Losing one's state license (even for non-tax reasons) removes the basis for practicing before the IRS as a CPA — grounds for suspension under Circular 230.",wrongs:["Location abroad doesn't bar practice.",null,"Hiring unenrolled preparers is perfectly legal.","Aggressive-but-defensible positions aren't sanctionable."]},
{n:29,topic:"E-File Application",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"As part of the application to become an authorized IRS e-services provider, which of the following categories of tax professionals is required to be fingerprinted?",opts:["Unenrolled tax return preparers","Enrolled agents and unenrolled tax return preparers","Only CPAs and tax attorneys because they are licensed by the state, not the federal government","All e-services applicants must be fingerprinted by the IRS"],a:0,why:"Fingerprinting applies to applicants who are NOT attorneys, CPAs, EAs, or otherwise exempt professionals — i.e., unenrolled preparers.",wrongs:[null,"EAs are exempt from fingerprinting.","CPAs and attorneys are exempt, not required.","Credentialed professionals are exempt."]},
{n:30,topic:"CE Records",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Betty is an enrolled agent who is cleaning up her office files and purging old records. When is the earliest she may shred the records related to her continuing education (CE)?",opts:["Six years following the date of the EA renewal for which the CE is credited","Immediately after the date of the EA renewal for which the CE is credited","Four years following the date of the EA renewal for which the CE is credited","Betty should not destroy her CE records as long as she is in active status"],a:2,why:"CE records must be retained for FOUR YEARS following the date of renewal for which the CE is credited.",wrongs:["Six years is longer than required.","Immediate destruction violates the retention rule.",null,"Indefinite retention isn't required."]}
,
{n:31,topic:"Injured Spouse",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Hardy and Cecilia were recently married and filed jointly. Both spouses were employed during the year, and filed a joint return showing a $2,000 refund. Rather than receiving the refund, they received a notice advising that the entire refund was applied to an amount Hardy owed from a prior year, before they were married. What can Cecilia do to recover her portion of the refund?",opts:["Cecilia could file Form 8857 as an innocent spouse to receive her share of the refund.","Cecilia could be an \"injured spouse\" and should file Form 8379 to obtain her portion of the refund.","Hardy must file Form 8857 as an innocent spouse to separate his share of the refund from Cecilia's.","Nothing, because both spouses are jointly and severally liable."],a:1,why:"Her share of a joint refund was taken for HIS pre-marital debt — the injured spouse remedy, Form 8379.",wrongs:["Form 8857 (innocent spouse) addresses understated tax, not offsets.",null,"Hardy is the debtor — the remedy belongs to Cecilia.","Joint liability doesn't defeat an injured spouse allocation."]},
{n:32,topic:"Paid Preparer Rules",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"A tax preparer must complete the paid preparer section of the tax return in which of the following scenarios?",opts:["An enrolled agent who prepares his father's return for free","A CPA who prepares a return for her brother and charges him only a nominal fee","An employee preparer who completes employment tax returns for his employer","An enrolled agent who prepares her own tax return"],a:1,why:"Any compensation — even a nominal fee — makes someone a PAID preparer who must sign and complete the preparer section.",wrongs:["Free preparation for family isn't paid preparation.",null,"Employees preparing their employer's returns aren't paid preparers.","Self-prepared returns have no paid preparer."]},
{n:33,topic:"Assessment Statute",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"The statute of limitations on assessment increases to six years if there is an omission of more than ________ of the gross income stated on the return.",opts:["75%","25%","50%","15%"],a:1,why:"A substantial omission — more than 25% of gross income — extends the assessment statute from 3 to 6 years.",wrongs:["Too high.",null,"Too high — 25% triggers it.","Too low — the threshold is 25%."]},
{n:34,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"How long is an IRS power of attorney authorization valid?",opts:["Until revoked or withdrawn","Until the close of the taxable year for which it was filed","For three years","Until the taxpayer's retirement"],a:0,why:"A Form 2848 remains in effect until the taxpayer revokes it or the representative withdraws (or the taxpayer dies).",wrongs:[null,"It doesn't expire with the tax year.","No automatic three-year expiration.","Retirement is irrelevant."]},
{n:35,topic:"OPR Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following from Circular 230 is correct regarding sanctions that the OPR can make against an enrolled agent (EA)?",opts:["An EA can be censured.","There cannot be both monetary and nonmonetary sanctions imposed on an EA for the same violation.","A monetary penalty can exceed the gross income derived from the conduct giving rise to the penalty.","An EA can face imprisonment, up to 10 years."],a:0,why:"Censure (public reprimand) is a standard OPR sanction.",wrongs:[null,"Monetary penalties CAN be imposed in addition to other sanctions.","Monetary penalties are capped at the gross income derived from the conduct.","OPR cannot imprison anyone — that requires criminal prosecution."]},
{n:36,topic:"Refund Checks",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Sylvie, an enrolled agent, prepares William's income tax return. William gives Sylvie power of attorney, including the authorization to receive his federal income tax refund check. The IRS sends William's $1,000 refund check to Sylvie's office. William is slow paying his bills and owes Sylvie $500 for tax services. Sylvie should:",opts:["Turn the check directly over to William","Use William's check as collateral for a loan to tide her over until William pays her","Refuse to give William the check until he pays her the $500","Get William's written authorization to endorse the check, cash the check, and reduce the amount William owes her"],a:0,why:"She may RECEIVE the check but must promptly turn it over — she can never endorse, negotiate, or hold it hostage for fees.",wrongs:[null,"Using a client's check as collateral is prohibited misuse.","Withholding the check over a fee dispute is prohibited.","Endorsing/cashing a client's refund check is flatly prohibited, consent or not."]},
{n:37,topic:"Statute Extensions",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Jeronimo's individual income tax return was under IRS examination. Five months before the expiration of the statute of limitations, the Revenue Agent wanted Jeronimo to agree to extend the statute of limitations. Which of the following is true?",opts:["The Revenue Agent must advise Jeronimo that he has a right to either extend the statute of limitations, but if he does so, he must file a formal protest with the Secretary of the Treasury","The Revenue Agent must advise Jeronimo that he has a right to refuse to extend the statute of limitations and if he does agree to an extension, the agreement can be restricted as to particular issues on the tax return","Jeronimo cannot refuse to extend the statute of limitations, if his return is still under examination","The Revenue Agent must advise Jeronimo that he has a right to refuse to extend the statute of limitations, but if he does so, his case will be immediately referred to the U.S. Tax Court"],a:1,why:"The IRS must inform the taxpayer that consent is voluntary — he may refuse, limit the extension to specific issues, or limit the extension period.",wrongs:["No protest to Treasury is involved.",null,"Consent is voluntary — he can refuse.","Refusal doesn't trigger a Tax Court referral; the IRS would simply issue a deficiency notice."]},
{n:38,topic:"Frivolous Returns",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Which of the following is an example of an individual filing a frivolous tax return?",opts:["A person mistakenly claims the Earned Income Tax Credit without meeting the eligibility requirements due to misunderstanding the instructions.","A taxpayer who files a tax return and strikes out the jurat","A fiduciary who files an incorrect tax return and later amends it","A preparer who files a delinquent tax return because the taxpayer refused to file"],a:1,why:"Striking out or altering the jurat (the penalties-of-perjury statement) invalidates the return and is a defined frivolous position.",wrongs:["Honest mistakes aren't frivolous filings.",null,"Errors corrected by amendment aren't frivolous.","Filing late returns isn't frivolous conduct."]},
{n:39,topic:"OPR Complaints",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"In serving a complaint against a practitioner, the Office of Professional Responsibility may use all of the methods listed below EXCEPT:",opts:["First class mail","In person","E-mail","Private delivery service"],a:2,why:"Complaints may be served by certified/first class mail, in person, or private delivery service — E-MAIL is not an authorized service method.",wrongs:["Authorized method.","Authorized method.",null,"Authorized method."]},
{n:40,topic:"EA Enrollment",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Rachel passed all three parts of the EA exam. When is she considered \"officially enrolled\"?",opts:["When she submits Form 23, and the form is processed by the IRS","When the IRS issues her enrollment card","When she passes the final Special Enrollment Exam","When she goes through an IRS suitability check"],a:1,why:"Enrollment is official when the IRS issues the enrollment card (or certificate) — not upon passing the exam or applying.",wrongs:["Application processing isn't the final step.",null,"Passing the SEE is a prerequisite, not enrollment itself.","The suitability check precedes issuance of the card."]}
,
{n:41,topic:"Practitioner Fees",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following fees would NOT be permitted under Circular 230?",opts:["A flat fee for an initial consultation with a client","A contingent fee based on an IRS examination of an original tax return","Unconscionable fees for audit representation services","A written schedule of hourly fees"],a:2,why:"§10.27 prohibits UNCONSCIONABLE fees. (Note: contingent fees ARE allowed for services in connection with an IRS examination or challenge of a return.)",wrongs:["Permitted.","Actually permitted — exam-related contingent fees are an exception to the contingent fee ban.",null,"Permitted."]},
{n:42,topic:"Civil Fraud Penalty",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Tasha's tax return was audited by the IRS. The examiner found evidence of unreported income from illegal activities. The IRS issued an assessment, determining an underpayment of tax due to fraud. How much is the penalty added to her tax?",opts:["100% added to any other penalty provided by law","20% of the underpayment due to fraud","75% of the underpayment due to fraud","50% of the underpayment due to fraud"],a:2,why:"The civil fraud penalty (IRC §6663) is 75% of the underpayment attributable to fraud.",wrongs:["Not the fraud rate.","20% is the accuracy-related penalty rate.",null,"Not the fraud rate."]},
{n:43,topic:"Standards for Returns",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Circular 230 states a practitioner may not willfully, recklessly, or through gross incompetence sign a tax return or claim for refund that the practitioner knows, or reasonably should have known, contains a position that:",opts:["Understates the liability for tax","Lacks a reasonable basis","Is reckless or has intentional disregard of rules or regulations","All of the above"],a:3,why:"§10.34 covers all three: willful understatement, positions lacking a reasonable basis, and reckless or intentional disregard of rules.",wrongs:["Correct but incomplete.","Correct but incomplete.","Correct but incomplete.",null]},
{n:44,topic:"Disreputable Conduct",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following actions is NOT considered \"disreputable conduct\" by the IRS?",opts:["Performance as a notary by a practitioner","Failing to include a valid PTIN on tax returns","Willfully failing to e-file returns electronically if they fall under the e-filing mandate","Willfully failing to file a tax return"],a:0,why:"Being a notary is fine — the prohibition (§10.26) only bars notarizing documents in matters where the practitioner has a material interest before the IRS.",wrongs:[null,"Listed as disreputable conduct in §10.51.","Listed — willful e-file mandate violations.","Listed — willful failure to file one's own returns."]},
{n:45,topic:"Disclosure Statements",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Dottie is a CPA. She is taking a position on a client's tax return that requires disclosure. Which form can be used to disclose a position on a tax return?",opts:["Form 8823","Form 8821","Form 8275","Form 656"],a:2,why:"Form 8275 (or 8275-R for positions contrary to regulations) discloses return positions to avoid accuracy-related and preparer penalties.",wrongs:["8823 is a low-income housing form.","8821 is a tax information authorization.",null,"656 is the Offer in Compromise form."]},
{n:46,topic:"Copies of Returns",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"A preparer is required to provide a copy of the tax return to a client. Which of the following is NOT required?",opts:["The tax preparer must retain a copy or record of the return they prepared.","The tax return must be provided on paper","The tax return must be provided along with a copy of the preparer's PTIN","The preparer must provide a complete copy of the tax return to the taxpayer"],a:1,why:"The client copy may be provided in any medium — electronic copies are fine. Paper is not required.",wrongs:["Required — retain a copy or client list for 3 years.",null,"The completed return includes the preparer's PTIN.","Required — a complete copy must be furnished."]},
{n:47,topic:"Due Diligence",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Suzanne is an enrolled agent interviewing her new client, Jackson, who wants to claim the American Opportunity Tax Credit. Jackson has no proof that he was a student during the year, and other information Jackson offers seems incorrect to Suzanne. Under her due diligence requirements, what should she do?",opts:["Refuse to accept the engagement","Take note of the inconsistent answers and go ahead and submit the claim anyway","Ask additional questions if the information furnished seems incorrect or incomplete","Report the client to the IRS fraud hotline"],a:2,why:"Due diligence requires REASONABLE INQUIRIES when information appears incorrect, inconsistent, or incomplete — and documenting the answers.",wrongs:["Declining isn't required; inquiry is the duty.","Submitting despite known inconsistencies violates due diligence.",null,"Reporting the client isn't the prescribed step."]},
{n:48,topic:"Preparer Numbers",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Amanda is an enrolled agent, and she prepares approximately 250 tax returns for compensation during the year. She is a U.S. citizen. Which of the following numbers is required in order for her to prepare tax returns for compensation?",opts:["An EFIN and an EIN","An EFIN and a PTIN","Only a PTIN","A PTIN and an EIN"],a:1,why:"Preparing 250 returns puts her under the e-file mandate (11+ returns), so she needs an EFIN to e-file — plus the PTIN every paid preparer needs.",wrongs:["An EIN is for employers, not required to prepare returns.",null,"At 250 returns the e-file mandate requires an EFIN too.","An EIN isn't required."]},
{n:49,topic:"Private Letter Rulings",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"What is a private letter ruling?",opts:["A private letter issued by the IRS Office of Appeals","A communication from the Internal Revenue Service in response to a taxpayer's written request for guidance on a particular tax issue","A private letter ruling becomes public once Tax Court litigation begins","A response from Congress to the IRS to clarify Congressional action on a specific tax issue"],a:1,why:"A PLR is the IRS's written response to a specific taxpayer's request for guidance applying the law to their situation.",wrongs:["Appeals doesn't issue PLRs — the Office of Chief Counsel does.",null,"That's not what defines a PLR.","Congress doesn't issue PLRs."]},
{n:50,topic:"Small Case Procedure",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"What is the dollar limit (including taxes and penalties) for a taxpayer to be eligible to request the U.S. Tax Court small case procedures?",opts:["$100,000","$15,000","$50,000","$25,000"],a:2,why:"S-case (small case) procedures are available when the disputed amount is $50,000 or less per year or period.",wrongs:["Too high.","Too low.",null,"Not the S-case threshold."]}
,
{n:51,topic:"Treasury Regulations",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following is NOT a type of Treasury regulation?",opts:["Legislative regulation","Procedural regulation","Interpretive regulation","Congressional regulation"],a:3,why:"Treasury regulations come in three types: legislative, interpretive, and procedural. \"Congressional regulation\" doesn't exist — Congress writes statutes, not regulations.",wrongs:["Real type — issued under specific congressional delegation.","Real type — housekeeping/administrative rules.","Real type — interpreting the Code.",null]},
{n:52,topic:"Knowledge of Omission",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Josiah is an enrolled agent. His new client, Paloma, asks Josiah to prepare her tax return. He requests her prior-year returns, and Paloma admits she has failed to file tax returns for the prior three years. Paloma only wants Josiah to prepare the current year return. What is Josiah required to do?",opts:["He must decline the engagement and report Paloma to the IRS for tax fraud","He must decline the engagement","He must report Paloma to the Office of Professional Responsibility","He must advise Paloma that she did not comply with Federal tax law, and the consequences for not filing the returns"],a:3,why:"§10.21 requires advising the client of the noncompliance and its consequences. He isn't required to decline the current-year engagement or report her.",wrongs:["Reporting the client isn't required (and would breach confidentiality).","Declining isn't mandated.","OPR handles practitioners, not taxpayers.",null]},
{n:53,topic:"Examinations",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"Who is allowed to determine the time and place for an audit of a taxpayer?",opts:["The taxpayer's representative and the IRS have an equal say in the determination of the time and location of an audit","The IRS","The taxpayer","The IRS, the taxpayer, and the taxpayer's representative have an equal say in determining the time and location of an audit"],a:1,why:"The IRS sets the time, place, and method of examination — considering reasonableness, but the decision is the IRS's.",wrongs:["Requests can be made, but there's no equal say.",null,"The taxpayer may request changes, not dictate.","The authority rests with the IRS."]},
{n:54,topic:"Third-Party Designee",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"A Third-Party Designee has the right to do which of the following tasks?",opts:["Respond to IRS notices about math errors, offsets, and return preparation","Receive refund checks","Represent the taxpayer before the IRS","Sign a binding agreement for the taxpayer"],a:0,why:"The checkbox designee can exchange information about the specific return — processing questions, math errors, offsets, and refund status.",wrongs:[null,"Receiving refund checks requires POA authorization.","Representation requires Form 2848 and practice eligibility.","Signing agreements requires a POA with that authority."]},
{n:55,topic:"Foreign Asset Reporting",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Alexander plans to e-file his federal tax return. He owns specified foreign assets and must file Form 8938, Statement of Specified Foreign Financial Assets, as well as the FBAR. What should he do to make sure his tax return filing is complete?",opts:["He must mail both forms separately to the Internal Revenue Service.","Attach Form 8938 and the FBAR to his federal return as a pdf attachment.","Attach only the FBAR to his federal return. The Form 8938 must be sent to the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network.","Attach only Form 8938 to his federal return and file the FBAR through the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network's E-Filing system."],a:3,why:"Form 8938 goes WITH the tax return to the IRS; the FBAR (FinCEN Form 114) is filed separately through FinCEN's BSA E-Filing System.",wrongs:["The FBAR never goes to the IRS.","The FBAR can't be attached to the return.","This reverses the two forms.",null]},
{n:56,topic:"Direct Deposit Rules",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Renata is an enrolled agent. Her client, Lucian, wants to use direct deposit. Which of the following statements about direct deposit is INCORRECT?",opts:["Lucian should not request a deposit of his refund to an account that is not in his name.","Renata must accept any direct deposit election to any financial institution designated by Lucian.","Lucian may designate refunds for direct deposit to a credit card account","Lucian may designate refunds for direct deposit to up to three qualified accounts."],a:2,why:"Credit card accounts are NOT eligible refund destinations — that statement is incorrect (the answer to this 'incorrect' question).",wrongs:["True statement — accounts must be in the taxpayer's name.","True — preparers must honor the taxpayer's direct deposit election.",null,"True — up to three accounts via Form 8888."]},
{n:57,topic:"CAF Number",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"What is the purpose of a Centralized Authorization File (CAF) number?",opts:["It is an IRS file designed to prevent the theft of taxpayer identification numbers","It is an IRS file containing authorization information from both Forms 2848 and Forms 8821","It is an IRS file containing the PTINs of all paid tax preparers","It is an IRS file containing information from Forms 2848 only"],a:1,why:"The CAF records third-party authorizations from BOTH Form 2848 (POA) and Form 8821 (tax information authorization).",wrongs:["It's not an anti-theft system.",null,"PTINs are managed separately by the RPO.","It includes Forms 8821 as well."]},
{n:58,topic:"AFSP",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"The Annual Filing Season Program is a _____________.",opts:["Charitable program","Voluntary program","Volunteer program","Mandatory program"],a:1,why:"The AFSP is a voluntary IRS program recognizing unenrolled preparers who complete annual continuing education.",wrongs:["Not charitable.",null,"'Volunteer' describes VITA — AFSP participants are paid preparers.","Participation is optional."]},
{n:59,topic:"Conflicts of Interest",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Stacy is an enrolled agent with two clients who are both parties in the potential sale of a business. The sale is not prohibited by law. Stacy believes she can provide competent and diligent representation to each client. Which statement best describes the action Stacy must take to fulfill the requirements of Circular 230 before representing both clients?",opts:["Stacy must meet with both clients at the same time to inform them of the potential for conflict of interest.","Stacy must obtain either oral or written permission from both clients, stating they have been made aware of the potential for conflict of interest.","Stacy must inform each client of the potential for a conflict of interest and then obtain written waivers of the conflict from both clients.","Since she has already researched the situation and determined she is competent to handle the matter, Stacy is not required to take further action."],a:2,why:"§10.29 requires informed consent CONFIRMED IN WRITING from each affected client, retained for 36 months.",wrongs:["A joint meeting isn't the requirement — written waivers are.","Oral permission is insufficient.",null,"Written waivers are still required regardless of her own assessment."]},
{n:60,topic:"Preparer Penalty Appeals",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Edward is a tax preparer. One of his client's returns was recently audited, and a large penalty was assessed against the taxpayer. A few weeks later, Edward receives a letter from the IRS with a proposed §6694 penalty against him for understatement of a taxpayer's liability. Before Edward is formally assessed, how many days does he have to file an appeal?",opts:["10 days","45 days","30 days","The penalty is assessed immediately and must be appealed retroactively"],a:2,why:"Proposed preparer penalties come with a 30-day letter — Edward has 30 days to request Appeals consideration before assessment.",wrongs:["Too short.","Not the window.",null,"Pre-assessment appeal rights exist."]}
,
{n:61,topic:"Remote E-Signatures",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"A \"remote transaction\" for electronic signature is one in which the taxpayer is electronically signing the signature authorization form and the ERO is not physically present with the taxpayer. For remote transactions, the ERO must record which of the following information?",opts:["The taxpayer's name, address, and a copy of their birth certificate.","The taxpayer's SSN and driver's license number.","The taxpayer's name, social security number, address and driver's license number.","The taxpayer's name, social security number, address and date of birth."],a:3,why:"For remote e-signature transactions the ERO must record the taxpayer's name, SSN, address, and DATE OF BIRTH as part of identity verification.",wrongs:["No birth certificate requirement.","Incomplete — name, address, and DOB are required.","Driver's license number isn't the required element — DOB is.",null]},
{n:62,topic:"Taxpayer Advocate",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Gregory is facing financial hardship paying his tax debt, and would like assistance from the Taxpayer Advocate Service. All of the following are considerations in determining whether his request will be granted EXCEPT:",opts:["Gregory is facing a significant and unexpected tax liability.","Gregory is facing an immediate threat of adverse action.","Gregory is experiencing economic harm or is about to suffer economic harm.","Gregory will suffer irreparable harm or long-term adverse impact if relief is not granted."],a:0,why:"Merely OWING a large or unexpected tax bill isn't a TAS criterion. TAS looks at economic harm, immediate adverse action threats, irreparable harm, and systemic IRS breakdowns.",wrongs:[null,"A real TAS criterion.","A real TAS criterion.","A real TAS criterion."]},
{n:63,topic:"Continuing Education",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Natalie became an enrolled agent several years ago. For renewal purposes, what is the minimum number of hours of continuing education (CE) she must take each year?",opts:["She is required to take a minimum of 16 hours per year, two of which must be on ethics","She is required to take a minimum of 15 hours per year, two of which must be on ethics","She is required to take a minimum of 18 hours per year, four of which must be on ethics","She is required to take a minimum of 24 hours per year, two of which must be on ethics"],a:0,why:"EAs need 72 hours per 3-year cycle, with a MINIMUM of 16 hours each year — including 2 hours of ethics annually.",wrongs:[null,"15 isn't the annual minimum.","Not the requirement.","24/year would be 72 total, but the annual MINIMUM is 16."]},
{n:64,topic:"E-File Rejections",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"A rejected electronic individual income tax return can be corrected and retransmitted without new signatures if changes do not differ from the amounts on the original electronic return by more than:",opts:["$50 of total income or AGI","$500 of tax due or AGI","$100 of total income or AGI","A rejected e-file return cannot be retransmitted without new signatures"],a:0,why:"The tolerance is $50 for \"total income\" or \"AGI\" (and $14 for tax/withholding/refund/amount-due fields).",wrongs:[null,"Wrong amounts.","The threshold is $50, not $100.","Retransmission within tolerances doesn't need new signatures."]},
{n:65,topic:"Splitting Refunds",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Paul and Donatella are married but live separately. They choose to file jointly, but they want to split their refund between their respective checking accounts. How is this accomplished, if possible?",opts:["Splitting a refund is not possible","They must complete Form 8888, Allocation of Refund (Including Savings Bond Purchases)","They must file separate tax returns if they wish to split their refund","This can only be accomplished if filing a paper return"],a:1,why:"Form 8888 allocates a refund among up to three accounts (and savings bond purchases).",wrongs:["Splitting is fully supported.",null,"No need for separate returns.","Works with e-filed returns too."]},
{n:66,topic:"Return Signatures",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Which of the following is NOT an acceptable method for a taxpayer to sign a completed tax return?",opts:["Practitioner PIN","Identity Protection (IP) PIN","Handwritten signature on a paper-filed return","Self-select PIN"],a:1,why:"The IP PIN is an identity-theft protection number — it is NOT a signature method. Signatures use the Self-Select PIN, Practitioner PIN, or a handwritten signature on paper.",wrongs:["Valid signature method.",null,"Valid on paper returns.","Valid signature method."]},
{n:67,topic:"E-File Rejections",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"When the IRS rejects a return that an ERO attempted to e-file for a taxpayer, the ERO must advise the taxpayer of the rejection and also provide the taxpayer with what additional information?",opts:["Declaration Control Number","The reject code, accompanied by an explanation","IRS code section","IRS Customer Service phone number for assistance"],a:1,why:"The ERO must give the taxpayer the reject code(s) WITH an explanation of what they mean.",wrongs:["The DCN isn't the required disclosure.",null,"No code section requirement.","Not the required item."]},
{n:68,topic:"OPR Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following sanctions will the Office of Professional Responsibility NOT impose on a practitioner?",opts:["Formal reprimand","Disbarment","Censure","Criminal penalties"],a:3,why:"OPR imposes administrative sanctions only. Criminal penalties come from courts after DOJ prosecution.",wrongs:["Available OPR sanction.","Available OPR sanction.","Available OPR sanction.",null]},
{n:69,topic:"Refund Options",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"All of the following are options for how a taxpayer may direct their tax refund EXCEPT:",opts:["Receive the refund as a paper check.","Split the refund, with a portion applied to next year's estimated tax and the remainder received as direct deposit or a paper check.","Direct deposit the refund into an eligible mobile app or prepaid debit card.","Use the refund (or part of it) to purchase U.S. Series I Savings Bonds."],a:0,why:"Per this exam's framing, a paper check is the DEFAULT delivery when no direction is made — it isn't one of the refund 'direction' options (splitting, applying to estimates, deposits, savings bonds) the question tests.",wrongs:[null,"A genuine direction option — applying part to next year's estimated tax.","A genuine option — eligible cards/apps accept deposits.","A genuine option — Form 8888 allows I-bond purchases."]},
{n:70,topic:"Tax Court Jurisdiction",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"For which of the following does the U.S. Tax Court NOT have jurisdiction?",opts:["FBAR penalties","Income tax deficiencies","Gift tax deficiencies","Innocent spouse relief"],a:0,why:"FBAR penalties arise under Title 31 (Bank Secrecy Act), not the tax code — they're litigated in district court or the Court of Federal Claims, not Tax Court.",wrongs:[null,"Core Tax Court jurisdiction.","Within Tax Court jurisdiction.","The Tax Court reviews innocent spouse determinations."]}
,
{n:71,topic:"EA Practice Rights",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Larry is an enrolled agent. He has a new client, Danielle, who is under audit and has serious tax difficulties. Regarding the representation of Danielle, how may Larry represent her before the IRS?",opts:["Only if Larry prepared the return under examination","He may represent her before the U.S. Tax Court","At any conferences or meetings with the IRS Examination Division","At any tax-related federal court proceedings"],a:2,why:"An EA may represent any taxpayer at all administrative levels of the IRS — including all Examination Division conferences and meetings.",wrongs:["EA rights don't depend on who prepared the return.","Tax Court requires separate admission.",null,"Federal courts require bar admission."]},
{n:72,topic:"Family Representation",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Callum has a 25-year-old daughter named Genie. Callum has an accounting degree, but he is not an enrolled practitioner. Genie's tax return was chosen for examination. Genie is not claimed as a dependent on Callum's return. Which of the following statements is correct?",opts:["Callum may represent his daughter before the IRS if Genie signs a Form 2848","Callum may not represent his daughter before the IRS because Genie is no longer a minor and Callum is not enrolled to practice","Callum may not represent his daughter because she is not his dependent","Callum is not an enrolled preparer, so he may not represent an adult child because of privacy regulations and conflicts of interest"],a:0,why:"Under the limited practice rules, immediate family members (parent, spouse, child, sibling) may represent a taxpayer before the IRS with a signed Form 2848.",wrongs:[null,"Adult children still count as family for limited practice.","Dependency status is irrelevant.","Family representation is expressly permitted."]},
{n:73,topic:"Installment Agreements",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Penny has an installment agreement with the IRS. On January 5, 2025, she gets into a bad car accident and misses a payment. The IRS sends her a notice 30 days later proposing to terminate her installment agreement. Which of the following statements is correct?",opts:["Penny may appeal the termination by filing a petition with the U.S. Tax Court within 30 days of receipt of the notice","Penny may appeal the termination of her installment agreement","Penny may appeal the termination, and the IRS will be prohibited from levying until 60 days after her appeal is completed","Penny has no appeal rights once the installment agreement is in default"],a:1,why:"Proposed installment agreement terminations can be appealed through the Collection Appeals Program (CAP).",wrongs:["Tax Court isn't the forum for IA terminations.",null,"The specific 60-day levy prohibition claim isn't accurate.","Appeal rights exist via CAP."]},
{n:74,topic:"ITIN Documentation",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Deborah is a tax preparer. She has a new client who does not have a Social Security card and is not eligible for a Social Security number. What type of documentation should she request from the taxpayer in this case?",opts:["A driver's license","A passport","An ITIN card or letter","None of these documents are acceptable"],a:2,why:"A taxpayer ineligible for an SSN uses an ITIN — the IRS-issued ITIN card or assignment letter documents the number for return preparation.",wrongs:["Shows identity, not a taxpayer ID number.","No U.S. taxpayer number on it.",null,"The ITIN documentation is exactly right."]},
{n:75,topic:"Power of Attorney",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"An IRS power of attorney must contain all of the following information EXCEPT:",opts:["The specific date the tax return was filed, if delinquent","The name and taxpayer identification number of the taxpayer","The name and address of the representative","The type of tax involved"],a:0,why:"Form 2848 requires taxpayer identity, representative details, type of tax, form number, and years/periods — NOT the filing date of any delinquent return.",wrongs:[null,"Required element.","Required element.","Required element."]},
{n:76,topic:"Reasonable Basis",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following can constitute a \"reasonable basis\" for a position taken on a tax return?",opts:["IRS publications","Internal Revenue Manual","Congressional committee reports","IRS tax forms and instructions"],a:2,why:"Committee reports are legislative history — recognized AUTHORITY that can support a reasonable basis. Publications, the IRM, and form instructions are not authority.",wrongs:["Not authority for penalty protection.","Internal guidance only — not authority.",null,"Not authority."]},
{n:77,topic:"Circular 230 Definitions",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Under Circular 230, the definition of a \"tax return\" includes:",opts:["Only an original return","An original return or an amended return","A claim for a refund, an original return, and an amended return","A claim for a refund, an original return, and an extension request"],a:2,why:"The Circular 230 definition covers original returns, amended returns, and claims for refund.",wrongs:["Too narrow.","Omits refund claims.",null,"Extension requests aren't part of the definition."]},
{n:78,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Martina Gonzalez submitted an Offer in Compromise (OIC) to the IRS. Under which condition might the IRS accept her offer?",opts:["Martina has been referred to the Department of Justice for criminal prosecution.","Martina's tax debt might not be accurate, and there is doubt as to the correctness of the amount owed.","Martina is insolvent and currently filing for Chapter 13 bankruptcy","Martina has not filed all legally required tax returns before submitting the offer."],a:1,why:"Doubt as to LIABILITY — a genuine dispute about the correctness of the assessed amount — is a valid basis for OIC acceptance.",wrongs:["DOJ referral bars OIC acceptance.",null,"Open bankruptcy disqualifies an OIC.","Filing compliance is required before an offer is processed."]},
{n:79,topic:"Circular 230 Jurisdiction",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following individuals is NOT under the jurisdiction of Circular 230?",opts:["An enrolled agent who does representation work for clients before the IRS but does not prepare tax returns.","An employee of a tax preparation firm who collects receipts, organizes records, and gathers information for a practitioner.","An enrolled agent who files Form 2848 for a client he is representing in an examination.","A licensed attorney that provides written tax advice, but does not prepare tax returns."],a:1,why:"Purely clerical/administrative staff who gather and organize records aren't practicing before the IRS and fall outside Circular 230.",wrongs:["EAs are always covered.",null,"Representation is squarely covered.","Written tax advice is practice — attorneys giving it are covered."]},
{n:80,topic:"Notarizing",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following actions is always prohibited by Circular 230?",opts:["Representing two clients when there is a conflict of interest","Notarizing documents for the clients that the practitioner represents before the IRS","Promoting a tax shelter to an existing client","Charging contingent fees"],a:1,why:"§10.26: a practitioner may not act as a notary with respect to matters in which they are employed as counsel or have a material interest before the IRS — this is an absolute bar.",wrongs:["Conflicts CAN be waived with written consent.",null,"Not all tax shelters are abusive — promotion isn't automatically prohibited.","Contingent fees are permitted in specific situations (exams, refund claims for interest/penalties, judicial proceedings)."]}
,
{n:81,topic:"AFSP Representation",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Isidore is not an enrolled agent, CPA, or attorney, but she has a valid AFSP certificate. Lorenzo, the president of Carlsbad Engineering, Inc. hired Isidore to prepare the company's 2025 Form 1120-S. Isidore e-filed the return on February 16, 2026, signing as preparer. A year later, the IRS began an examination of Carlsbad Engineering's 2023, 2024, and 2025 Federal tax returns. Isidore has a power of attorney to represent Carlsbad Engineering. Under Circular 230, Isidore is permitted to represent Carlsbad Engineering during the examination with regard to its:",opts:["Isidore cannot represent Carlsbad Engineering in any capacity.","She can represent the company for all tax years","2025 Form 1120-S only","She can only represent Carlsbad Engineering if Lorenzo, the company president, is present during the examination."],a:2,why:"AFSP limited representation covers only returns the holder PREPARED AND SIGNED — here, just the 2025 Form 1120-S. The 2023 and 2024 returns are off-limits.",wrongs:["She can represent for the return she prepared and signed.","Her rights don't extend to years she didn't prepare.",null,"No 'president present' rule exists."]},
{n:82,topic:"AFSP Exemptions",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Some unenrolled preparers are exempt from the Annual Filing Season course requirement because of their completion of other recognized state or national competency tests. Which of the following individuals would be REQUIRED to complete the Annual Filing Season course in order to obtain an official Record of Completion?",opts:["Tax preparers who have passed the Special Enrollment Exam Part I within the past two years.","A tax preparer who passed the Registered Tax Return Preparer exam.","Accounting students registered at a recognized educational institution","Volunteer Income Tax Assistance (VITA) volunteers"],a:2,why:"Exemptions cover SEE Part 1 passers, former RTRP exam passers, VITA volunteers, and certain state-credentialed preparers. Merely being an accounting student earns no exemption — the course is required.",wrongs:["Exempt from the course.","Exempt from the course.",null,"Exempt from the course."]},
{n:83,topic:"Competence",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Sterling is an enrolled agent. A long-time client starts a new business in an industry with numerous specialized tax regulations and incentives. Sterling begins the client's tax return but finds he does not understand most of the elections and credits applicable to the new business. Can Sterling prepare and sign this tax return with his current lack of understanding?",opts:["Yes, but Sterling must have someone else from his firm review the return before signing it.","No. Sterling cannot prepare or sign a tax return if he lacks sufficient competence","Yes. It is not unusual for a tax preparer to lack understanding in complex areas of tax law","Yes, but only if Sterling accepts a reduced fee for the work from his client"],a:1,why:"§10.35 requires COMPETENCE — the knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation necessary for the matter. He must become competent (study, consult) or decline/refer.",wrongs:["A colleague's review doesn't cure his own incompetence to sign.",null,"Complexity doesn't excuse the competence requirement.","Fee discounts don't substitute for competence."]},
{n:84,topic:"Permitted Practices",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Landry is an enrolled agent. Which of the following actions would be permitted?",opts:["Landry uses the official IRS eagle logo in all her advertising","Landry offers to file a client's tax return using the client's last pay stub, instead of Form W-2","Landry offers direct deposit of refund checks for a small fee","Landry offers her clients refund anticipation loans"],a:3,why:"Offering RALs is PERMITTED — provided the advertising is accurate (described as a loan) and fee rules are followed.",wrongs:["Official IRS insignia are prohibited in advertising.","Pay stub filing is prohibited.","Charging for direct deposit is prohibited.",null]},
{n:85,topic:"Interest on Unpaid Tax",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Desiree owes $900 in income taxes. She doesn't have the funds to pay the entire amount all at once. She plans to file her tax return on time, but she would like to make monthly payments on the amount due. When does interest begin to accrue on the amount that she owes?",opts:["She will not owe any interest because the amount that she owes is less than the safe harbor threshold of $1,000","Interest accrues on any unpaid tax from the due date of the return (including extensions) until the date of payment in full","Interest begins to accrue on the amount due on the close of the tax year (December 31)","Interest accrues on any unpaid tax from the due date of the return (not including extensions) until the date of payment in full"],a:3,why:"Interest runs from the ORIGINAL due date of the return — an extension of time to file is not an extension of time to pay, so extensions don't delay interest.",wrongs:["The $1,000 threshold relates to the estimated tax penalty, not interest.","Extensions do NOT delay the start of interest.","Interest starts at the return due date (typically April 15), not December 31.",null]},
{n:86,topic:"Identity Theft Prevention",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"What information should a tax preparer review to deter the possibility of identity theft?",opts:["Form W-2","Utility statement","Last year's tax return","Photo identification"],a:3,why:"Reviewing government-issued photo ID confirms the person sitting in front of you is who they claim to be — the front-line defense against identity thieves.",wrongs:["A W-2 can be stolen or forged — it doesn't prove identity.","Utility bills show an address, not identity.","A prior return could be in a thief's possession.",null]},
{n:87,topic:"PTIN Rules",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Can multiple preparers in one tax office share a single PTIN?",opts:["Yes, but only if the principal preparer continues to sign the tax returns","Yes","Yes, but only at the same office location","No, each preparer must obtain their own PTIN"],a:3,why:"A PTIN is personal to the individual preparer — it can never be shared, even within one firm.",wrongs:["No sharing arrangement is permitted.","PTINs are individual.","Location doesn't matter — sharing is prohibited.",null]},
{n:88,topic:"Appeals",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Kareem's tax return was chosen for examination. He did not agree with the examiner's findings and wishes to appeal the examiner's conclusion. What must he do if he wants to request an appeal?",opts:["He can file a claim with the U.S. Tax Court within 30 days of the examiner's decision letter","He has 30 days from the date of the examiner's decision letter to appeal","He can request a collection due process hearing within 30 days of the examiner's decision","He can submit an offer in compromise, requesting a review of the examiner's decision"],a:1,why:"The 30-day letter gives the taxpayer 30 days to file a protest and request an Appeals conference.",wrongs:["Tax Court comes later, after a 90-day letter.",null,"CDP hearings relate to collection actions, not exam results.","An OIC settles debts; it doesn't review exam findings."]},
{n:89,topic:"Missed Deficiency Deadline",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Sophie receives a notice of deficiency in the mail with respect to individual income tax. She ignores the notice, and fails to respond within 90 days. What is Sophie's option if she wants to contest her tax deficiency in court?",opts:["She can request an extension to the period of time for petitioning the U.S. Tax Court","She cannot contest her tax in the U.S. Tax Court. She must pay a portion of the amount owed and submit a claim for a refund to the IRS. If denied, she can sue the IRS for a refund in a U.S. district court or Court of Federal Claims","She cannot contest her tax deficiency in the U.S. Tax Court. She must pay the entire amount owed and submit a claim for a refund to the IRS. If denied, she can sue the IRS for a refund in a U.S. district court or Court of Federal Claims","She has no more appeal rights and owes the tax deficiency"],a:2,why:"After the 90-day window closes, the Tax Court door shuts. Her remaining route: pay the tax IN FULL, file a refund claim, and sue in district court or the Court of Federal Claims if denied.",wrongs:["The 90-day deadline is jurisdictional — no extensions.","Refund suits require FULL payment first (Flora rule), not partial.",null,"She still has the pay-and-sue refund route."]},
{n:90,topic:"Appeals Representation",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Which of the following persons may represent a taxpayer at an IRS Appeals conference?",opts:["An unenrolled tax preparer who prepared the tax return in question","A disbarred practitioner with a current PTIN","An enrolled agent","A friend of the taxpayer"],a:2,why:"Appeals conferences require a practitioner with full representation rights — EA, CPA, or attorney. Even AFSP holders cannot go to Appeals.",wrongs:["Unenrolled preparers (even AFSP) can't appear before Appeals.","Disbarment removes all representation rights; a PTIN doesn't restore them.",null,"Friends have no representation rights."]},
{n:91,topic:"Employee Representation",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Karen is a full-time employee of Bridgeline Construction, Inc. She is the company bookkeeper and prepares all the payroll tax returns and W-2 forms. During the year, Bridgeline receives an IRS notice regarding a payroll tax issue. Karen is not an enrolled practitioner. Can Karen represent her employer before the IRS?",opts:["Karen may represent her employer before the IRS because she is a full-time employee of the business.","Karen may only respond to the notice if she is a bona-fide corporate officer.","Karen may respond to the notice, but a company officer must sign it.","No, Karen may not represent her employer before the IRS."],a:0,why:"Under the limited practice rules, a regular full-time employee may represent her employer before the IRS.",wrongs:[null,"Officer status isn't required — full-time employment suffices.","No officer signature requirement applies.","Employee representation is expressly permitted."]},
{n:92,topic:"ERO Application",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Dabir is a tax preparer working for a national tax franchise. He would like to become an electronic return originator (ERO), so he can start his own home-based tax practice and e-file his clients' returns. What is the FIRST step in this application process?",opts:["Apply for an electronic filing identification number (EFIN)","Undergo a thorough background check of his tax compliance history","Apply for a PTIN","Create an IRS e-Services account"],a:3,why:"The e-file application is submitted THROUGH an IRS e-Services account — creating that account is step one.",wrongs:["The EFIN application happens inside e-Services — after the account exists.","The suitability check happens after the application is submitted.","He's already a preparer, so he has a PTIN; it's not the ERO first step.",null]},
{n:93,topic:"Advertised Fees",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Solomon is an enrolled agent. He mailed an advertisement announcing a flat rate of $100 for the preparation of a tax return for new clients. Solomon gets inundated with calls, and wants to increase the price. Solomon is bound by this advertised rate for a minimum of _______ days after the last date on which the fee schedule was published.",opts:["45 days","30 days","20 days","60 days"],a:1,why:"§10.30: a practitioner must honor published fee schedules for at least 30 calendar days after the last date they were published.",wrongs:["Not the required period.",null,"Too short.","Longer than required."]},
{n:94,topic:"Delay Tactics",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Omar is an enrolled agent. His client, Fatima, has a large tax deficiency. Fatima tells Omar she has no intention of paying her tax liability, no matter what, even though she has more than enough financial resources to pay in full. Knowing his client is not compliant, Omar advises Fatima to submit a request for a Collection Due Process hearing in order to stop collection activity. Is Omar in violation of Circular 230?",opts:["There is insufficient information to make a determination","No, since Omar cannot control his client's actions and is simply giving advice regarding her legal options","No, since it gives Omar's client additional time to pay her tax liability","Yes, as the submission is only to delay tax administration"],a:3,why:"§10.23/§10.34: a practitioner may not advise submissions made primarily to DELAY tax administration. Fatima can pay and simply won't — the CDP request has no legitimate purpose.",wrongs:["The facts are clear — pure delay.","Advising a filing whose sole purpose is delay is itself the violation.","Buying time for a client who CAN pay but won't is exactly the abuse.",null]},
{n:95,topic:"Currently Not Collectible",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"If the IRS declares a taxpayer to be in \"currently not collectible\" status, which of the following is true?",opts:["Penalties and interest accruals will be suspended","The IRS will not require or expect the taxpayer to make payments toward the liability","The collection statute of limitations is suspended","The IRS is prohibited from filing a notice of federal tax lien"],a:1,why:"CNC means the IRS stops expecting payments and halts levies while hardship continues. But interest/penalties keep accruing, the collection clock keeps RUNNING, and the IRS may still file a lien.",wrongs:["Accruals continue during CNC.",null,"The CSED generally continues to run during CNC.","Liens can still be filed to protect the government's interest."]},
{n:96,topic:"Payment Options",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Alexa owes $4,000 when she files her tax return. She is not able to pay the entire balance due by the due date of the return. Which of the following is NOT an acceptable payment option?",opts:["Alexa can pay her IRS debt using her credit card","Alexa can submit an Installment Agreement Request and pay in installments","Alexa can contest her liability in the U.S. Tax Court","Alexa can make payments online at www.pay.gov"],a:2,why:"There's nothing to contest — she agrees she owes the tax on her own return, and no notice of deficiency exists. Tax Court isn't a payment option.",wrongs:["Valid payment method (processor fees apply).","Valid — installment agreements exist for this.",null,"Valid online payment channel."]},
{n:97,topic:"Bankruptcy and Tax Debt",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following statements is true regarding the discharge of federal tax liabilities in bankruptcy proceedings?",opts:["The filing of bankruptcy cannot eliminate tax debt, only defer it to a later date.","Bankruptcy will always permanently stop the Internal Revenue Service from enforcement action.","Not all tax debts are dischargeable, but Federal income tax debt can sometimes be discharged via bankruptcy.","All federal tax liabilities are automatically discharged in a Chapter 7 or Chapter 11 bankruptcy."],a:2,why:"Income tax debts CAN be discharged if they meet timing rules (e.g., returns due 3+ years ago, filed 2+ years ago, assessed 240+ days). Trust fund taxes and recent liabilities are not dischargeable.",wrongs:["Discharge IS possible for qualifying income taxes.","The automatic stay is temporary, not permanent.",null,"Discharge is conditional, never automatic for all tax debts."]},
{n:98,topic:"PTIN Requirements",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following professional designations is ALWAYS required to obtain a PTIN?",opts:["CPAs","Attorneys","Enrolled agents","Certified Financial Planners"],a:2,why:"Holding an active PTIN is a condition of the EA credential itself — every EA must maintain one. CPAs, attorneys, and CFPs only need PTINs if they prepare returns for compensation.",wrongs:["Only if they prepare returns for compensation.","Only if they prepare returns for compensation.",null,"CFP work doesn't itself require a PTIN."]},
{n:99,topic:"Currently Not Collectible",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Oliver has a large tax debt from a prior year. In 2025 he sustains a serious injury and subsequently loses his job. He is experiencing serious financial hardship and cannot pay his normal living expenses. What status allows people in financial hardship situations to defer paying their tax bill until their situation improves?",opts:["Currently not collectible","Permanently not collectible","Financially bankrupt","Typically uncollectible"],a:0,why:"CNC (hardship) status suspends collection until the taxpayer's financial situation improves.",wrongs:[null,"No such status exists.","Bankruptcy is a court proceeding, not an IRS status.","Not a real IRS designation."]},
{n:100,topic:"Enrollment Cycle",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"In the licensing of enrolled agents, an \"enrollment cycle\" refers to:",opts:["The year in which the enrolled agent receives their initial enrollment","The three successive enrollment years preceding the effective date of renewal","The enrollment year preceding the effective date of renewal","The amount of continuing professional education required each year of enrollment"],a:1,why:"The enrollment cycle is the three successive enrollment years preceding the renewal effective date — the period over which the 72 CE hours are counted.",wrongs:["Initial enrollment year isn't the cycle.",null,"The cycle is three years, not one.","CE amounts are a requirement within the cycle, not its definition."]}
]; // END_MOCK3

const MOCK4 = [
{n:1,topic:"Federal Tax Liens",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is a Notice of Federal Tax Lien?",opts:["A petition filed by a debtor that begins the bankruptcy process","A statutory demand to ask for payment of a debt from an individual","A document filed with the local government recording office that identifies Federal tax liabilities owed by the taxpayer.","A filing that allows tax debt to be collected through a collection agency"],a:2,why:"The NFTL is publicly filed with local recording offices to put creditors on notice of the government's claim against the taxpayer's property.",wrongs:["That's a bankruptcy petition.","That describes a notice and demand for payment.",null,"The NFTL doesn't involve collection agencies."]},
{n:2,topic:"PTIN Requirements",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Abella is a full-time bookkeeper for Ortega Brothers, LLC, a landscaping company. As part of her job duties, Abella processes the company's payroll and also prepares the partnership return for Ortega Brothers, including Schedules K-1 for each of the individual members. She does not prepare any other returns for compensation. Is Abella required to obtain a PTIN?",opts:["Abella must request a PTIN, an EFIN and also submit to a background check.","Yes, because she is considered a paid preparer and must obtain a PTIN.","Abella is allowed to forgo obtaining a PTIN only if the IRS provides her a written waiver.","No, she is not considered a paid preparer, and she is not required to obtain a PTIN."],a:3,why:"An employee who prepares her EMPLOYER'S returns as part of her job duties is not a 'tax return preparer' — no PTIN needed.",wrongs:["None of those are required for employees preparing employer returns.","She isn't a paid preparer within the meaning of the rules.","No waiver process exists or is needed.",null]},
{n:3,topic:"Withdrawing from Representation",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Judith is an enrolled agent, and she fired a client for nonpayment. She had been representing the client in an IRS audit of their business. What action should Judith take with the IRS if she chooses to permanently end her representation of this particular client?",opts:["Nothing. It is the taxpayer's responsibility to notify the IRS","She must call the IRS to revoke the POA","She cannot withdraw from an audit engagement once the audit has already begun","She should formally withdraw from the engagement by logging into her Tax Pro account with the IRS and withdrawing her POA for the client or by writing \"WITHDRAW\" across the top of Form 2848 and sending it to the IRS via mail or fax."],a:3,why:"A representative withdraws by writing \"WITHDRAW\" across the top of the Form 2848 copy (signed and dated) and sending it in, or via the online Tax Pro Account.",wrongs:["The representative must formally withdraw.","Phone revocation isn't accepted.","Withdrawal is allowed at any stage.",null]},
{n:4,topic:"Written Advice",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Circular 230 outlines expectations for written advice provided by a tax practitioner. Which of the following is a correct statement?",opts:["A practitioner cannot use the possibility of an IRS audit as a factor in providing written advice","A practitioner can use the possibility of an IRS audit as a factor in providing written advice","A practitioner can use the possibility of an IRS audit as a factor in providing written advice for individual taxpayers but not for business entities","Depending on the client's individual situation, a practitioner can use the possibility of an IRS audit as a factor in providing the written advice"],a:0,why:"§10.37 prohibits relying on audit-lottery odds — written advice may never factor in the chance the return won't be examined.",wrongs:[null,"Expressly prohibited.","No individual/business distinction exists.","No client-situation exception exists."]},
{n:5,topic:"EA Licensing",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following statements is NOT correct about becoming an enrolled agent?",opts:["All applicants must apply for enrollment by submitting Form 23","All applicants must pass the three parts of the Special Enrollment Examination","All applicants must pass a suitability check","All applicants must be at least 18 years old"],a:1,why:"Not ALL applicants take the SEE — former IRS employees with qualifying experience can enroll without the exam. That makes this the incorrect statement.",wrongs:["True — Form 23 is required of everyone.",null,"True — everyone passes the suitability check.","True — minimum age is 18."]},
{n:6,topic:"AFSP Requirements",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for participation in the Annual Filing Season Program?",opts:["The participant must have a valid EIN","The participant must consent to Circular 230","The participant must have a valid PTIN","The participant must pass a suitability and tax compliance check"],a:0,why:"An EIN is an employer number — irrelevant to AFSP participation. The program requires a PTIN, CE completion, Circular 230 consent, and compliance checks.",wrongs:[null,"Required — consent to Circular 230 subpart B duties.","Required.","Required."]},
{n:7,topic:"Notary Rules",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Rory is a licensed notary public as well as an enrolled agent. Regarding Rory's notary commission, which of the following statements is correct?",opts:["A federally-authorized tax practitioner cannot notarize any documents in which he has a material interest in matters before the IRS","A federally-authorized tax practitioner is required to be a notary public","A federally-authorized tax practitioner can only notarize documents for clients if they sign a written consent","A federally-authorized tax practitioner is not allowed to be a notary public"],a:0,why:"§10.26: practitioners may be notaries generally, but may not notarize documents in IRS matters where they act as counsel or have a material interest.",wrongs:[null,"No notary requirement exists.","Consent doesn't cure the material-interest bar.","Being a notary is perfectly fine in general."]},
{n:8,topic:"Appeals Process",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"If a taxpayer and the IRS fail to settle a non-docketed examination controversy in the IRS Appeals Office, the next event to occur is a/an:",opts:["Issuance of notice and demand for payment","Return of the case to the Revenue Agent for further review","Referral of the case to the Taxpayer Advocate","Issuance of a notice of deficiency"],a:3,why:"When Appeals can't settle, the IRS issues the statutory notice of deficiency (90-day letter), opening the door to Tax Court.",wrongs:["Notice and demand comes after assessment — which requires the deficiency process first.","The case doesn't go back to the examiner.","TAS doesn't adjudicate disputes.",null]},
{n:9,topic:"Accuracy-Related Penalty",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Mandy is self-employed, but she refuses to keep books and records. Instead, she simply estimates her income and expenses every year. Later, the IRS examines Mandy's most recent tax return and disallows the majority of the expenses. What penalty would the IRS be most likely to assess against Mandy?",opts:["A fraud penalty of 75% for tax evasion","A $5,000 penalty for filing a frivolous return","An accuracy-related penalty of 20% of the underpayment of tax","A 25% penalty for failure-to-file a timely tax return"],a:2,why:"Failing to keep adequate records is NEGLIGENCE — the 20% accuracy-related penalty under §6662, not fraud.",wrongs:["No intent to deceive shown — carelessness isn't fraud.","Her return isn't frivolous, just sloppy.",null,"She filed on time — no failure-to-file issue."]},
{n:10,topic:"Practice Categories",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following enrolled practitioners has the most limited area of practice before the IRS?",opts:["Certified public accountant","Attorney","Enrolled agent","Enrolled actuary"],a:3,why:"Enrolled actuaries may practice only on specified retirement/pension plan matters — the narrowest scope among these.",wrongs:["Unlimited practice rights.","Unlimited practice rights.","Unlimited practice rights.",null]}
,
{n:11,topic:"Confidentiality Privilege",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Enrolled agents are allowed to assert a limited practitioner confidentiality privilege relating to:",opts:["Communications regarding tax shelters","Noncriminal federal tax matters","Criminal and noncriminal tax matters brought before the IRS","The preparation of tax returns"],a:1,why:"The §7525 privilege covers tax ADVICE in noncriminal tax matters before the IRS and noncriminal federal court proceedings.",wrongs:["Tax shelter communications are expressly excluded.",null,"Criminal matters are NOT covered.","Return preparation is not privileged."]},
{n:12,topic:"Extensions and Penalties",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Veronica owes a balance of $400 but does not have her paperwork together by April 15. She files Form 4868 (extension) before the deadline, eventually files her return June 1, and pays her balance due with the return. Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding an extension of time to file?",opts:["The extension extends the time for Veronica to pay all the taxes that she owes","Veronica will avoid a failure-to-file penalty, but she will owe a failure-to-pay penalty, as well as a small amount of interest.","No penalty applies because Veronica filed an extension","The extension prevents all penalties, including late-payment penalties. But she will still owe interest on the amount due."],a:1,why:"An extension covers FILING only. Paying after April 15 still triggers the failure-to-pay penalty (0.5%/month) plus interest — but no failure-to-file penalty since she filed within the extension.",wrongs:["Extensions never extend the payment deadline.",null,"The late-payment penalty still applies.","It doesn't prevent the late-payment penalty."]},
{n:13,topic:"Disbarment Effects",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"If an enrolled agent is disbarred from practice, the IRS will:",opts:["Not recognize a power of attorney that names the individual as a representative","Allow the enrolled agent to retain limited practice rights for representation of past clients","Allow the practitioner to give occasional tax shelter advice","Allow the practitioner to represent clients before the IRS examination division"],a:0,why:"Disbarment ends all representation rights — the IRS will reject any POA naming the disbarred individual.",wrongs:[null,"No grandfather rights for past clients.","Tax advice as practice is prohibited.","No representation of any kind remains."]},
{n:14,topic:"Joint Liability",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"On a jointly filed return (MFJ), both spouses are _______ for all the tax due, even if only one spouse earns any income.",opts:["Jointly and severally liable","Independently liable","Not responsible","Partially liable"],a:0,why:"Joint and several liability means each spouse can be pursued for 100% of the joint tax — regardless of who earned the income.",wrongs:[null,"'Independent' liability isn't the standard for joint returns.","Both are fully responsible.","Each is liable for the WHOLE amount, not a part."]},
{n:15,topic:"Preparer Recordkeeping",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Under Circular 230, a tax preparer is required to keep which of the following?",opts:["Copies of all returns they have prepared or retain a list of clients, and the types of tax returns prepared","Either a copy of all tax returns prepared, or a list of clients and tax returns prepared. However, hard copies are required in both cases.","Copies of all tax returns prepared","A list of clients and tax returns prepared that includes information about the taxpayer, tax year, and type of return prepared. The list may be stored electronically or kept as a hard copy"],a:0,why:"The preparer must keep EITHER copies of returns OR a list of clients with the returns prepared — retained for 3 years after the return period.",wrongs:[null,"Electronic storage is fully acceptable — no hard copy mandate.","Copies aren't mandatory if a client list is kept.","A list is one option, but keeping copies is equally valid — the either/or is the rule."]},
{n:16,topic:"ERO Eligibility",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Which of the following persons could be an Electronic Return Originator (ERO)?",opts:["Human Resources Specialist","Certified Network Administrator","Tax attorney","Volunteer Retirement Coordinator"],a:2,why:"EROs originate e-filed returns — a role for tax professionals. A tax attorney can apply and qualify.",wrongs:["Not a tax practice role.","IT credentials don't confer ERO status.",null,"Not a tax practice role."]},
{n:17,topic:"Enrollment Denial",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following may be grounds for denial of enrollment?",opts:["Failure to have a valid Social Security number","Failure to have valid U.S. citizenship","Failure to timely pay personal income taxes","Personal bankruptcy"],a:2,why:"Personal tax noncompliance — unfiled returns or unpaid taxes — is classic grounds for denying enrollment on suitability.",wrongs:["Alternative procedures exist for those without SSNs.","Citizenship is not required to be an EA.",null,"Bankruptcy alone isn't disqualifying."]},
{n:18,topic:"Form 8867",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"For which refundable credit is a tax preparer NOT required to file Form 8867, Paid Preparer's Due Diligence Checklist?",opts:["American Opportunity Tax Credit","Credit for Excess Social Security and RRTA Tax Withheld","Additional Child Tax Credit","Earned Income Credit"],a:1,why:"Form 8867 covers EITC, CTC/ACTC/ODC, AOTC, and HOH status. Excess Social Security withholding credit has no due diligence checklist.",wrongs:["Covered by Form 8867.",null,"Covered.","Covered."]},
{n:19,topic:"Bankruptcy and Collection",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Alma filed for personal bankruptcy during the year. She has an existing federal tax debt. What are the consequences in this scenario?",opts:["Delinquent returns will be filed on the taxpayer's behalf (SFR; substitute for return)","A levy will be filed automatically against the taxpayer","The filing of a Tax Court petition will be delayed","The collection statute of limitations is suspended by the length of time Alma is in bankruptcy, plus an additional six months"],a:3,why:"Bankruptcy suspends IRS collection (automatic stay), and the collection statute is tolled for the bankruptcy period plus 6 months.",wrongs:["SFRs aren't an automatic bankruptcy consequence.","The automatic stay PREVENTS levies.","Tax Court timing isn't the direct consequence here.",null]},
{n:20,topic:"EITC Requirements",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"What is required by every taxpayer who claims the Earned Income Tax Credit?",opts:["Physical presence in the U.S. for the entire tax year","U.S. citizenship","A valid Social Security number","A qualifying dependent"],a:2,why:"Every EITC claimant needs a valid SSN. Citizenship isn't required (resident aliens qualify), and childless workers can claim a smaller EITC.",wrongs:["More than half the year suffices for residency purposes; full-year physical presence isn't the test.","Resident aliens can claim EITC.",null,"The childless EITC exists — no dependent needed."]}
,
{n:21,topic:"Disbarment Reinstatement",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Reginald is an enrolled agent. He was disbarred from practice by the IRS for filing fraudulent returns. How many years must he wait before he can petition the IRS for reinstatement?",opts:["Disbarment is permanent","One year","Five years","Ten years"],a:2,why:"A disbarred practitioner may petition OPR for reinstatement after 5 years.",wrongs:["Reinstatement is possible after 5 years.","Too soon.",null,"Longer than the rule."]},
{n:22,topic:"IRS Summons",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Esther is an enrolled agent. She has a former client named Kent, whom she fired for nonpayment. During the year, Esther receives an IRS summons relating to Kent, with an order for her to appear before the IRS. What must Esther do about the summons?",opts:["Esther is required to forward the summons to Kent, but she is not required to respond to it since Kent is no longer her client","Esther is not required to respond to the summons, but she must attempt to notify the IRS by mail that Kent is no longer her client","Esther must respond to the summons","Esther is not required to respond to the summons, nor is she required to notify her former client of the summons"],a:2,why:"A summons is legally enforceable — she must respond and appear regardless of whether Kent is a current client.",wrongs:["Client status doesn't nullify a summons.","She must respond, not just notify.",null,"Ignoring a summons risks enforcement and contempt."]},
{n:23,topic:"Collection Statute",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Once a taxpayer's tax liability is __________, the statute of limitations for collections begins to run.",opts:["Levied","Assessed","Determined","Estimated"],a:1,why:"The 10-year collection clock starts on the date of ASSESSMENT.",wrongs:["Levies are collection actions taken during the period.",null,"'Determined' isn't the statutory trigger.","Estimates don't start the clock."]},
{n:24,topic:"Assessment Statute",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"With regards to federal taxes, the \"statute of limitations for assessment\" refers to what?",opts:["The maximum time period in which the IRS can charge a felony tax crime","The maximum time period in which the IRS can collect unpaid taxes","The maximum time period in which the IRS can disbar a tax practitioner","The maximum time period in which the IRS can assess a tax deficiency"],a:3,why:"The assessment statute (generally 3 years from filing) limits how long the IRS has to ASSESS additional tax.",wrongs:["Criminal statutes are separate (generally 6 years).","That's the COLLECTION statute (10 years).","Practitioner discipline isn't governed by this statute.",null]},
{n:25,topic:"Best Practices",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Tax practitioners should use \"best practices\" when representing a client before the IRS. All of the following are cited as examples of best practices in Circular 230 EXCEPT:",opts:["Acting fairly and with integrity in dealings with the IRS","Advising the client regarding any potential accuracy-related penalties","Clearly communicating with clients and the IRS","Arriving at a conclusion supported solely by the client's information"],a:3,why:"Best practices require conclusions supported by THE LAW AND THE FACTS — not merely whatever the client says. Establishing facts, evaluating reasonableness, and applying law to facts is the standard.",wrongs:["A cited best practice.","A cited best practice.","A cited best practice.",null]},
{n:26,topic:"Competence",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Circular 230 requires practitioners to demonstrate ____________ when representing their clients.",opts:["Competence","Courtesy","Trustworthiness","Intelligence"],a:0,why:"§10.35 explicitly requires COMPETENCE — appropriate knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation.",wrongs:[null,"Courtesy is nice but not the §10.35 standard.","Not the codified term.","Not the codified requirement."]},
{n:27,topic:"Tax Court Practice",unit:"SU 11: IRS Appeals and the US Tax Court",q:"Which of the following tax professionals is allowed to practice before the U.S. Tax Court without first passing the Tax Court exam?",opts:["Enrolled agent","Attorney","CPA","Enrolled Actuary"],a:1,why:"Attorneys are admitted to the Tax Court bar on application without examination. Non-attorneys (EAs, CPAs) must pass the Tax Court's non-attorney exam.",wrongs:["EAs must pass the Tax Court exam.",null,"CPAs must pass the exam too.","Enrolled actuaries must also pass it."]},
{n:28,topic:"Return Signatures",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Taxpayers must sign their e-filed 1040 returns:",opts:["Before the return has been prepared","With a digital signature","Under penalty of perjury","With an original, handwritten signature"],a:2,why:"All return signatures — electronic or not — are made under penalties of perjury.",wrongs:["Signing blank/unprepared returns is prohibited.","PIN methods are used; 'digital signature' isn't the required standard.",null,"E-filed returns use electronic signature methods, not handwritten ones."]},
{n:29,topic:"Direct Deposit",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Taxpayers often elect the direct deposit option because it is the fastest way of receiving refunds. What is the preparer's responsibility if the taxpayer wishes to direct deposit their refund?",opts:["A preparer does not have to accept a taxpayer's direct deposit election.","A preparer must accept any direct deposit election designated by the taxpayer. The preparer cannot charge an additional fee for direct deposit.","A preparer must accept any direct deposit election designated by the taxpayer, but the preparer can charge an additional fee for direct deposit.","A preparer does not have to accept any direct deposit election designated by the taxpayer, whether or not an additional fee is charged."],a:1,why:"Preparers must honor the taxpayer's direct deposit designation and may NOT charge a separate fee for it.",wrongs:["The election must be accepted.",null,"Direct deposit fees are prohibited.","The election must be accepted."]},
{n:30,topic:"Trust Fund Recovery Penalty",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Who could be a \"responsible person\" for purposes of the trust fund recovery penalty?",opts:["Any employee within an organization who has the duty to perform and the power to direct the collecting, accounting, and paying of employment (trust fund) taxes","Any person with authority and control over funds to direct their disbursement","A member of the board of trustees for a nonprofit charity","All of the above may be a \"responsible person.\""],a:3,why:"Responsibility follows FUNCTION, not title — employees with payroll authority, anyone controlling disbursements, and even nonprofit board members can all be responsible persons.",wrongs:["True, but incomplete.","True, but incomplete.","True, but incomplete.",null]}
,
{n:31,topic:"IRC §7216",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"IRC Section 7216 prescribes criminal penalties for tax professionals who illegally:",opts:["Fail to file required information returns","Fail to pay taxes due","Contact taxpayers by phone or e-mail","Disclose tax return information"],a:3,why:"§7216 criminalizes the knowing or reckless DISCLOSURE OR USE of tax return information without consent.",wrongs:["Information return failures are §6721/6722 civil penalties.","Payment failures are different penalties.","Contacting clients isn't a crime.",null]},
{n:32,topic:"Bypassing a Representative",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"In which situation may the IRS contact the taxpayer directly, even if a recognized representative is in place?",opts:["The taxpayer has been deemed incompetent","The recognized representative has unreasonably delayed an IRS examination","The recognized representative is not an enrolled tax practitioner","The taxpayer is outside the United States"],a:1,why:"If the representative is responsible for unreasonable delay or hindrance, the IRS may bypass the representative and contact the taxpayer directly (with manager approval).",wrongs:["Incompetence involves fiduciaries, not bypass.",null,"A valid representative's enrollment status isn't the bypass trigger.","Location abroad doesn't authorize bypass."]},
{n:33,topic:"IRS Guidance",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Which of the following types of IRS guidance generally has the highest authority in establishing precedence for all taxpayers?",opts:["Technical advice memoranda","Revenue Ruling","Official IRS publications","Private letter ruling"],a:1,why:"Revenue rulings are PUBLISHED official positions applicable to all taxpayers and citable as precedent — the highest among these options.",wrongs:["TAMs apply to the specific case only.",null,"Publications aren't authority.","PLRs bind only the requesting taxpayer."]},
{n:34,topic:"Third-Party Designee",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Lacey wants to designate her tax preparer, Walcott, as the third-party designee on her individual tax return. Walcott has an AFSP certificate, but no other licensing. Based solely on the third-party authorization, which of the following actions will Walcott NOT be able to take on Lacey's behalf?",opts:["Walcott can respond to IRS notices about math errors on her return","Walcott can receive (but not cash) her tax refund check on her behalf","Walcott can speak with the IRS regarding the status of a payment related to Lacey's return","Walcott can call the IRS for information about the processing of her return"],a:1,why:"Receiving a refund check requires specific POA authorization — the return checkbox designation never covers it.",wrongs:["Within designee authority.",null,"Within designee authority.","Within designee authority."]},
{n:35,topic:"Federal Tax Liens",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is a federal tax lien?",opts:["A legal seizure of the taxpayer's property to satisfy a tax debt","A collection action by the courts","A preliminary assessment of tax","A legal claim to the property of the taxpayer as security for a tax debt"],a:3,why:"A lien is the government's legal CLAIM against property as security — it attaches to assets but seizes nothing.",wrongs:["Seizure is a LEVY, not a lien.","Liens arise by statute, not court action.","A lien follows assessment; it isn't an assessment.",null]},
{n:36,topic:"Criminal Investigations",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Yasmin is an enrolled agent who has been in practice for many years. She has just learned that one of her long-time clients, Ronald, is being investigated by the IRS for criminal tax evasion and fraud. What should Yasmin do?",opts:["Contact a criminal defense attorney and arrange a deal to jointly represent the client in the criminal proceedings","Enter into discussions with the client in order to prepare a case to defend him against possible criminal charges","Contact the authorities who are investigating her client and offer to turn over all of her client's records","End the representation immediately and refer the client to a licensed attorney"],a:3,why:"Criminal tax matters exceed an EA's practice rights, and the §7525 privilege doesn't apply in criminal cases — the client needs an attorney (with attorney-client privilege) immediately.",wrongs:["Joint arrangements risk her communications being discoverable; the attorney should engage her if needed (Kovel).","EAs can't defend criminal cases, and her communications aren't privileged there.","Volunteering client records breaches her duties.",null]},
{n:37,topic:"Joint Return Representation",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Zena is an enrolled agent. She represents Joaquin, whose tax return is under examination. The return was filed jointly with his wife, but they have since divorced. Joaquin does not know the whereabouts of his ex-wife. Joaquin still wants Zena to represent him. What is the proper course of action?",opts:["Joaquin must represent himself during the examination of his joint return","Zena is allowed to represent Joaquin during the examination of his joint return, provided Joaquin signs a valid power of attorney (Form 2848)","Zena is allowed to represent Joaquin, but only if he agrees to amend his filing status to married filing separately","Zena cannot represent Joaquin, because she does not have authorization from his ex-wife"],a:1,why:"Each spouse on a joint return can independently authorize their own representative — the ex-wife's consent is not required for Zena to represent Joaquin alone.",wrongs:["He can be represented.",null,"No filing status amendment is required (or even possible post-deadline).","The ex-spouse's authorization is only needed to represent HER."]},
{n:38,topic:"Audit Probability Advice",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Alexander is an enrolled agent. His client, Josefina, asks him the odds of being audited if he claims certain deductions. Alexander replies that the risk is very low, with only 1% of similar tax returns being audited. Is Alexander in violation of Circular 230?",opts:["No, but only if the advice he provides is oral and not in writing","Circular 230 does not address this issue","Yes. He cannot take into consideration whether a tax return may or may not be audited on a certain issue","No. He is providing good service in helping his client pay the lowest amount of tax"],a:2,why:"Practitioners may not evaluate positions based on audit-lottery odds — advising based on the chance of examination violates §10.37 standards.",wrongs:["The prohibition isn't limited to written advice in this context.","Circular 230 addresses it directly.",null,"'Service' based on audit odds is the violation."]},
{n:39,topic:"EITC Bans",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Patsy improperly claims the EITC on her return. During the audit, she cannot provide documentation. The IRS disallows the EITC and imposes interest and penalties. The IRS later determines the SSN for the claimed dependent was stolen, and Patsy knowingly committed tax fraud. What other possible repercussions are likely?",opts:["The IRS will likely impose a lifetime ban on claiming EITC","The IRS will likely impose a two-year ban on claiming EITC","The IRS will arrest the taxpayer for fraud","The IRS will likely impose a ten-year ban on claiming EITC"],a:3,why:"FRAUDULENT EITC claims trigger a 10-year ban. (Reckless disregard triggers 2 years.)",wrongs:["No lifetime ban exists.","Two years applies to reckless/intentional disregard — fraud gets 10.","The IRS doesn't arrest; criminal referrals go to DOJ.",null]},
{n:40,topic:"Bankruptcy Stay",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Which of the following immediately stops all IRS assessment and collection actions for a taxpayer, regardless of the year and type of assessment?",opts:["When a taxpayer's authorized representative contacts the IRS on his behalf","When a taxpayer files for bankruptcy protection","When a taxpayer files Form 1127, Extension of Time for Payment Due to Hardship","When a taxpayer files a case in Tax Court for only certain, but not all, years in which it has a tax assessment."],a:1,why:"The bankruptcy AUTOMATIC STAY immediately halts IRS assessment and collection across the board.",wrongs:["Representative contact changes nothing about collection.",null,"Form 1127 extends payment for specific hardship — no blanket stay.","A Tax Court petition only affects the years petitioned."]}
,
{n:41,topic:"EA Advertising",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Benjamin Lopez is an enrolled agent. He wants to print new business cards for his office. Which of the following descriptions would be prohibited by Circular 230?",opts:["Benjamin Lopez, enrolled agent, enrolled to practice before the Internal Revenue Service","Benjamin Lopez, enrolled agent, authorized to practice before the Internal Revenue Service","Benjamin Lopez, EA, certified to practice before the Internal Revenue Service","Benjamin Lopez, EA, enrolled to practice before the IRS"],a:2,why:"EAs may say 'enrolled to practice' or 'admitted to practice' — but never 'CERTIFIED,' which falsely implies certification by the IRS.",wrongs:["Approved phrasing.","Approved phrasing.",null,"Approved phrasing."]},
{n:42,topic:"Form 2848",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"IRS Form 2848 provides space for the information and signatures of up to ____________.",opts:["One tax year","Two spouses","Four authorized representatives","Three different entities"],a:2,why:"Form 2848 accommodates up to FOUR representatives.",wrongs:["Multiple years/periods can be listed.","Each spouse files their own Form 2848 — joint filers can't share one.",null,"One taxpayer/entity per form."]},
{n:43,topic:"OPR Complaints",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Nellie is an enrolled agent who received a complaint from the Office of Professional Responsibility (OPR). A formal complaint against a practitioner is NOT required to disclose:",opts:["The deadline for response to the complaint","The name of the IRS employee who submitted the complaint","The specific sanctions recommended against the practitioner","The nature of the complaint"],a:1,why:"The complaint must name the respondent, describe the violations, state the sanction sought, and give the response deadline — but it need not identify the referring IRS employee.",wrongs:["Required element.",null,"Required element.","Required element."]},
{n:44,topic:"Knowledge of Error",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Bernice is an enrolled agent with a new client, Archer, who owns several rental properties. In reviewing his prior-year tax returns, she discovers that Archer has failed to claim depreciation for any of the rental properties. What should Bernice do to comply with Circular 230?",opts:["Nothing. Claiming depreciation deductions is a choice a taxpayer is free to make, but it is not required","Promptly advise the client of the error and the consequences of not correcting the error","Contact the IRS, in confidence, to notify revenue agents about the error","Insist on amending the client's prior-year tax returns"],a:1,why:"§10.21 — advise the client promptly of the error and its consequences. (Depreciation isn't optional: allowed-or-allowable rules reduce basis whether or not it was claimed.)",wrongs:["Failing to claim depreciation IS an error with real consequences (basis reduction on sale).",null,"Disclosure to the IRS without consent breaches confidentiality.","The client decides whether to amend — the practitioner can't insist."]},
{n:45,topic:"EITC Bans",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"The IRS audited Darden's tax return and disallowed his claimed EITC. The IRS determined that Darden's erroneous claim was due to a reckless disregard of the rules because he knowingly claimed a dependent who was not his qualifying child. For how many tax years is Darden barred from claiming the EITC?",opts:["4 years","2 years","1 year","3 years"],a:1,why:"Reckless or intentional disregard of EITC rules triggers a 2-YEAR ban. (Fraud triggers 10 years.)",wrongs:["Not the statutory ban.",null,"Too short.","Not the statutory ban."]},
{n:46,topic:"Disclosure Consent",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Which of the following disclosures of taxpayer information is NOT allowed without a taxpayer's written consent?",opts:["When a disclosure solicits business from an existing client, and the business is not related to the IRS","When a disclosure is made to a taxpayer's fiduciary","When there is a disclosure of taxpayer information for preparation of state or local tax returns","When a tax preparation firm discloses return information to others in the firm for purposes of assisting in the preparation of the tax return"],a:0,why:"Using return information to solicit UNRELATED business requires written §7216 consent. The other disclosures fall under recognized exceptions.",wrongs:[null,"Fiduciary disclosures are excepted.","State/local return preparation is excepted.","Intra-firm preparation assistance is excepted."]},
{n:47,topic:"Disreputable Conduct",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"All of the following are examples of disreputable conduct that can lead to suspension or disbarment for an enrolled agent EXCEPT:",opts:["Willfully failing to e-file returns electronically if the preparer falls under the e-filing mandate","Solicitation of former clients using direct mail","Maintaining an active partnership with a practitioner who has been disbarred (but does not sign returns)","Failing to include a valid PTIN on tax returns"],a:1,why:"Truthful direct mail solicitation is permitted advertising — not disreputable conduct.",wrongs:["Listed in §10.51 — willful e-file mandate violations.",null,"Knowingly maintaining a practice partnership with a disbarred person is prohibited assistance.","Listed — willful PTIN omissions."]},
{n:48,topic:"Continuing Education",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"What is the minimum number of continuing education hours that an enrolled agent must take each year of their renewal cycle?",opts:["18 hours","72 hours","24 hours","16 hours"],a:3,why:"The ANNUAL minimum is 16 hours (including 2 ethics); the full cycle requires 72 hours over 3 years.",wrongs:["Not the annual minimum.","72 is the 3-year cycle total, not the annual minimum.","24/year would exceed the minimum — 16 is the floor.",null]},
{n:49,topic:"E-File Rejections",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"If an e-filed return is rejected, what is the preparer required to do?",opts:["It is the taxpayer's responsibility to ensure that their e-filed return is correctly submitted","The preparer must take reasonable steps to inform the taxpayer within 72 hours of the rejection","The preparer must take reasonable steps to inform the taxpayer within 24 hours of the rejection, with the exception of weekends and holidays","The preparer must take reasonable steps to inform the taxpayer within 24 hours of the rejection"],a:3,why:"When the IRS rejects a return and it can't be fixed and retransmitted, the ERO must take reasonable steps to inform the taxpayer within 24 HOURS.",wrongs:["The ERO carries the notification duty.","24 hours, not 72.","No weekend/holiday carve-out exists.",null]},
{n:50,topic:"EA Practice Scope",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following tasks can be performed by ANY enrolled agent on behalf of their client?",opts:["Prepare and sign a United States Tax Court petition to contest a notice of deficiency","Prepare and sign a protest to challenge examination results in the IRS Appeals Office","Prepare and file a lawsuit for a refund in the United States District Court","Prepare and file a bankruptcy petition in a United States Bankruptcy Court due to unpaid tax balances"],a:1,why:"Appeals protests are administrative IRS work — squarely within every EA's practice rights. Court filings require bar admission.",wrongs:["Tax Court petitions require Tax Court admission.",null,"District court requires attorney representation.","Bankruptcy petitions are legal practice for attorneys."]}
,
{n:51,topic:"EA Practice Limits",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"An enrolled agent is able to represent tax clients before all of the following EXCEPT:",opts:["IRS Office of Appeals","A U.S. district court","IRS Collections division","IRS Examination division"],a:1,why:"Courts require bar admission — an EA's practice rights cover only administrative IRS levels.",wrongs:["Within EA rights.",null,"Within EA rights.","Within EA rights."]},
{n:52,topic:"OIC Appeals",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Martha is a taxpayer who is unable to pay her tax liability. She applied for an offer in compromise to settle her debt, but the IRS rejected her offer. How many days does Martha have to appeal the rejection?",opts:["180 days","30 days","90 days","60 days"],a:1,why:"A rejected OIC may be appealed within 30 days of the rejection letter (Form 13711).",wrongs:["Too long.",null,"90 days is for Tax Court petitions.","Not the OIC window."]},
{n:53,topic:"Direct Deposit Rules",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Andrea is a tax return preparer. When preparing a return for her client, Darius, Andrea learns he does not have a bank account to receive a direct deposit of his tax refund. Andrea offers to use HER account to receive the direct deposit and turn the money over to Darius once deposited. Is this an acceptable action?",opts:["This is acceptable if Darius signs a power of attorney, giving Andrea the right to receive his refund","This is never acceptable","This is acceptable in all circumstances","This is acceptable if Andrea receives prior written consent from Darius"],a:1,why:"A preparer may NEVER have a client's refund deposited into her own account — no consent or POA can authorize it.",wrongs:["POAs can't authorize this.",null,"Never acceptable.","Written consent doesn't cure it."]},
{n:54,topic:"Identity Theft",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Chelsea attempted to e-file her tax return on February 15, but her return was rejected because another return bearing her Social Security number had already been filed. She does not know who used her SSN. What should Chelsea do?",opts:["Chelsea must paper-file her return and attach Form 14039, Identity Theft Affidavit","Chelsea should not file her return if someone has already filed a return using her Social Security number","Chelsea should attempt to correct the error and e-file the return again","Chelsea should request an identity protection PIN and wait to file her return until she gets it"],a:0,why:"When a duplicate-SSN rejection indicates identity theft, the victim paper-files the true return with Form 14039 attached.",wrongs:[null,"She must still file her legitimate return.","Retransmitting will keep rejecting — the SSN is already 'used.'","Waiting delays her filing; the paper-file-with-14039 procedure is the fix."]},
{n:55,topic:"Disbarred Individuals",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Disbarred individuals are still allowed to:",opts:["State that they are eligible to practice before the IRS","Appear before the IRS as a fiduciary or a trustee","File powers of attorney with the IRS","Prepare and submit client-related correspondence with the IRS"],a:1,why:"A fiduciary/trustee IS the taxpayer in the eyes of the IRS — appearing in that capacity isn't 'practice' and survives disbarment.",wrongs:["Claiming eligibility would be false.",null,"POAs naming them will be rejected.","Client correspondence is representation — prohibited."]},
{n:56,topic:"POA Exceptions",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"In which of the following cases is a power of attorney (Form 2848) NOT required?",opts:["When a general partner wishes to represent his partnership before the IRS","When a taxpayer wishes to be represented during an IRS examination","By an attorney of record in a case that is docketed in the Tax Court","When an enrolled agent represents a taxpayer before the IRS appeals division"],a:2,why:"Once a case is docketed in Tax Court, the attorney of record's authority comes from the court — no Form 2848 needed for related IRS dealings.",wrongs:["A general partner representing the partnership still files a written authorization/POA per the exam's framing.","Exam representation requires a POA.",null,"Appeals representation requires a POA."]},
{n:57,topic:"Lien Release",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"The IRS will release an existing tax lien in all of the following cases EXCEPT:",opts:["When the statute of limitations for collection has expired","When the taxpayer requests an offer in compromise","When the tax debt is fully paid","When the payment of the debt is guaranteed by a bond"],a:1,why:"Merely REQUESTING an OIC releases nothing — liens stay in place during OIC consideration and are released only after the offer is accepted AND paid.",wrongs:["Release required — the debt is unenforceable.",null,"Release within 30 days of full payment.","A bond guaranteeing payment triggers release."]},
{n:58,topic:"Who May Practice",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"All of the following persons may practice before the IRS EXCEPT:",opts:["Certified public accountants","Enrolled actuaries","Certified financial planners","Enrolled retirement plan agents"],a:2,why:"A CFP designation confers NO IRS practice rights — it's a financial planning credential, not a Circular 230 category.",wrongs:["May practice.","May practice (limited scope).",null,"May practice (limited scope)."]},
{n:59,topic:"CAF System",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"With regard to the Centralized Authorization File (CAF) number on powers of attorney, which of the following statements is TRUE?",opts:["A power of attorney that does not include a CAF number will not be rejected","A CAF number is an indication of authority to practice before the Internal Revenue Service","Powers of attorney that relate to specific tax periods, or to any other Federal tax matter such as application for an employee identification number, will be entered onto the CAF system","The fact that a power of attorney cannot be entered onto the CAF system affects its validity"],a:0,why:"Missing a CAF number doesn't invalidate a POA — the IRS simply assigns one. The CAF number is just a filing identifier.",wrongs:[null,"It's an identifier, not a credential.","Specific-matter POAs (like EIN applications) are NOT recorded on the CAF.","Non-CAF POAs remain perfectly valid."]},
{n:60,topic:"FTC Safeguards Rule",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Federal law gives the Federal Trade Commission authority to set data safeguard regulations for various entities, including professional tax return preparers. According to the FTC Safeguards Rule, tax return preparers are now required to have what type of plan?",opts:["A written information security plan to protect client data","A conflict of interest waiver for each client represented","A well-written engagement letter for each client","A §7216 disclosure consent for each client before starting any engagement"],a:0,why:"The FTC Safeguards Rule requires paid preparers to maintain a WRITTEN INFORMATION SECURITY PLAN (WISP) protecting client data.",wrongs:[null,"Conflict waivers are Circular 230 — only when conflicts exist.","Engagement letters are best practice, not FTC-required.","§7216 consents apply only to specific disclosures."]}
,
{n:61,topic:"Censure",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Jonathan is an enrolled agent who was officially censured by the IRS. What are the consequences after a censure?",opts:["Jonathan is prohibited from practicing before the IRS and from filing tax returns","Jonathan is not prohibited from filing tax returns or representing taxpayers before the IRS","Jonathan is prohibited from practicing before the IRS","Jonathan is prohibited from filing tax returns"],a:1,why:"Censure is a public reprimand — embarrassing, but practice rights and return preparation continue (possibly with conditions).",wrongs:["Censure doesn't remove practice rights.",null,"He may still practice.","He may still prepare and file returns."]},
{n:62,topic:"Advertising Records",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Manuel is an enrolled agent. He sends out a targeted direct mail advertisement to his existing clients once a year. How long must he retain a copy of the advertisement?",opts:["Four years from the date of the last transmission or use","36 months from the date of the first mailing or use","24 months from the date of the last mailing or use","36 months from the date of the last transmission or use"],a:3,why:"§10.30: copies of communications distributed by mail must be retained for at least 36 months from the date of the LAST transmission or use.",wrongs:["Four years exceeds the rule.","The clock runs from the LAST use, not the first.","24 months is too short.",null]},
{n:63,topic:"Contested Positions",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Celia is an enrolled agent with a client who wishes to take a position regarding a particular tax credit. Celia's research shows the issue is currently being litigated, with taxpayers arguing that the Revenue Ruling the IRS relies on does not comply with statutory guidance. Based on her client's circumstances, Celia determines her client has a good argument. What is Celia's responsibility?",opts:["Celia can claim the credit as long as the position is not incorrect, inconsistent, or incomplete and is not frivolous. Additional disclosure is not required","Celia can claim the credit on her client's tax return as long as the position is reasonable, is not incorrect, inconsistent, or incomplete; is not frivolous, and is adequately disclosed","Celia cannot sign the return or recommend a position that is contrary to an IRS position","Celia should request a private letter ruling for her client. Otherwise, she may not take the position on the return"],a:1,why:"A position contrary to a Revenue Ruling may be taken if it has a reasonable basis, isn't frivolous, and is ADEQUATELY DISCLOSED (Form 8275).",wrongs:["Contrary-to-ruling positions require disclosure.",null,"Practitioners CAN take positions contrary to IRS rulings with proper support and disclosure.","No PLR is required to take a supportable position."]},
{n:64,topic:"Advising on Penalties",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Wallace is an enrolled agent, and Sabrina is his client. Sabrina wants to take a tax position on her return. There is a reasonable basis for the position, based on a recent court case. What is Wallace's duty if he advises Sabrina regarding a position for which penalties may be incurred?",opts:["Wallace must advise Sabrina of the penalties that are reasonably likely to apply regarding a position on her tax return, if he advises the client regarding the position or if he prepares the tax return","Wallace must advise Sabrina of the possibility of the tax return being audited and estimate the risk of that occurring. If the audit risk is high, he should decline the engagement","Wallace should advise Sabrina of the audit risk, and consider not disclosing the position based on the risk of eventual penalties","Wallace should not advise Sabrina about the possibility of penalties resulting from a tax return that he prepared"],a:0,why:"§10.34(c): the practitioner must inform the client of penalties reasonably likely to apply, and of penalty-avoidance through disclosure.",wrongs:[null,"Audit-risk estimation is prohibited as a basis for advice.","Concealing positions to dodge penalties is misconduct.","Silence violates the duty to advise."]},
{n:65,topic:"E-File Provider Requirements",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Webster just became an enrolled agent. He now wants to apply to become an authorized e-file provider. Which of the following is NOT a requirement to become an IRS e-file provider?",opts:["To be at least 18 years old as of the date of application","To have a permanent business location outside his home, in order to ensure the safety of client confidentiality and client records","To meet applicable state and local licensing or bonding requirements for the preparation and collection of tax returns","To be a United States citizen or a lawful permanent resident"],a:1,why:"Home-based tax businesses are perfectly acceptable — no outside office is required for e-file authorization.",wrongs:["Real requirement — 18+.",null,"Real requirement.","Real requirement — citizen or legal permanent resident (or resident alien authorized to work)."]},
{n:66,topic:"Collection Statute",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Ishaan was behind on filing his tax return and ended up owing money when he finally submitted it. However, at the time of filing, he did not have the funds to cover the taxes due. The amount due is correct. How much time does the IRS normally have to collect the outstanding amount from Ishaan?",opts:["Twenty years.","Five years.","Fifteen years.","Ten years."],a:3,why:"The collection statute is 10 years from assessment.",wrongs:["Too long.","Too short.","Too long.",null]},
{n:67,topic:"Tax Compliance",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"A practitioner is required to be in compliance with all existing tax obligations in order to maintain their licensing. Under Circular 230, what is the definition of \"tax compliance\"?",opts:["When all returns that are due have been filed","When all returns that are due have been filed, and all taxes that are due have been paid (or acceptable payment arrangements have been established).","When all returns that are due have been filed, and all taxes that are due have been paid. A payment arrangement is not sufficient.","When all taxes that are due have been paid (or acceptable payment arrangements have been established), even if there are unfiled returns."],a:1,why:"Compliance = all required returns FILED plus all taxes PAID or under an acceptable payment arrangement (like an installment agreement).",wrongs:["Filing alone isn't enough — payment status matters.",null,"Acceptable payment arrangements DO count.","Unfiled returns break compliance regardless of payments."]},
{n:68,topic:"Preparer Signature",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"When multiple tax preparers are involved in the preparation of a single return, who is required to sign the return?",opts:["Any of the preparers involved in the task of preparation may sign","The signing tax preparer should be the individual preparer who has the primary responsibility for the overall accuracy of the return","Any preparer who has the proper licensing may sign the return","Multiple preparers may sign in the preparer section of the return, because all the preparers have joint and several liability"],a:1,why:"The preparer with PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITY for the return's overall substantive accuracy signs.",wrongs:["Not just anyone — the primarily responsible preparer.",null,"Licensing doesn't determine the signer.","Only one preparer signs."]},
{n:69,topic:"Reasonable Inquiries",unit:"SU 5: Practitioner Standards and Tax Advice",q:"Umeko is an enrolled agent. Her client, Kiyoshi, tells her that he used his automobile 100% for business purposes. Kiyoshi does not own any other vehicles. Can Umeko rely on Kiyoshi's statement without any further verification?",opts:["No. She must make reasonable inquiries of her client","No. She must review a mileage log and other documents provided by her client before she can claim any deductions","No. She needs to verify the information with third parties","Yes"],a:0,why:"A 100%-business-use claim on his ONLY vehicle is implausible on its face — the practitioner must make reasonable inquiries before accepting it.",wrongs:[null,"Document review isn't strictly mandated — reasonable inquiry is the standard.","Third-party verification isn't required.","Blind reliance on implausible facts violates due diligence."]},
{n:70,topic:"Record Retention",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"How long must a paid preparer retain a copy of a client's tax return?",opts:["Three years after the close of the return period","Indefinitely","Four years after the close of the return period","Six years after the close of the return period"],a:0,why:"Preparers keep copies (or a client list) for 3 years after the close of the return period.",wrongs:[null,"No indefinite requirement.","Four years applies to CE records, not returns.","Six years is not the rule."]}
,
{n:71,topic:"Offer in Compromise",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"Bruno owes $10,000 in federal income tax. He does not dispute the amount but does not have the ability to pay the full amount because he is disabled and cannot work. Bruno agrees to pay $5,000 of his liability as part of an offer in compromise with the IRS based on the grounds of:",opts:["Doubt as to liability","Undue hardship","Doubt as to collectibility","Effective tax administration"],a:2,why:"He agrees he owes the tax but can't pay it in full — DOUBT AS TO COLLECTIBILITY.",wrongs:["He doesn't dispute the liability.","Not a standalone OIC ground.",null,"ETA applies when the tax IS fully collectible but collection would be inequitable."]},
{n:72,topic:"Examination Location",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"If a taxpayer receives an IRS examination notice, but wishes to change the location of the examination, under what circumstances does the IRS generally grant a request to transfer the place of examination?",opts:["The taxpayer has a planned vacation and cannot be present in the current location.","The current examination location poses a temporary inconvenience to the taxpayer.","The taxpayer's books, records, and source documents are maintained at a location other than where the examination has been scheduled.","The taxpayer wishes to delay the examination to a later date that is more convenient."],a:2,why:"Transfers are granted when the records are kept elsewhere — e.g., books maintained at a different business location or the representative's office in another district.",wrongs:["Vacations warrant rescheduling, not transfer.","Temporary inconvenience isn't sufficient.",null,"Delay isn't grounds for a location change."]},
{n:73,topic:"Failure-to-File Penalty",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"If a taxpayer does not file their tax return by the due date (including extensions), the taxpayer is most likely to be assessed which of the following penalties?",opts:["A failure-to-file penalty","An accuracy-related penalty","A civil fraud penalty","A trust fund recovery penalty"],a:0,why:"Late filing triggers the failure-to-file penalty: 5% per month (or part), capped at 25%.",wrongs:[null,"Accuracy penalties relate to understatements, not late filing.","Fraud requires intent to evade.","TFRP is for unpaid payroll trust fund taxes."]},
{n:74,topic:"OPR Discipline",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"All of the following may cause a practitioner to face disciplinary action by the Office of Professional Responsibility EXCEPT:",opts:["Misconduct while representing a taxpayer","A misdemeanor conviction for public drunkenness","Giving a false opinion through gross incompetence","A felony drug conviction"],a:1,why:"§10.51 targets crimes under tax laws and crimes involving dishonesty or breach of trust — a public drunkenness misdemeanor doesn't qualify.",wrongs:["Sanctionable.",null,"Sanctionable — false opinions through gross incompetence.","Felony convictions are sanctionable."]},
{n:75,topic:"OIC Requirements",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"When submitting an Offer in Compromise based on \"doubt as to collectibility\" or \"effective tax administration,\" taxpayers must submit Form 656, Offer in Compromise, and also submit:",opts:["Reasonable offer terms","Proof of residency","A collection information statement","A lump sum cash offer"],a:2,why:"These offer types require Form 433-A (OIC) or 433-B (OIC) — the Collection Information Statement documenting finances.",wrongs:["'Reasonable terms' isn't a required document.","No residency proof requirement.",null,"Periodic payment offers are also allowed — lump sum isn't mandatory."]},
{n:76,topic:"IRC §7216",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Desmond is an enrolled agent. An investment services firm wants Desmond to share his client list so the firm can market investments to his clients. Desmond agrees, and emails a spreadsheet with all his clients' names, addresses and other personal information — without asking his clients' permission. What penalty may be assessed against Desmond?",opts:["Desmond may be assessed a penalty for criminal fraud under IRC §6663","Desmond may be assessed an IRC §7216 penalty, for disclosure or use of information by the preparer of the return without the taxpayer's consent.","No penalty, this type of information disclosure is permitted as long as it is not malicious.","Desmond may be assessed a due diligence penalty under IRC §6695(g)"],a:1,why:"Selling or sharing client return information for marketing without written consent is exactly what §7216 criminalizes.",wrongs:["§6663 is the taxpayer civil fraud penalty.",null,"Consent was required — this is not permitted.","§6695(g) is the refundable credit due diligence penalty."]},
{n:77,topic:"Fraud vs. Negligence",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"The key distinction between fraud and negligence is that fraud is always:",opts:["Intentional","More complex","More difficult to prosecute","Harder to detect"],a:0,why:"Fraud requires willful INTENT to deceive; negligence is carelessness without intent.",wrongs:[null,"Complexity isn't the defining line.","Prosecutorial difficulty isn't the distinction.","Detectability isn't the distinction."]},
{n:78,topic:"ITIN Applications",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Diego plans to submit Form W-7 for his client, Giovanna, who needs to request an ITIN in order to file her return. What type of identifying documents must accompany the ITIN application?",opts:["Form W-7 must include original or certified copies of supporting documentation, such as a passport or birth certificate. Photocopies are no longer sufficient","Form W-7 must include original or notarized copies of supporting documentation. Photocopies are not sufficient","Form W-7 must include photocopies of supporting documentation, such as a passport or birth certificate","Form W-7 does not require any additional documentation as long as it is filed with an original return"],a:0,why:"W-7 applications require ORIGINAL documents or copies CERTIFIED by the issuing agency. Notarized copies and photocopies are no longer accepted.",wrongs:[null,"Notarized copies stopped being accepted years ago — certification must come from the issuing agency.","Plain photocopies are insufficient.","Supporting identity documents are always required."]},
{n:79,topic:"AOTC Fraud",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"Mickey fraudulently claimed the American Opportunity Tax Credit. He was not a college student and had no qualifying educational expenses. Mickey received and spent his $2,500 refund when an audit notice was issued. During the examination, Mickey cannot provide documentation to support his AOTC claim. What is likely to happen?",opts:["The AOTC will be disallowed, and Mickey will have a balance due, including penalties","The AOTC will be disallowed for the current year return, but it will not be disallowed in future years","The AOTC will be disallowed, and Mickey will have a balance due, including penalties and interest. In addition, he may be disallowed from claiming the credit in future years","The credit will be disallowed, but Mickey will not owe any additional penalties"],a:2,why:"Fraudulent AOTC claims bring repayment with penalties AND interest, plus a multi-year ban on claiming the credit (10 years for fraud).",wrongs:["Incomplete — interest and a future ban also apply.","Future-year bans DO apply to fraudulent claims.",null,"Penalties definitely apply to fraud."]},
{n:80,topic:"EA Title Use",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Which of the following practitioners would be allowed to use the term \"enrolled agent\" to describe their status?",opts:["An enrolled agent who has let his license lapse","An enrolled agent who is currently appealing an official OPR censure","An enrolled agent who has been placed on inactive status","A person who has passed all three parts of the EA exam, but who has not received his confirmation of enrollment yet"],a:1,why:"A censure doesn't remove the credential — even during an appeal, he remains an active EA entitled to the title.",wrongs:["Lapsed license = no title use.",null,"Inactive EAs may not use the designation.","Not enrolled until the IRS confirms enrollment."]}
,
{n:81,topic:"OPR Sanctions",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Yanni is an enrolled agent who was sanctioned by the Office of Professional Responsibility for violating provisions of Circular 230. Which of the following from Circular 230 is correct regarding the potential IRS sanctions against Yanni?",opts:["The monetary penalty against Yanni can exceed the gross income derived from the conduct giving rise to the penalty","Yanni can be publicly censured, and his name published in the Internal Revenue Bulletin","Yanni can face imprisonment up to 1 year, as part of his sanction","The IRS cannot impose both monetary and nonmonetary sanctions against Yanni"],a:1,why:"Censure is public, and censured practitioners' names are published in the Internal Revenue Bulletin.",wrongs:["Monetary penalties are CAPPED at the gross income derived from the conduct.",null,"OPR cannot imprison anyone.","Monetary AND nonmonetary sanctions can be combined."]},
{n:82,topic:"Examination Rights",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"During the IRS examination process, a taxpayer has the right to:",opts:["Both request that an enrolled practitioner represents him and appeal any determination made by the IRS examination division","Decline an IRS summons","Request that an enrolled practitioner represents him","Appeal any determination made by the IRS examination division"],a:0,why:"Taxpayers have BOTH rights: representation by an enrolled practitioner and appeal of exam determinations.",wrongs:[null,"A summons is legally enforceable — it can be challenged, not simply declined.","True but incomplete.","True but incomplete."]},
{n:83,topic:"Practice Definition",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"Which of the following acts is NOT considered \"practice\" before the IRS?",opts:["Preparing and submitting a response to an IRS notice or inquiry","Offering written tax advice with the potential for tax avoidance","Appearing as a witness for a taxpayer","Representing a taxpayer before IRS Appeals"],a:2,why:"Appearing as a WITNESS (like furnishing information at IRS request) is expressly excluded from the definition of practice.",wrongs:["Advocating in responses to notices is practice.","Written tax advice is practice.",null,"Appeals representation is quintessential practice."]},
{n:84,topic:"Permitted Actions",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Landry is an enrolled agent. Which of the following actions would be permitted?",opts:["Landry uses the official IRS eagle logo in all her advertising","Landry offers to file a client's tax return using the client's last pay stub, instead of Form W-2","Landry offers direct deposit of refund checks for a small fee","Landry offers her clients refund anticipation loans"],a:3,why:"Offering RALs is legal and permitted — as long as advertising describes them accurately as loans and fee rules are respected.",wrongs:["IRS insignia are prohibited in ads.","Pay stub filing is prohibited.","Direct deposit fees are prohibited.",null]},
{n:85,topic:"Credit for Other Dependents",unit:"SU 6: Due Diligence for Refundable Credits",q:"For the non-refundable $500 \"Other Dependent Credit,\" which of the following would NOT be a qualifying person for the purposes of the credit?",opts:["A qualifying relative, including a taxpayer's aging parent","A qualifying child that is the dependent of another taxpayer","A qualifying child with ITIN","A qualifying relative of the taxpayer, 17 years and older"],a:1,why:"The person must be THE TAXPAYER'S dependent — someone claimed by another taxpayer can't generate the ODC.",wrongs:["Qualifies — dependent parents.",null,"Qualifies — ITIN children get ODC.","Qualifies — older dependents."]},
{n:86,topic:"Continuing Education",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"At the end of the year, the following enrolled agents totaled their CE hours: Sebastian — 24 hours regular CE, 1 hour ethics. Gloria — 16 hours regular, 2 hours ethics. Mona — 14 hours regular, 4 hours ethics. Which of them has NOT met the minimum annual educational requirements?",opts:["Sebastian and Mona","Mona","Sebastian","Gloria"],a:2,why:"The annual floor is 16 total hours INCLUDING 2 ethics hours. Sebastian has 25 total but only 1 ethics hour — he fails. Gloria (18/2) and Mona (18/4) both comply.",wrongs:["Mona complies — 18 total with 4 ethics.","Mona complies.",null,"Gloria complies — 18 total with 2 ethics."]},
{n:87,topic:"Installment Agreements",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"For the IRS to grant a \"guaranteed\" installment agreement, a taxpayer must have not failed to file any income tax returns or pay any tax shown on such returns during any of the preceding:",opts:["6 taxable years","3 taxable years","10 taxable years","5 taxable years"],a:3,why:"Guaranteed installment agreements require clean compliance for the preceding 5 taxable years.",wrongs:["Not the lookback.","Too short.","Too long.",null]},
{n:88,topic:"Charitable Substantiation",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"Which of the following types of documentation is sufficient for a taxpayer to claim a charitable deduction for a cash donation of $250 or more?",opts:["No documentation is needed for cash donations of under $500","No receipt is required, but the organization must write a good faith estimate of the value of goods and services provided","A receipt or written acknowledgment from the organization listing the date and amount of the donation, plus a statement regarding whether the organization gave any goods or services as a result of the contribution","A self-prepared receipt is sufficient if the taxpayer also has a canceled check"],a:2,why:"$250+ gifts require a contemporaneous written acknowledgment from the charity with amount, date, and a goods-and-services statement.",wrongs:["All cash gifts need records; $250+ needs the acknowledgment.","A written acknowledgment IS required.",null,"Self-prepared receipts don't satisfy the rule."]},
{n:89,topic:"30-Day Letter",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"What is a \"30-day letter\"?",opts:["A revenue agent report","A summons to appear before an IRS examiner","A notice of deficiency","A notice explaining a taxpayer's right to appeal and a statement of proposed tax owed after an IRS examination"],a:3,why:"The 30-day letter transmits proposed exam changes and explains the 30-day window to request an Appeals conference.",wrongs:["The RAR is enclosed, but the letter is defined by its appeal-rights function.","Not a summons.","That's the 90-day letter.",null]},
{n:90,topic:"Circular 230 Source",unit:"SU 1: Legal Authority of the IRS",q:"Circular 230 is found in __________ of the Code of Federal Regulations, which governs practice before the Internal Revenue Service.",opts:["Title 90","Title 31","Title 53","Title 26"],a:1,why:"Circular 230 is codified at 31 CFR Part 10 — Title 31, implementing 31 USC §330.",wrongs:["Doesn't exist.",null,"Not the title.","Title 26 is the IRC itself."]}
,
{n:91,topic:"Expedited Suspension",unit:"SU 8: Practitioner Misconduct",q:"Under expedited suspension procedures, the Office of Professional Responsibility can take quicker disciplinary actions against a practitioner who:",opts:["Has been convicted of a crime involving a breach of trust","Has been convicted of a crime involving dishonesty","Has failed to file his federal income tax returns in four of the five previous tax years","All of the above"],a:3,why:"Expedited suspension covers convictions for dishonesty/breach of trust, license revocations, and willful non-filing patterns (4 of the last 5 years).",wrongs:["True but incomplete.","True but incomplete.","True but incomplete.",null]},
{n:92,topic:"Fraudulent Failure to File",unit:"SU 7: Recordkeeping Requirements and Penalties",q:"If a taxpayer fails to file a return, and the failure is due to fraud, the penalty is:",opts:["15% for each month or part of a month that the return is late, up to a maximum of 25%","10% for each month or part of a month that the return is late, up to a maximum of 50%","15% for each month or part of a month that the return is late, up to a maximum of 50%","15% for each month or part of a month that the return is late, up to a maximum of 75%"],a:3,why:"Fraudulent failure to file: 15% per month, maximum 75% — triple the normal FTF penalty.",wrongs:["25% is the NON-fraud cap.","Wrong rate and cap.","The cap is 75%.",null]},
{n:93,topic:"Refund Checks",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Which of the following actions is permitted by Circular 230?",opts:["A taxpayer may authorize an enrolled agent to file their return without a signature","A taxpayer may authorize an enrolled agent to receive a refund check on behalf of the taxpayer","A taxpayer may authorize an enrolled agent to receive and endorse a refund check on behalf of the taxpayer","A taxpayer may authorize an enrolled agent to represent them in U.S. district court"],a:1,why:"RECEIVING a client's refund check (with POA authorization) is permitted — endorsing or negotiating it never is.",wrongs:["Returns require signatures.",null,"Endorsement is always prohibited.","District court requires bar admission."]},
{n:94,topic:"IRS Direct Pay",unit:"SU 9: Tax Payments and IRS Collections",q:"What is IRS Direct Pay?",opts:["Direct Pay is a third-party payment service that charges a fee to withdraw an IRS payment","Direct Pay is a secure IRS service that may be used to pay income taxes. The IRS charges a nominal fee for Direct Pay.","Direct Pay is an IRS service that may be used only by corporations to make federal tax payments of any amount.","Direct Pay is a free IRS service that may be used to pay an individual or business tax bill or estimated tax payment."],a:3,why:"Direct Pay is free, IRS-run, and handles tax bills and estimated payments directly from a bank account.",wrongs:["Not third-party, and free.","No fee.","Not corporations-only.",null]},
{n:95,topic:"Burden of Proof",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"In any case in which the IRS asserts fraud, what party has the \"burden of proof\" with respect to the fraud?",opts:["Government","Tax preparer","Taxpayer","Tax Court"],a:0,why:"Fraud must be proven by the GOVERNMENT by clear and convincing evidence.",wrongs:[null,"Not a party to the burden.","The taxpayer's usual burden flips on fraud.","Courts decide; they don't bear burdens."]},
{n:96,topic:"Information Matching",unit:"SU 10: The IRS Examination Process",q:"What does the IRS \"information matching program\" automatically check on each tax return?",opts:["It scans for errors in the refund amount","It checks for mathematical errors","It looks for typos in the taxpayer's name","It checks against filed information statements to verify that income and major deductions on the return are correct"],a:3,why:"Returns are matched against W-2s, 1099s, and other third-party information statements.",wrongs:["Different check.","Separate math-error processing.","Identity processing is separate.",null]},
{n:97,topic:"Authorizations",unit:"SU 3: Authorizations and Disclosures",q:"Which IRS authorization provides the highest degree of representation rights to an enrolled practitioner?",opts:["Form 2848","Form 8821","Third Party Designee Authorization","Oral Authorization"],a:0,why:"Form 2848 grants full representation authority — the strongest of the authorizations.",wrongs:[null,"Information-only.","Very limited scope.","Most limited."]},
{n:98,topic:"Reliance on Others",unit:"SU 4: Circular 230 and Best Practices",q:"Warrick has a new client, Selena, who had her prior return prepared by another tax office. Warrick would like to rely on the other practitioner's work product by using the depreciation schedule on the prior-year return. Can he do so without question?",opts:["Yes, in all circumstances","Generally, yes, but due care must be taken if Warrick relies on the work product of another person","No; reliance on another preparer's work product is prohibited","Only if the practitioner is another member of the first practitioner's firm"],a:1,why:"Reliance on another's work product is fine — WITH due care in evaluating its reasonableness.",wrongs:["Due care is still required.",null,"Reliance is permitted.","No same-firm restriction."]},
{n:99,topic:"EFINs",unit:"SU 12: The IRS E-File Program",q:"Kira is a tax preparer who plans to retire this year. She sells her entire tax practice to Agnes, a CPA. With regards to Kira's EFIN, what must occur when a tax business is sold?",opts:["Agnes is not required to submit a new e-file application as long as Kira gives her written permission to use the existing one.","The IRS will issue a new EFIN automatically when the preparer's PTIN information changes on the submitted returns","Kira may transfer her EFIN to Agnes, as long as the physical location of the practice does not change","Agnes is required to submit a new e-file application and request her own EFIN. Kira's EFIN cannot be transferred"],a:3,why:"EFINs are non-transferable — new owners must apply for their own.",wrongs:["Permission can't transfer an EFIN.","Nothing happens automatically.","EFINs never transfer, location notwithstanding.",null]},
{n:100,topic:"Circular 230 Jurisdiction",unit:"SU 2: Practice Before the IRS",q:"The regulations under Circular 230 govern all of the following persons EXCEPT:",opts:["Licensed insurance broker","Enrolled agents","Enrolled Actuaries","CPAs"],a:0,why:"Circular 230 governs practitioners before the IRS — attorneys, CPAs, EAs, enrolled actuaries, ERPAs, AFSP participants. Insurance brokers as such are outside it.",wrongs:[null,"Governed.","Governed.","Governed."]}
]; // END_MOCK4
